1785609175640333	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-2pmc	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-04 delivered in workspace bn-2pmc, commit c52f19b. Parent tracking: extended explore() (not a parallel fn) — Reachable gains a third parallel column 'origins: Vec<Discovery>' beside states/depths; Discovery is Initial or Step{predecessor, action}. Behaviour preservation: the per-expansion 'discovered' set changed from BTreeSet<State> to BTreeMap<State, usize> keyed by the same State with the same Ord, so keys, iteration order and length are literally unchanged — admissions, queue, depths, frontier, counters and bound checks are the same sequences and values. Existing bfs_contract.rs (23) and bfs_diehard.rs (17) pass byte-for-byte unchanged; new tests/bfs_origins.rs (10) asserts it against an independent origin-blind hand walk and against the frozen queue-ordered frontier. src/witness.rs: shortest(model, exploration, target) -> Result<Witness, NoWitness>; Target is data (State / Holds(pred) / Fails(pred)), never a closure; endpoint = shallowest satisfying state, ties to canonically least; refuses on Exhausted (NoWitness::Truncated) mirroring closed(). Die Hard: shortest NotSolved violation is the six-step path ending at (4,3) and it EQUALS the Lean path (DieHard.lean:14-22) — pinned. New tests 40 (10 origins + 15 diehard + 15 contract); crate total 152. just check exit 0 (fmt, clippy -D warnings, tests, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier, lean). validation-results.json untouched. Did not touch certificate.rs, model.rs, expr.rs, diehard.rs; lib.rs limited to the mod line + re-exports (its 'What is here' prose still says three bones remain — left for the lead once IMPL-03/05 land, to avoid a semantic conflict with the concurrent certificate work)."}	blake3:NMmgO9_8EwQ_IXk1t7xLPKA1LzXitnVG9B6ACoinuqI
1785609281459882	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEytQc		item.comment	bn-2pmc	{"body":"Landed @ 3694a3a. Lead ran all 10 crate suites in-workspace (152 tests green). Ratified: the third-parallel-column extension over a second walk (a second walk is a second thing that can disagree — right shape argument); the behavior-preservation proof via BTreeSet→BTreeMap same-keys-same-order plus the queue-ordered-frontier assertion (the one observable a sort couldn't hide); Target as data never closures; truncation refused even for found endpoints; the deliberate NO self-replay so the tests' replay stays independent evidence; depth-bounded carve-out correctly declined (a different claim needs its own type). The Lean-path equality pin documented as observed-fact-not-guarantee is exactly right. Memory trade-off (predecessor by value) accepted with the docs recording IMPL-05's projection alternative. lib.rs prose refresh rides after bn-3e4l lands."}	blake3:5QKbbtGAKZzm1DWox9UF11aARys83669vn8P8bPccTc
1785609281497713	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEytgc		item.move	bn-2pmc	{"state":"done"}	blake3:GpQoLcMQIuaUnfFssjUGISkzkfax2BsiTNHaJTpIir0
1785608532564364	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-3e4l	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-05 dependency finding, resolved before writing code. The engine->kernel-core edge is admitted in DEV position only, and that is the position the differential needs.\n\nFORWARD EDGE (kernel -> engine): forbidden, mechanically. tools/check_crate_boundaries.py RULE certificate-checker-not-search walks the transitive closure of CHECKER = the four kernel crates + continuum-certificate and fails on any engine or forge in it (checker lines 237-247, CHECKER at 126). Plan section 20 states it as 'proof/certificate checker does not depend on search engines'.\n\nREVERSE EDGE (engine -> kernel-core), NORMAL position: not forbidden by any rule, but not taken. No rule in check_crate_boundaries.py names it, and nothing in plan section 20's rule list forbids it. It is still wrong for this bone: plan section 20 requires 'a serialization boundary between every engine and the kernel — a certificate is checked from its wire form, never from shared memory', and an engine that linked the kernel to borrow its constants would be sharing exactly the codec that docs/03 section 8 lists as an axis independent paths must differ along. So src/certificate.rs writes the bytes from the DOCUMENTED grammar in kernel-core/src/wire.rs:43-96 with no import, the way kernel-core's own fixture.rs:5-9 writes them ('deliberately written against the documented wire grammar rather than against the decoder's internals').\n\nREVERSE EDGE, DEV position: explicitly admitted, in the checker's own words. check_crate_boundaries.py:37-39 — 'Normal and build dependencies are enforced; dev-dependencies are reported but not enforced, because a differential test may legitimately link an engine it would never ship against' — and build_graph() (lines 175-179) buckets kind == 'dev' into a dev map that evaluate() is never called with (main(), line 384: evaluate(enforced, members)); dev edges appear in the report only, under dev_dependency_edges_not_enforced. Every other closure walk in the workspace agrees: check_code_policy.py's checker-closure, network, ambient and reference-engine rules all pass kinds ('normal', 'build') explicitly (lines 1087-1130, 1821), and check_kernel_covenant.py's dependency-freedom rule is about what the KERNEL depends on, which a dev edge from the engine does not change.\n\nSo case 3 of the brief's three: the differential lives in crates/continuum-engine-reference/tests/, with continuum-kernel-core as a dev-dependency. No checker is weakened. Cost, stated: GOV-1-07 requires every dependency in any position to have an entry in tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml, and check_code_policy.py:1275 requires a dependency observed only in dev position to be classified tcb_class = 'dev-only'. I add that entry."}	blake3:ZttrXrOPsq0x3WUrAlOofL6493mZZq2jZdS9Vlb_7o8
1785609321576883	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAg		item.comment	bn-3e4l	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-05 delivered (1/2 — design). crates/continuum-engine-reference/src/certificate.rs (882 lines) + 24 tests in two new suites. Crate total 136; the 112 tests from IMPL-01/02 are untouched and green.\n\nNO IMPORT. certificate.rs writes the CONTCERT wire form from the grammar documented in kernel-core/src/wire.rs:43-96 and imports nothing from the kernel. MAGIC, WIRE_EPOCH=1, FINITE_CLOSURE_KIND=1, PROPERTY_CLASS_STATE_DOMAIN=1, MAX_DOMAIN_PACKS=64 and MAX_CERTIFICATE_BYTES=1<<26 are declared here with the kernel line each was read from; MAX_STATES/MAX_TRANSITIONS/MAX_VARIABLES/MAX_ACTIONS are REUSED from bfs.rs and model.rs rather than respelled, since those modules already recorded the same kernel constants. The evidence that the transcription is right is byte-exact and independent of the kernel: byte-layout-of-a-two-state-model-is-exactly-the-grammar builds the whole certificate of a one-variable, one-action, two-state model by hand in the test with a third encoder written for that test alone, and asserts equality.\n\nTYPE-LEVEL SEAM (bn-3p8u's, restated where a signature can require it). emit-finite-closure takes ClosedSet<'a>, a private-field newtype whose ONLY constructor is ClosedSet::of(&Exploration) -> Option<Self>, delegating to Exploration::closed(). Exhausted therefore has no path to emission and EmissionError has no 'not closed' arm to report. Taking &Reachable would NOT have been a seam: Exploration::reachable() hands one out for either arm, deliberately (bfs.rs:452-466). This is constructor-shaped rather than compile-fail-harness-shaped — a trybuild-style negative needs an external dev-dependency the workspace does not admit — and the API-shaped proof is asserted three ways: closed() is None, ClosedSet::of is None, and each of the three bounds tripping closes the same door. It matters because the kernel could not catch the mistake: a bounded run's explored set is closed by accident whenever the bound tripped on a row that discovered nothing new.\n\nENVELOPE = SEAM, NOT DEFAULTS. ClaimEnvelope<'a> is a borrowed struct of six &str fields plus a domain-pack slice, modelled on kernel-core receipt.rs:38-70's Seam ('carried, labelled, and named as trusted, never invented'). Nothing is defaulted or synthesized: six of RFC 0005's eight fields are digests/epochs of artifacts this crate cannot see (it declares no content hasher — ADR-0013 puts canonical identity in continuum-value, which this crate declines to depend on for the reason ident.rs records — and it is handed a Model, not the CML that elaborated to it). The two NON-caller fields are the two the format fixes: schema-epoch must equal the header epoch or the decoder rejects the pair (wire.rs:419-424), and property-class is StateDomain because the property this certificate carries IS the declared state domain it writes. A settable field whose only other value names a rejection is not a choice. PRODUCER is offered as a constant (concat! of CARGO-PKG-NAME and CARGO-PKG-VERSION, a compile-time manifest read, not an ambient one) rather than filled in, so the seam's rule stays true of all eight fields.\n\nTYPED FAILURE BEFORE ANY BYTE. Validation runs in the decoder's own field order, so a caller with two bad fields is told about the same one every time. EnvelopeError has four arms: MalformedToken{field, IdentError}, MalformedDomainPack{index, IdentError}, TooManyDomainPacks, DomainPacksNotAscending (which is duplicate AND descending — strictness is what forbids a duplicate). EmissionError adds Evaluation{state, Box<EvaluationError>}, CountOutOfRange{field, found, min, max} mirroring the kernel's own Rejection::CountOutOfRange, and Oversized. Every row is recomputed and every count checked BEFORE the first byte is written, so a failed emission never leaves a partial artifact.\n\nWHAT IS COPIED AND WHAT IS RE-DERIVED. Six wire sequences must be strictly ascending; all six arrive ordered — variables and actions sorted by name at model build, the state table straight off the BTreeMap walk ('certificate emission is a copy, not a sort'), initial states ascending at build, and one successor row ascending by (action, target) from Model::successors. Emission SORTS NOTHING and no ordering argument rests on a comparison written in certificate.rs. Reachable carries the reachable set and its depths but NOT the transition relation, so rows are recomputed by Model::successors as they are written — a second evaluation of a pure function, which the reference path can afford, and which buys a real check: Model::successors validates arity and domain first, so a reachable set explored from a DIFFERENT model fails there (tested) instead of producing a certificate about nothing. Init subset S, Post(S) subset S and S subset P are NOT re-checked here — they are the kernel's to re-derive from the bytes, and the module documents where each one comes from instead of marking its own homework."}	blake3:G9-LW479BZeQRHUMRCSLRcnxe6u0ubSBWZGbV08tJ10
1785609357339087	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-3e4l	{"body":"PR-8 IMPL-05 (2/2 — the differential, evidence, gates, concerns).\n\nTHE FOURTH FROZEN FACT, ASSERTED. tests/certificate-kernel-differential.rs (6 tests) is the only place in the crate that links continuum-kernel-core, in dev position. Die Hard is explored through bfs::explore, emitted, and the BYTES go to check-certificate. Verdict::Verified with claim.states()==16, claim.transitions()==96, claim.initial-states()==1, kind FiniteClosure, property StateDomain — the same three numbers check.rs:253-257 pins for the kernel's own hand-built fixture, now re-derived by the checker from bytes this engine wrote. All six envelope fields round-trip and the claim's trusted-components are asserted verbatim (certificate-model-correspondence, envelope-digest-binding) so the test cannot read as 'verified, full stop'. A second test decodes the body through wire::decode and compares it to the model term by term: domain (big 0..5, small 0..3), the 16-state table at the engine's own indices with the kernel's binary search agreeing on every position, initial state [0,0], the six corpus action spellings in byte order (BigToSmall < EmptyBig < EmptySmall < FillBig < FillSmall < SmallToBig — the same INDICES as the kernel fixture's kebab-case names, as diehard.rs:62-71 predicted), one row per state, and every row equal transition-for-transition to Model::successors, totalling 96.\n\nNON-VACUITY. Every byte of the canonical state table (16 x 2 x 8 = 256) is mutated two ways (xor 0xFF and wrapping +1), 512 artifacts, and NONE verifies. The table offset is computed from the grammar in the test, not measured, and is sanity-checked against the 16 zero bytes of state (0,0) before the sweep trusts it. Header and tail are attacked by name with the EXACT verdict each must produce: wrong magic -> Rejected(BadMagic); epoch 2 -> Unsupported(WireEpoch{found:2}), not a rejection, because the artifact may be valid under a contract this build does not know; kind 7 -> Unsupported(CertificateKind{found:7}); one trailing byte -> Rejected(TrailingBytes{extra:1}); every one of the ~2.7k prefixes rejected; garbage (empty, bare magic, zeros, ASCII) not verified. I deliberately did NOT sweep the WHOLE certificate: a widened hi bound still verifies, and rather than hide that, a-widened-domain-bound-still-verifies-so-the-binding-lives-in-the-envelope asserts it and records why — the mutated bytes describe a weaker claim that no self-contained checker can catch, which is what envelope-digest-binding being TRUSTED means.\n\nTESTS (24 new; 18 + 6). certificate-wire.rs (no kernel): the byte-exact layout, the header constants, PRODUCER's spelling and token-shape, purity three ways (same inputs twice, two INDEPENDENT explorations, and a separately built model — all byte-identical) plus a bounded-but-complete run at exactly 16/7/96 emitting the same bytes as the certifiable ceiling, the closed-set seam (all three bounds), all six single-token envelope fields empty -> MalformedToken naming that field, a non-printable byte located at its offset, the 128/129-byte token boundary, the 64/65 domain-pack boundary (64 ascending packs EMIT, 65 is TooManyDomainPacks), descending/duplicate/malformed packs, the foreign-model case, and Display/source on both error types.\n\nGATES. cargo fmt, clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -D warnings, cargo test --workspace --locked, cargo doc: clean. Full 'just check' exit 0 (I ran it twice — the first run's exit code was masked by a pipe, so I re-ran capturing it; both passed). tools/check-crate-boundaries.py: pass, and the new dev edge is listed under dev-dependency-edges-not-enforced exactly as its docstring says it should be. GOV-1-01..12: all pass with the new dependency-rationale entry. START_HERE PR-8 bullet 5 annotated in prose with no underscored identifiers (traceability.py:77 strips them as markdown emphasis); traceability regenerated, PR-8-IMPL-05 active -> satisfied, 724 -> 723 active. validation-results.json NOT touched. Commit 6b81e89.\n\nPHASE-A-DEL-07 NOT ANNOTATED, and the honest reading. The bullet is 'executable finite reference engine and certificates from Revision 2'. The certificates half is now real: TV-009's certificate is emitted by the engine and accepted by an independent checker. The ENGINE half is not complete in this workspace — PR-8-IMPL-03 (invariant/deadlock) and IMPL-04 (shortest witness) are both still active, and no witness.rs exists here. So I left DEL-07 alone. When the sibling's witness bone lands, the reading is: model + BFS + witness + certificate = engine and certificates both delivered, and DEL-07 is annotatable by the lead in the same regen that closes the sibling. PR-8-EXIT correctly stays active regardless: it is a daemon-API claim and this bone touches no daemon code.\n\nCONCERNS. (1) EmissionError::CountOutOfRange is UNTESTED and unreachable from any model the declaration layer admits: model.rs already caps variables at 64 and actions at 4096, and tripping the state or transition ceiling needs a 2^20-state exploration, which I judged not worth the memory on this host. The arms exist because the usize->u16/u32 conversions must be total, not because a caller can reach them; the reachable boundary evidence is at the envelope (128-byte token, 64 domain packs), which is exact. (2) Same for Oversized: 64 MiB of certificate is unreachable below the state ceiling. (3) The dev edge costs a tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml entry classified tcb-class = 'dev-only', which the checker REQUIRES for a dependency observed only in dev position (check-code-policy.py:1275). Reading 'dev-only' next to the trusted kernel looks odd; the class describes this EDGE (never in a shipped artifact of the engine), not the kernel's own trust status, and the rationale says so. If the lead prefers the class to describe the crate, the checker's rule would have to change and I did not touch it. (4) certificate.rs emits the finite-closure family only. The state-type family (kind 2) is a strict prefix of this body and would be ~10 lines, but it is a separate claim with its own property digest and was not in the bone; the fourth frozen fact reads 'closure/type' and I have delivered the closure half. Worth a follow-up bone if the lead wants both. (5) lib.rs is the one file the sibling agent and I both edit — I kept it to the mod line, the pub-use block, and the paragraph that said 'the other THREE ... are separate bones', which this bone falsifies. Expect a two-hunk conflict there at most."}	blake3:ttlemdzvmjzFPr4RSH6huLKMO8_K2uocCt5Pf6_foqI
