1785612428193350	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0gc		item.comment	bn-dw81	{"body":"Landed @ cafd09a. Lead ran the 8 new tests in-workspace (green). Ratified: the toolchain-drift finding is exactly what evidence bones exist for — AxiomManifest.lean's hardcoded version now cross-pinned against lean-toolchain, verified-to-fail-on-drift before committing; the test-only-not-fix discipline held; annotation declined per the all-controls-delivered precedent (Lean receipt block has no producer until bn-3ie, Phase D). INV-014 coverage moves to bn-3ie."}	blake3:AR43gt7MDYi76IQypRebvDdIvZzIepfx4OtXp06ehtY
1785612428222369	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0wc		item.move	bn-dw81	{"state":"done"}	blake3:B4fm818Rv3mRhuKnY9ZnGdKMCTsoR99ZW6tIHcEMnl8
1785611780186023	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-3gi	{"body":"Design settled after reading the landed protocol module, RFC 0026/0027, the IDL rules, and the continuum-workspace/continuum-intent surfaces. The operation layer is pure request-in/result-out over explicit state (no tokio, no ambient clock/entropy): dispatch order is (1) protocol-version window, (2) registry lookup of the operation, (3) shape agreement between envelope.operation and the typed arguments, (4) the family's pure scope claim, (5) admission T1-T4 over the wire CapabilityDescriptor+profile, (6) registry-driven annotation obligations, (7) idempotency ledger, (8) family handler. The codec stays out: RequestEnvelope.arguments/ResultEnvelope.payload are Opaque and RFC 0026 fixes neither canonical field order nor union tagging, so this layer carries typed arguments and typed payloads beside the envelope and the transport bone encodes them."}	blake3:bGiWp9AvP8DKDjqe3z__tpd3A_2j4O_wbLEDDV2vEiI
1785613492251128	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAg		item.comment	bn-3gi	{"body":"Wire-shape defects found while implementing (protocol/ NOT edited, per the fence):\n(1) SnapshotComponents.files is list<Commitment> and carries no paths, so a snapshot cannot be reconstructed from a workspace.create request alone — two commitments cannot say which file goes where. The daemon covers it with an explicit staging area whose commitment is derived over a canonical (path, content) record, documented in daemon/state.rs::stage; the IDL gap is real and belongs to a later IDL revision.\n(2) rule errors.unsupported_surface and rule errors.common contradict each other for the eleven operations declaring 'errors []' (workspace.seal, task.status, task.subscribe among them): the first REQUIRES UnsupportedSemanticFeature for an unshipped operation, the second FORBIDS any code outside the common union plus the (empty) clause. Resolved in favour of errors.unsupported_surface, in one documented construction path (daemon/mod.rs::unsupported_surface); every other code still goes through the union check.\n(3) SnapshotEpochs.toolchain is an EpochIdentity, but continuum-workspace's WorkspaceDescriptor carries a toolchain *component* (a pin blob). The wire has no field for the blob, so the descriptor's toolchain component is left unset by workspace.create.\n(4) workspace.create has no disk-import path on the wire at all — the IDL's request is content identities only — so DiskImporter is not reachable through an operation; the disk boundary stays out-of-band, which is also what keeps the operation layer pure."}	blake3:aSfhlGdKwM3VyyjvO8g0rbkUmkhEphimocCaxcNpRAo
1785613499569173	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-3gi	{"body":"PR-5 START_HERE annotation decision: NOT annotating pr-5-impl-01/02 as delivered. START_HERE's PR-5 bullets say 'Implement request/response types **and local transport** for the plan §10.2 operation names', and the transport half is not in this bone: RFC 0026 fixes two encodings but neither their canonical field order nor their union tagging, and the IDL's open item 3 leaves the envelope's Opaque payloads without a declared shape, so a codec here would be invented wire format. What this bone delivers is the daemon-behaviour half — the operation layer plus both families — with typed arguments/payloads travelling beside the envelope, exactly where a codec plugs in. The lead may take a different view; the honest statement is that bullets 1 and 2 are one half delivered, and the START_HERE text already says a bullet needs both halves."}	blake3:bWv-wsFbsOeRqkOT42hMGtQGoDljYrMDIbYE_I9J2II
1785613541107309	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-3gi	{"body":"Delivered and committed. 10 modules under crates/continuumd/src/daemon (mod, family, admission, obligation, errors, result, state, identity, workspace, intent) + 34 tests in crates/continuumd/tests/daemon_operations.rs. just check exits 0 (fmt, clippy -D warnings workspace-wide, cargo test --workspace --locked, boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier, Lean). Dependency additions continuum-workspace and continuum-intent carry rationale + TCB class in tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml (GOV-1-07 passes). protocol/ untouched."}	blake3:GEsNutCIIklzdyFi0fI-PzEkCZ1Phiy7h28Qt3EbQHM
1785613617583384	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1Ac		item.create	bn-i4aem	{"description":"# Context\nThree wire-contract defects surfaced by the first daemon bone (full analysis in bn-3gi's comments and daemon module docs):\n1. RULE CONFLICT: errors.unsupported_surface requires UnsupportedSemanticFeature for unshipped operation surfaces, but errors.common forbids it for the eleven operations declaring errors [] — the daemon resolved in favour of unsupported_surface in one documented path; the IDL must reconcile (likely: add the code to the common union or to those operations' error sets).\n2. SnapshotComponents.files: list<Commitment> carries NO PATHS — a snapshot is not reconstructable from a workspace.create request alone; the daemon covers it with a documented staging area keyed on canonical (path, content) records. The wire shape should carry paths (compatible additive change under 3.1+).\n3. SnapshotEpochs.toolchain is an epoch, not the descriptor's toolchain component blob, which has no wire field at all.\nAny IDL change must be mirrored in crates/continuumd/src/protocol/ in the same commit (the conformance suite enforces) and follows RFC 0026's versioning rules (compatible additions; minor bump discipline — check whether these ride 3.1 pre-freeze or need 3.2).\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each defect reconciled in the IDL + RFC 0026 with direction recorded, or explicitly deferred with reason.\n- [ ] continuumd protocol types + conformance suite green; daemon paths updated where shapes changed.\n- [ ] just check green.","kind":"bug","title":"IDL fixes from the daemon implementation (bn-3gi findings)"}	blake3:CQGFyDlhst395PB5emQtVYyD9iy-xjtGBKBtbSSXyCU
1785613635170424	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1Qc		item.update	bn-i4aem	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"protocol"}}	blake3:X0axhxOmYDSDrLkZ3uCiPaj1KJm8aopi877BJSYLd6A
1785613635198620	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1gc		item.update	bn-i4aem	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-5"}}	blake3:znLhQ8djNicGExbQpPfNyv8dhTSK3ECbALG86TF9kv0
1785613635227331	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1wc		item.comment	bn-3gi	{"body":"Landed @ 5f65e00. Lead ran all 8 continuumd suites in-workspace (green). Ratified: the eight-step registry-driven dispatch with its documented ordering decisions (shape-before-admission for T2; admission-before-ledger against probing); the errors.common debug_assert catching stray codes; LineageError::Unknown → CapabilityDenied (a distinguishable not-found IS X2's oracle — exactly right); the unsupported_surface-over-common resolution as the documented interim; the annotation-declined call on pr-5-impl-01/02 (local transport genuinely not here). Three wire defects routed to bn-i4aem. The family seam + inheritance list is the siblings' contract — bn-18z/bn-24i dispatch next against a READ-ONLY skeleton."}	blake3:A_sqxAcm9Xj4uyjzj2QjycKXnUCACmGSPor6lvi_mV8
1785613635258868	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2Ac		item.move	bn-3gi	{"state":"done"}	blake3:nnMGXWZHwkOdkKlDAc9ASHJrTw4NIHeCNa-mS-vGl64
1785613661682183	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2Qc		item.update	bn-89km	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-5-impl-01"}}	blake3:sJHCtdkglNOu8QQS9rDXw6OKkPPFzsUj0rH4hbVSJX8
1785613661710843	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2gc		item.update	bn-89km	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-5-impl-02"}}	blake3:U8SBhYObASQYznms4AuRe7Tfl0Foky3OboMvcgMt37w
1785615306844124	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-24i	{"body":"Wire-surface reading, settled before writing code. The bone's brief names evidence.append and handshake→mint/delegate/revoke; registry::OPERATIONS and the IDL declare neither. What the wire actually exposes for my namespaces is evidence.get/query/verify/subscribe and observe.ingest/classify/result, plus the connection handshake. So: (1) there is NO evidence-append verb — the only wire verb that appends an evidence node is observe.ingest, which admission::required_grant was already written for ('observe.ingest → production_trace … the sibling that lands observe should inherit the check'), so observe is implemented here as the producer half; (2) there is no capability namespace and no wire mint/delegate/revoke — RFC 0026 correction 20 makes administration out-of-band at major 3 precisely so 'no operation in this protocol can widen the authority of the connection that invokes it' (INV-015), and continuum-workspace's ReferenceStore::mint/revoke are deliberately given no wire caller. What is implemented is the three of the handshake's five things that need daemon state (authority, limits, epoch position) beside the two protocol::handshake::negotiate already decides."}	blake3:Wf-9saz1sxIDsZSJKM6-Obi_ZRveiL2CsUkBL_aAlik
1785615317254499	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAg		item.comment	bn-24i	{"body":"Fence note — two skeleton files touched, both at the documented extension point, no behaviour changed for the landed families. (1) daemon/family.rs: seven Arguments + seven Payload variants, which is step 1 of the four-step seam family.rs itself documents ('Adding the next one — task, evidence, verification — is these four edits and no others'). A closed enum has no other way to admit a namespace. The task/verification sibling will add its variants to the same two enums; the conflict is textual and additive. (2) daemon/family.rs + daemon/result.rs: Effect gained an 'assurance: Optional<AssuranceEnvelope>' and result::success now copies it. This is forced, not convenient: evidence.verify declares 'verdict SemanticVerdictValue', and rule envelope.assurance_required says a result whose verdict is semantic or evaluation MUST carry the nine-dimension envelope with every dimension naming an engine or a typed Unsupported(reason). The skeleton hardcoded 'assurance: Optional::Absent' with a comment saying neither landed family produces one — true then, false for the first semantic verdict. Absent would have been non-conforming. daemon/mod.rs gained only 'pub mod capability; pub mod evidence; pub mod observe;'. admission.rs, obligation.rs, errors.rs and all of src/protocol/ are untouched."}	blake3:8LUvUm6TJooKuVQP_gkxfgBc43j6YWunUw7HlkZKDII
1785615326409590	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-24i	{"body":"INV-004 enforcement design, in four places, each a type rather than a check. (1) No wire field carries a status into a write: evidence.verify's only status input is expected_status, the compare-and-set GUARD; the written status comes from what the daemon re-derived. (2) The producer's append cannot name a status at all — observe.ingest declares 'trace' and 'instrumentation_profile' and nothing else — so an appended node lands at ClaimStatus::BOTTOM as a property of the wire's shape; a test asserts the registry's declared field list to pin that. (3) DaemonState::promote_evidence takes a daemon::evidence::Promotion whose fields are private to that module and which has no public constructor, and the producer family lives in daemon::observe, a DIFFERENT module — so producer-side code physically cannot reach the status write. That is why observe.rs is a separate file from evidence.rs; merging them would silently hand the ability back. Same device admission::Denied uses for X1. (4) A verification service may not verify its own production: EvidenceFamily::service is compared against the node's provenance.actor (written from the admitted grant, never from the request body) and the promotion is refused with InsufficientEvidence. Chose InsufficientEvidence over CertificateRejected: nothing was rejected BY a checker — there was no independent checker — and CertificateRejected would falsely impugn valid evidence. The IDL has no code for 'the only available checker is the producer'; noted as a vocabulary observation, not papered over."}	blake3:HyF7A4RvKmhGapiLAOewy9ePObdKQvAXNog8M3TKb60
1785615568619127	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-24i	{"body":"Per-operation semantics. evidence.get (read): resolves the node or denies (X2, never a not-found); returns the evidence-graph-node record as canonical JSON conforming to evidence-graph-node.schema.json, with the three conditional members (service_identity, validation_basis, inconclusive_reason) emitted exactly when the schema's if/then clauses admit them — additionalProperties is false, so a field written where the schema forbids it is as invalid as one missing where it requires it. edge reads null: no operation in the 72 appends an edge, and inventing an edge record would be inventing an artifact. Redacted arm per RFC 0026 'Redacted values': the stub travels BESIDE the record (never in place of it) plus an omissions entry with reason=redaction (INV-007); all three reasons round-trip. evidence.query (read, paginated): conjunctive filter over node_kinds/statuses/claim_id/roots, BTreeMap key order (rule pagination.deterministic), edges always empty and honestly so. evidence.verify (read authority, @mutation @task_starting, semantic verdict): the promotion path — see the INV-004 comment. evidence.subscribe: typed UnsupportedSemanticFeature. Its response body is a frontier but the OPERATION is a stream (events EvidenceEvent), and delivering deltas is the transport half this layer does not contain; a frontier from a subscription that can never deliver an event is the 'empty success' rule errors.unsupported_surface forbids. The daemon-side half IS built — every committed delta is recorded in DaemonState::evidence_events — so only the channel is missing. Note evidence.subscribe is not in START_HERE's PR-5 bullet either (the bullet reads 'evidence.get / query / verify')."}	blake3:KpYGxJ0M4jhZRrZJdkNTsAdDLY9yEpsXW3UXgU5lmNA
1785615578948351	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBQ		item.comment	bn-24i	{"body":"evidence.verify's independent check, and what it deliberately will NOT establish. The check re-derives the whole reference chain through the same ContentIdentifier seam that named each link: ev_ handle derives from (artifact commitment, instrumentation profile), and the artifact commitment derives from (path, content bytes held). Both arrows are recomputed and compared. The handle-to-node arrow is the one an attacker can bend — file a node under someone else's identity, or retarget an existing node at other content — and both are refused CertificateRejected with tests. The derivation is evidence::node_identity, called from observe::ingest too, so the check runs against the rule rather than a second copy of it. The status it establishes is Observed and NOTHING HIGHER: per the lattice chain (Proposed < Inconclusive < Observed < Sampled < Bounded < Validated < Proved), a re-derived reference to a captured execution supports 'one or more concrete executions'. Sampled/Bounded name a producing engine this daemon does not run; Validated needs the independent certificate checker (continuum-certificate, still a scaffold); Proved needs the Lean kernel, out of process. Asked to verify a node of such a class, verify answers InsufficientEvidence rather than promoting on a check that did not run. Refusing to overstate is INV-004 one level below authority. Vocabulary observation for the lead: EvidenceVerifyResponse.validation_basis is 'required' but its two admitted members (checked-certificate / trusted-solver) have no member for 'reference re-derivation'. I report checked-certificate on an established promotion (the daemon's own identity kernel did the checking, not a trusted solver) and trusted-solver on the redacted arm (no check ran). Inventing a third member would be a schema change, not a string."}	blake3:X7yM8yY9iTWie4zZaZHwXlncFnze6XTh97iWjfcMisk
1785615615173942	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBg		item.comment	bn-24i	{"body":"ANNOTATION DECISIONS — both declined, with reasons.\n\n(1) plan.md §2 INV-004 heading: NOT annotated. My honest reading is that INV-004's controls are NOT all mechanically evidenced, on three independent grounds. (a) SCOPE of 'Certificates cross an independent checker': the one behavioural artifact is crates/continuum-engine-reference/tests/certificate_kernel_differential.rs — one certificate family (finite closure), one engine (the reference engine), one kernel (kernel-core), with a 512-mutant non-vacuity sweep. kernel-sat/-smt/-temporal have real checkers but every input is a hand-built cfg(test) fixture, the four optimized engines are 21-line scaffolds, and continuum-certificate — the crate plan §20 names as THE certificate checker — is still a 23-line doc comment with no types. bn-11yx's own tests assert only that its Cargo.toml is empty. The heading's plural overstates a singular artifact. (b) A DIMENSION I COULD NOT REACH: plan.md:2751 — 'evidence-graph-edge requires checker on CHECKED_BY (INV-004)' — is unenforced, and not for want of trying: NO operation among the 72 appends an evidence EDGE, so the daemon's graph holds nodes only and there is no code path where a CHECKED_BY edge could be written or validated. What I did deliver is that rule's semantics without its artifact: evidence.verify records the checking service identity on the status write and reports it as the wire's 'checker', and refuses when that identity equals the node's provenance.actor. (c) The Lean half is real but its 'no sorry / empty axiom manifest' property is enforced by artifact-diff, not by an assertion — nothing asserts theoremsWithAxioms == 0, so a sorry landed together with a regenerated manifest would pass the gate. Also: no §2 invariant heading carries a (delivered: …) annotation today, and bn-11yx's ratified closing comment already flagged the no-precedent point 'for when the first one genuinely completes'. This is not that moment. Recommend the coverage stay open under a successor bone scoped to (b).\n\n(2) START_HERE PR-5 bullets req:pr-5-impl-04 (evidence.get/query/verify) and req:pr-5-impl-05 (capability negotiation): NOT annotated, following the bn-3gi precedent the lead ratified for impl-01/02. START_HERE's PR-5 stem reads 'Implement request/response types AND LOCAL TRANSPORT for the plan §10.2 operation names', and its own note says 'each still needs the local transport and the daemon behaviour that make it observable'. The daemon-behaviour half is here; the transport half is not, for the reason the skeleton already documents — RFC 0026 fixes two encodings but neither their canonical field order nor their union tagging, and the IDL's open item 3 leaves the envelope's Opaque payloads without a declared shape, so a codec here would be invented wire format. The lead may take a different view; the honest statement is that both bullets are one half delivered."}	blake3:MFqTjVUhYldmCOl0NFXfZe0jsq_6j9mwVb19p8vhh3w
1785615681147310	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBw		item.comment	bn-24i	{"body":"Delivered and committed @ ac07089. Three new modules under crates/continuumd/src/daemon (evidence 987 lines, observe 301, capability 338) + 43 tests in crates/continuumd/tests/daemon_evidence.rs, all green; the landed 34 daemon_operations tests still green. Full just check exits 0 (fmt, clippy -D warnings workspace-wide, cargo test --workspace --locked, boundaries, kernel covenant, governance incl. GOV-1-07, dossier, Lean). Traceability regenerated — no diff, because no plan/START_HERE annotation was taken (see the annotation-decisions comment). One dependency added: continuum-evidence, with rationale and tcb_class trusted-checking-base in tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml (used_by extended to continuumd). src/protocol/, admission.rs, obligation.rs and errors.rs are untouched. Test breakdown: 12 INV-004 (append lands at bottom; the registry's declared field lists pin that no producer field reaches a status; the checker establishes rather than the caller naming; a promoted guard loses the CAS; self-certification refused with the status unchanged, plus its control where only the producing identity differs; a deployment's own service identity is what the comparison uses; certificate-class refused because no independent checker ran; dangling reference refused; mis-filed identity and retargeted reference both CertificateRejected; X2 byte-identical denial), 4 append-only (re-ingest converges on the original identity and emits no second delta; re-ingest after a promotion does not lower the claim; the history grows and verify_promotion_history accepts it; no operation in either family removes or edits a node), 4 redaction (all three reasons round-trip through evidence.get with the INV-007 omission; verify returns structural success + the redaction + an inconclusive semantic verdict + a fully downgraded envelope), 2 assurance (nine dimensions on the semantic verdict, none on the structural one), 8 evidence/observe reads, 10 capability negotiation (grant downgrade, version downgrade and window refusal, feature intersection, one-answer refusal across unregistered/wrong-actor/expired/revoked, frameless close for a pre-3.1 client, R1/R2/E3 revocation, E1 expiry judged per request, D6/D7 escalation refused before the family runs, R-4 production-trace grant), 3 conformance."}	blake3:HQod1uzE1HZnHTc_sLosQVGMwaIIHj4KFVoyL9ILWc8
1785615780082788	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3wc		item.comment	bn-24i	{"body":"Landed @ 8628470. Lead ran all 9 continuumd suites in-workspace (77 tests green). Ratified: the wire-surface reading (no evidence.append verb, no capability namespace — the brief was wrong twice and the agent implemented the IDL that exists); the four-place type-level INV-004 enforcement (the private-constructor Promotion witness in a different module from the producer family is the standout — X1's device reapplied); promote-to-Observed-and-nothing-higher (a check that did not run promotes nothing); InsufficientEvidence over CertificateRejected for producer-is-only-checker (nothing was rejected BY a checker); evidence.subscribe as unsupported_surface (a stream that can never deliver is the empty success the rule forbids); the forced Effect.assurance skeleton edit accepted (envelope.assurance_required obliges it). Both annotation declines ratified on their grounds. Concerns 2-5 routed to bn-i4aem; INV-004's unreachable dimension (CHECKED_BY edges — no verb appends an edge) becomes bn-i4aem item 4 and bn-i4aem inherits req:inv-004."}	blake3:PvxEAmK1V8SHYeMC7RUd6IMCLV9h7qbpSJ70Qa8_pbI
1785615780112463	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4Ac		item.move	bn-24i	{"state":"done"}	blake3:Z98APRRc247SymBqNrT2d2e-F6l9Qs47X4IvwP2Fp_E
1785615780141168	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4Qc		item.update	bn-i4aem	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:inv-004"}}	blake3:oK-xG2VNsd9I2rcvDBoD9O2zNeKhp6uwS8mnNO6MvqY
1785615780171291	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4gc		item.comment	bn-i4aem	{"body":"Four more items from bn-24i: (4) plan.md:2751 requires checker on CHECKED_BY edges (INV-004) but NO operation among the 72 appends an edge — the wire needs an edge-append surface or the requirement needs rescoping; this bone now carries req:inv-004 for that dimension. (5) EvidenceVerifyResponse.validation_basis lacks a member for reference re-derivation (only checked-certificate/trusted-solver). (6) No error code for 'the only available checker is the producer' — InsufficientEvidence is the nearest honest fit; consider a dedicated code. (7) observe.classify's Opaque has no declared schema. All follow RFC 0026 versioning; mirror in continuumd protocol types per the conformance discipline."}	blake3:sd6RYCZ0CSGJHIQyOVdZa7_xEY2njlinsaYv1kvjVh8
1785615798537647	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4wc		item.update	bn-89km	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-5-impl-04"}}	blake3:aGWhedW7zYmMtbz4sQJltEXcXv7oXmhbi5rQy0NpLUc
1785615798566553	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5Ac		item.update	bn-89km	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-5-impl-05"}}	blake3:WTnvQEtvue1ThxjLg6GxJlFrg4TM9BniBQ7gsRDVwp4
1785615666087372	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-18z	{"body":"Design settled after reading the landed daemon skeleton, RFC 0026's task-lifecycle and resume clauses, the IDL's task.*/verification.* shapes, and continuum-engine-reference's bfs/checking/witness/diehard surfaces.\n\nTASK TABLE — synchronous-deterministic argument. A task is a value in DaemonState::tasks(), advanced only by an explicit dispatch. Daemon::dispatch takes &mut self, so TaskStatus::Running is representable and structurally unobservable: it is the state a task is in *during* the dispatch that advances it, and no second dispatch can see it while the &mut DaemonState borrow is held. The statuses a caller can read are Suspended, Completed, Failed, Cancelled. rule task.status_monotonic is enforced by construction — TaskEntry::advance is the ONLY writer of status and refuses every transition out of a terminal state; milestones and committed_evidence are append-only.\n\nCONTINUATION PARKING. bfs::Exploration::Exhausted already carries the explored set, the queue-ordered frontier, and which bound tripped — the frontier IS the continuation payload, so the type-level seam was already there and this bone only binds a cont_* handle to it. Continuation pins snapshot + intent + frontier + bounds + PinnedEpochs (five compatibility epochs + engine identity). PinnedEpochs deliberately has NO protocol field, so RFC 0026's 'a continuation MUST leave protocol Unpinned' is a property of the type rather than a step somebody remembers to skip.\n\nIDENTITY. task_* is the content identity of (snapshot, intent, target, portfolio, priority_class, budget, epochs); cont_* is the content identity of what the continuation pins. Nothing is drawn, counted, or timed. So the PR-5 exit ('replaying an idempotent request returns the same task identity') holds BEFORE the ledger is consulted — the ledger then makes the whole outcome byte-identical. It is also literally what the IDL asks verification.start for: 'a cached result when one exists for the same snapshot, intent, target, and epochs' is a lookup by that identity."}	blake3:UaxU4mV_C67Aq7RwW4QvdDvrxEclYfcTE6eXMqm9Blw
1785615691610167	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAg		item.comment	bn-18z	{"body":"PER-OPERATION SEMANTICS, with citations.\n\nverification.start (@mutation @task_starting, errors [UnsupportedSemanticFeature, InsufficientEvidence, BudgetExhausted, UntrustedDomainBoundary]) — envelope.snapshot required (null => MalformedRequest); the snapshot must be SEALED (unsealed => StaleSnapshot, which is that code's own definition: 'not current, OR is not sealed where sealing is required') and current in its lineage via staleness::check_current (superseded => StaleSnapshot). Then the MODEL BOUNDARY, below. Runs synchronously: Exploration::Complete => Completed; Exploration::Exhausted => Suspended + cont_*. ExplorationError::InitialStatesExceedBound => the task is CREATED and FAILED with failed_reason=BudgetExhausted + non_resumable_reason (RFC 0026 requires that field exactly here) rather than start returning a fault — this layer's failure envelope has nowhere to put non_resumable_reason.\n\nverification.result / verification.await — NOT in the bone's scope list, but unavoidable: dispatch routes by NAMESPACE, so registering a 'verification' family captures all three, and their union (COMMON + BudgetExhausted + ContinuationEpochMismatch) does NOT admit UnsupportedSemanticFeature, so refusing them would trip mod.rs's own errors.common debug_assert. Both are served. They are also where the CheckReport-shaped payload actually rides: verification.result's 'verdict SemanticVerdictValue' clause is the only wire home for the engine's three-way verdict. await == result at this grain and the IDL's own sentence is why — 'waiting never changes a verdict', and a task is already terminal or already parked before any operation can look at it, so timeout_ms bounds nothing.\n\ntask.status (errors []) — projects TaskEntry onto TaskRecord. Unknown task => Fault::denied(), byte-identical (X2); there is no UnknownTask code to build an oracle out of.\n\ntask.subscribe (errors [], @streaming) — SERVED, not refused. The IDL's response body is one field ('snapshot of the record at subscription time; events follow') and that is exactly what a codec-free layer can answer; the events that follow are TaskEntry::events, the recorded transitions, which a transport replays. rule subscription.hints_only makes it correct rather than approximate ('a client MUST be able to recover the same state by re-reading'). The bn-i4aem errors[]/unsupported_surface conflict does NOT bite: that rule is about an operation registered ahead of its producing SUBSYSTEM, and the task table is not a missing subsystem. Only common codes are returned, which the empty clause permits.\n\ntask.cancel (errors [PublicationAborted], verdict StructuralVerdictValue) — rule task.cancel_correct's request/drain/finalize are one step because a dispatch is atomic (it holds &mut DaemonState for its whole extent), so there is never a publication in progress to truncate (INV-017) and no leak is possible by construction at this grain. Suspended => Cancelled carrying its continuation ('committed partial evidence plus a valid continuation'); no continuation => Nullable::Null, the NAMED 'nothing published' outcome the nullable field exists for. Terminal => StructuralOutcome::Unchanged, because rule task.status_monotonic says a terminal status never changes.\n\ntask.update_budget (errors [BudgetExhausted, QuotaExhausted], verdict StructuralVerdictValue) — at synchronous grain there is no run to extend WHILE it runs, so rule task.update_budget's 'raising a dimension extends the current run' is served in two visible steps: this operation records the new budget and leaves the task Suspended with its continuation intact, and task.resume re-runs under it. The task identity does not change (the budget is a field of the entry and never re-enters the handle preimage) — that is the PR-5 exit's second clause. Terminal task => Unchanged and keeps the budget it ran under, because a terminal task's budget is a historical fact.\n\ntask.resume (errors [StaleSnapshot, ContinuationEpochMismatch, EpochUnsupported, BudgetExhausted]) — RFC 0026's decision table in order: unheld continuation => CapabilityDenied (X2); envelope.snapshot names a snapshot other than the pinned one => StaleSnapshot (the R3 spike §3 behaviour, 'rejection of continuation under a different workspace snapshot'; it reads the ENVELOPE because the request body carries only continuation+budget); pinned snapshot unheld/unsealed/superseded => StaleSnapshot; a pinned epoch whose kind the daemon pins nothing for => EpochUnsupported; a pinned epoch that disagrees => ContinuationEpochMismatch (P1); pinned engine identity disagrees => ContinuationEpochMismatch (P2). BOTH predicates are checked and neither implies the other (RFC 0026 'The two-predicate obligation'). Resume of a TERMINAL task is a monotone no-op returning the terminal status: monotonicity forbids changing it, resume 'MUST NOT replace prior artifacts under the same identity', and this operation's union has no code for 'already terminal' — pressing one of the common five into that service would name something false."}	blake3:HUaHub5NflxQycGnD8cBcL59bjDu9T_3qF63caqb6kQ
1785615711029553	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-18z	{"body":"THE verification.start MODEL-CONSTRUCTION BOUNDARY (the honest line, drawn where the truth is).\n\nA campaign needs a Model and the only path from a snapshot to one is a CML front end. There is none: the Finite core fragment's elaborator is PR 15a, continuum-cml-syntax/elab are scaffolds, and continuum-engine-reference/src/diehard.rs says outright that 'until the CML front end exists, this module IS the port'. So:\n\n- a deployment REGISTERS the models this daemon can construct, out of band through DaemonState::models_mut(), keyed by the CONTENT IDENTITY of the CML source they were transcribed from. Out of band for the same reason capability minting and content staging are (IDL §7: administration is not an operation);\n- verification.start derives that identity from the sealed snapshot's own *.ctm modules — a length-prefixed (path, content) record over every module in path order, the same canonical-record discipline DaemonState::stage uses — and looks it up. Not registered => UnsupportedSemanticFeature, the code rule errors.unsupported_surface requires AND this operation's errors clause declares. Never a degraded answer, an empty success, or a guess.\n\nKeying on SOURCE bytes rather than on the snapshot handle is the fail-closed direction and it is tested both ways: a fork that edits README.md still resolves to the same model; a fork that edits DieHard.ctm resolves to nothing (a changed model is a different model).\n\nOther refusals on this path: Portfolio::Custom => UnsupportedSemanticFeature (the wire carries no custom-portfolio definition to read). TargetKind Obligation/Refinement/Program/BenchmarkTask => UnsupportedSemanticFeature (not questions about finite reachability). A Target.id naming no declared predicate => MalformedRequest — there is no 'unknown property' code in the §10.3 taxonomy, and this is not an X2 oracle because the caller is already admitted for the snapshot. ExplorationError::Evaluation (transition relation undefined at a reachable state, docs/16 PO-MOD-003 failing) => UnsupportedSemanticFeature with NO task created, deliberately: resuming would fail identically because the engine is deterministic.\n\nPortfolio otherwise does not change a verdict: a portfolio selects engines and this daemon has one, so interactive and promotion select the same lane and the portfolio is recorded rather than acted on. The deadlock completion policy is the intent contract's (checking.rs says so) and reading it off the contract is RFC 0037 work this bone does not do; DeadlockPolicy::Defect is taken instead of a guess, and it is the SAFE direction — it can only turn a terminal state into a reported defect, never hide one.\n\nBUDGET => BOUNDS. Of RFC 0026's nine budget dimensions this daemon can honestly enforce exactly one: states, the engine's own state bound. wall_ms/cpu_ms need a clock INV-005 keeps out of the core, memory_bytes an allocator this layer does not own, and solver_ms/proof_ms/tokens/candidates/bytes name lanes that are not running. Depth and transitions have NO wire dimension and take Bounds::CERTIFIABLE (the kernel wire form's own declared ceilings, not an unreviewed default). Every dimension a caller DECLARES and this daemon does not enforce is reported as a typed Omission — INV-007, because silently ignoring a declared ceiling is precisely what that rule exists to prevent."}	blake3:DJvpquujJ3Au7_vUxymK7n10Jpn7mO2b3c5tVprEDD4
1785615771897720	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-18z	{"body":"SKELETON: one additive change, and why the workaround was not available.\n\nThe fence said family.rs is read-only. Two of the four steps of family.rs's OWN documented recipe are edits to family.rs (Arguments/Payload variants), and Arguments is a closed enum that OperationFamily::handle takes by reference — there is no way to route a task.*/verification.* request without a variant, so those edits are the declared seam and were made. Beyond them I made exactly ONE additive change, and it is the one thing the skeleton genuinely lacked:\n\n  Effect gains `assurance: Optional[AssuranceEnvelope]`, defaulted Absent by Effect::new, and result::success reads it instead of hard-coding Optional::Absent.\n\nWhy there was no workaround. verification.result and verification.await declare `verdict SemanticVerdictValue`; the dispatcher debug_asserts that a result carries a verdict exactly when its clause declares one, so they MUST emit a semantic verdict. rule envelope.assurance_required then says a semantic verdict MUST carry the nine-dimension envelope. result::success hard-coded it absent and Effect had no channel for it, so with the fence taken literally the only options were (a) emit a rule-violating envelope, (b) return a code outside the operations' union (UnsupportedSemanticFeature is NOT in COMMON u [BudgetExhausted, ContinuationEpochMismatch] — mod.rs's own debug_assert catches it), or (c) return CapabilityDenied for a task the daemon demonstrably holds, which is a lie X2 does not license. The change is additive (every existing call site and every existing test is unchanged; both older continuumd suites still green), it is family OUTPUT — the same kind of thing as verdict/artifacts/omissions/warnings, which Effect already carries — and it is none of the concerns the fence enumerates (version/admission/audit/obligation/ledger/epoch/cost/error-union). Three dimensions name continuum-engine-reference as producer; six carry typed Unsupported reasons (one of them the IDL's own 'sequential-consistency-only' example). Lead: revert it and verification.result/await become unshippable at this layer — say so if you would rather they were dropped.\n\nTwo further state.rs additions, both new sections, neither touching the ledger, the capability registry, workspaces, intents, or staged content: `tasks: TaskTable` with tasks()/tasks_mut(), and `models: ModelCatalog` with models()/models_mut(). The task table must live in DaemonState because OperationFamily::handle takes &self and &mut DaemonState — a family cannot hold mutable state of its own.\n\nNEW WIRE DEFECTS FOUND (protocol/ NOT edited; candidates for bn-i4aem):\n(5) Cost declares NO transition dimension, and no wire field carries a witness path short of a crash_* crashpack, so two of the four frozen TV-009 facts — 96 labelled transitions and the depth-6 shortest witness — cannot reach a client at protocol 3.1 even though the daemon computes both. They are held on TaskEntry::campaign and reachable through Daemon::state(); Cost.states carries the 16.\n(6) EpochSet.protocol is `required`, so an EpochSet cannot express RFC 0026's mandatory \"a continuation MUST leave protocol Unpinned\". Covered here by a separate PinnedEpochs type with no protocol field; a continuation-shaped epoch set belongs in the IDL.\n(7) task.resume's errors clause has no code for \"the model this continuation named is no longer one the daemon can construct\". Covered by collapsing any code outside the operation's union to the single X2 denial, documented at the call site: at X2's grain a continuation whose model went away is indistinguishable from one the daemon does not hold.\n(8) task.resume's and task.update_budget's responses carry no field able to report a task that PARKED AGAIN under a new continuation, so a client reads task.status — which rule subscription.hints_only already makes authoritative. A documented reliance rather than a defect.\n(9) result::success hard-codes ResultEnvelope.status = Ok and .task/.continuation = Absent, so a @task_starting operation cannot emit ResultStatus::TaskStarted/TaskSuspended with task+continuation on the envelope. Nothing is unreachable — the typed payload carries the task handle and task.status carries the continuation — but the envelope-level lane is skeleton work I did not take. Flagging rather than editing."}	blake3:4xFkXZXccacLT2tvPsoEYK12PEcGqTSL_HasYaMFI3I
1785615789339416	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBQ		item.comment	bn-18z	{"body":"PR-5 START_HERE ANNOTATION DECISION: declining pr-5-impl-03 and pr-5-impl-06, on bn-3gi's ratified precedent.\n\nSTART_HERE's PR-5 lead-in governs all six bullets: \"Implement request/response types **and local transport** for the plan §10.2 operation names\". The transport half is still absent for the same reason bn-3gi recorded and the lead ratified — RFC 0026 fixes two encodings but neither their canonical field order nor their union tagging, and the IDL's open item 3 leaves the envelope's Opaque payloads without a declared shape, so a codec here would be invented wire format. This bone delivers the daemon-behaviour half of bullet 3 (verification.start; task.status/cancel/resume/subscribe, plus task.update_budget and, unavoidably, verification.result/await) and exercises bullet 6's typed errors and idempotency keys end to end, with typed arguments and payloads travelling beside the envelope exactly where a codec plugs in. Consistent with bullets 1 and 2, that is one half of each. The lead may take a different view; the honest statement is the same one bn-3gi made.\n\nWhat IS demonstrable now, and the lead should weigh it against bn-89km: the PR-5 EXIT — \"replaying an idempotent request returns the same task/artifact identity\" — is asserted at the operation layer by `replaying_an_idempotent_verification_start_returns_the_same_task_identity` (identical replay returns the identical outcome AND the identical task_* handle, with one task in the table), by `reusing_an_idempotency_key_for_a_different_request_is_rejected` (IdempotencyKeyReused, no partial effect), by `a_second_start_under_a_new_key_returns_the_cached_result` (same identity by a second route, no second campaign), and by `the_task_identity_is_a_function_of_the_campaign_and_of_nothing_else` (two independently built daemons name the same task; a different budget names a different one). The identity holds by construction rather than by the ledger, which is a stronger form of the exit than the ledger alone gives.\n\nSimilarly the daemon-API half of the PR-8 exit — \"Die Hard returns 16 states and depth-6 solution through the daemon API\" — is `die_hard_returns_sixteen_states_and_a_depth_six_solution_through_the_daemon_api`: verification.start over the sealed TV-009 snapshot, task.status reporting cost.states = 16 on the wire, and the campaign's own 96 labelled transitions, max depth 7, and NotSolved refuted at depth 6. Minus transport, as the fence says. PR-8's bullets are already annotated delivered by the engine bones, so nothing is annotated for it here."}	blake3:n35WISiq3okp-EeXA8mJN6rJ8S6uFDtuWbSkTz7_vc4
1785615863025110	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBg		item.comment	bn-18z	{"body":"Delivered and committed at 762c52d. Two new modules under crates/continuumd/src/daemon (task.rs — the task table, continuations, PinnedEpochs, TaskFamily's five operations; verification.rs — the model catalog, VerificationFamily's three operations, the campaign runner and the assurance envelope) plus 32 tests in crates/continuumd/tests/daemon_task_operations.rs. Registration touched mod.rs (two pub mod lines), family.rs (8 Arguments + 8 Payload variants, the documented four-step seam, plus the one additive Effect.assurance field), result.rs (one line reading it) and state.rs (two new sections: the task table and the model catalog). protocol/ untouched. Nothing named \"evidence\" or \"capability\" touched.\n\njust check exits 0: fmt, clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -D warnings, cargo test --workspace --locked, boundaries (8 workspace-internal edges, continuumd -> continuum-engine-reference included, all 7 rules pass), kernel covenant, governance (GOV-1-01..12 pass, including GOV-1-07 with the new dependency-rationale entry classing continuum-engine-reference as `engine`), dossier (status pass), Lean. Traceability regen produced no diff — no labels or bone states changed.\n\nTest coverage, 32 cases: Die Hard end-to-end through dispatch with the frozen TV-009 facts (16 on the wire as cost.states, 96 transitions, max depth 7, NotSolved refuted at depth 6); the semantic verdict and the nine-dimension assurance envelope on verification.result; a single-property target establishing TypeOK; await == result; the full B18 loop (states=4 parks with a cont_*, interim result is inconclusive/ResourceExhausted carrying the continuation, update_budget re-admits with no identity churn, resume closes at 16, and every parked frontier state is asserted present in the resumed exploration — monotonicity checked, not assumed); a state budget of 0 failing the task non-resumably with failed_reason + non_resumable_reason; four resume-admissibility refusals through dispatch (different snapshot, advanced lineage, semantic-epoch disagreement, unheld corpus epoch, engine-identity disagreement) plus a direct unit test of the two-predicate obligation including the permissive unpinned case and the protocol-epoch non-participation; cancel at every reachable lifecycle point (suspended-with-continuation, twice, completed, failed, unknown); resume of a terminal task as a monotone no-op; idempotent replay, mismatched reuse, the cached-result lane, and cross-daemon identity determinism; the two denial paths asserted byte-identical (X2); unregistered model, unsealed snapshot, absent snapshot, custom portfolio, unsupported target kind, unknown predicate; the clockless daemon recording no milestone and naming the omission; and a table-driven check that every fault either family can raise is inside rule errors.common u the operation's own clause, plus a check that the two families answer for all 8 operations their namespaces declare (so neither namespace falls through to unsupported_surface).\n\nConcerns for the lead, in priority order:\n1. The Effect.assurance addition to family.rs/result.rs (comment 4). It is the one place I could not honour the read-only fence without either breaking rule envelope.assurance_required or putting an undeclared code on the wire. Additive; both older continuumd suites unchanged and green.\n2. verification.result and verification.await were outside the bone's stated scope but inside the namespace dispatch routes by, so they had to be served or the namespace could not be registered at all. They are also where the CheckReport-shaped payload actually rides.\n3. Five new wire defects (5)-(9) in comment 4, for bn-i4aem. (5) and (9) are the ones that cost real observability: two of the four frozen Die Hard facts and the ResultStatus::TaskStarted/TaskSuspended envelope lane.\n4. bn-24i lands concurrently and will also add Arguments/Payload variants in family.rs. Expect a merge in that enum; the hunks are disjoint from the Effect field."}	blake3:oDLPe911DK6xPovd7LSmAfaFhGDUk95ZkR7CsF4oVY0
1785615978570563	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5Qc		item.comment	bn-18z	{"body":"Landed @ f9b6d57 (lead union-resolved the predicted family.rs/result.rs enum merges — clean, only import ordering needed fmt; 10 suites green post-merge incl. the sibling's). Ratified: Running-structurally-unobservable via the &mut dispatch borrow; PinnedEpochs with no protocol field (the MUST as a type property); task/cont identities as content identities making the PR-5 exit hold BEFORE the ledger; the registered-models boundary keyed on .ctm source bytes (fail-closed both ways) as the honest no-CML-frontend answer; task.subscribe served under hints_only; the InitialStatesExceedBound created-failed-task shape; the second Effect.assurance addition (identical need, identical solution — convergent evidence the seam was right). Annotation declines ratified per precedent. THE PR-8 EXIT'S DAEMON-API HALF PASSES at the operation layer. Wire defects (5)-(9) routed to bn-i4aem."}	blake3:xT5Rcv9xc38MQ05CAot2lrbAVFB6g0Ra_ADfDt3CQDA
1785615978609471	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5gc		item.move	bn-18z	{"state":"done"}	blake3:ZW5_B5RtYhQ14ueWXLGNLwC6-97BJw4IKxaNpGBdm2o
1785615978649054	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5wc		item.comment	bn-i4aem	{"body":"Five more from bn-18z (items 8-12, full detail in bn-18z comment 4): the costly two — Cost has no transition dimension and no witness field (96 transitions/depth-6 are daemon-held only), and result::success hard-codes status=Ok with task/continuation absent so the TaskStarted/TaskSuspended envelope lane is unreachable. Plus the errors-[] interaction with task.subscribe resolved via hints_only (document), and two smaller shape gaps in bn-18z's comment."}	blake3:kc5X8cyPPYLOtEUUWlvDR32iSt-F5CrcsF8zS9mLPwE
1785615990197690	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6Ac		item.update	bn-89km	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-5-impl-03"}}	blake3:VREimnj-PcMO3RnA5Vdyz3IUpMCtb2Shcb-yRzMXeVc
1785615990227716	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6Qc		item.update	bn-89km	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-5-impl-06"}}	blake3:WwIfXHQ9BMJWMK2qrwWTGd6nnJWNoqs6touXTxuErec
1785616033351205	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6gc		item.create	bn-3bhkp	{"description":"# Context\nAll six PR-5 IMPL bullets are half-delivered: the operation layer is complete (bn-3gi/bn-18z/bn-24i — three families, dispatch skeleton, 126 daemon tests) but START_HERE's PR-5 lead-in requires 'request/response types and local transport', and the codec cannot exist until three things the IDL leaves unfixed are decided: canonical field order (RFC 0026 requires it identical in both encodings but never states it), union tagging, and the envelope's Opaque payloads (IDL open item 3). Those decisions are wire-format work coordinated with bn-i4aem's defect list.\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The three codec decisions taken and recorded in the IDL/RFC 0026 (coordinate with bn-i4aem; one minor bump discipline).\n- [ ] A local (in-process or socket) transport carrying canonical bytes end-to-end; envelope payload/arguments no longer Opaque-null at the boundary.\n- [ ] The six PR-5 IMPL bullets' remaining halves delivered; annotate them (delivered: bn-3gi/bn-18z/bn-24i + this bone) per the honesty precedent.\n- [ ] Golden byte vectors for representative operations (the conforming-daemon obligation bn-mtw recorded).\n- [ ] just check green.","kind":"task","title":"PR-5: local transport and canonical codec"}	blake3:Gv6e9S5ebPRHID3jpZl75J6rtjaB2dOgUKuIToKETdc
1785616046413511	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6wc		item.update	bn-3bhkp	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"protocol"}}	blake3:EIkK94yt-LCeL0xhtHz_q4S1fTdvM5j-hMuwCIvUB1M
