1785622740642050	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyogg		item.comment	bn-2gk	{"body":"Delivered (commit 3fed06f in .maw/workspaces/bn-2gk).\n\nDEPENDENCY WORLD: (c) in-between, resolved toward (b) by the dossier's own schedule. asupersync IS available — crates.io 0.3.10 (LicenseRef-MIT-OpenAI-Anthropic-Rider), locally cached in ~/.cargo/registry, source clone at /home/bob/repos/asupersync 0.3.9 — so integration was technically possible. It was NOT taken: plan §21 makes 'task/continuation lifecycle' a Phase A deliverable and the 'asupersync semantic adapter' a Phase B one landing in continuum-asupersync (PR 14, per that crate's own header); plan §20 assigns continuum-task the exact words of PR-6-IMPL-05/06; ADR-0001 requires model-only execution to remain possible. Declaring it now would add a full async runtime to a workspace whose one external edge is blake3. No dependency-rationale.toml entry was added (no dependency was added).\n\nWHAT LANDED: crates/continuum-task/src/region/{,worker,obligation,schedule}.rs — a deterministic region calculus. Region lifecycle Open -> Draining(Closed|Cancelled) -> Finalized; RFC 0026 request -> drain -> finalize; worker lifecycle = RFC 0026's six TaskStatus states with status_token() emitting their exact spellings (the daemon seam); 19 typed RegionFault variants; obligation ledger carrying RFC 0001's own named child-region-quiescence and cancellation-finalization plus worker-termination and provisional-publication. No thread, clock, executor or interior mutability.\n\nNO-ORPHAN PROOF: spawn refuses any non-Open region (the accounted set stops growing at the request); one private transition fn is the sole WorkerState writer and discharges worker-termination only on the terminal transition; finalize requires TERMINAL (not merely quiescent — a parked worker in a finalized region is a continuation into a dead scope). Finalization::is_total conjoins three independently computed facts (orphan set from worker states, unresolved set from evidence ledgers, ledger balance from an outstanding set AND opened/discharged counters).\n\nTESTS: 61 new (28 unit, 33 integration). 3,570 teardowns swept: 210 interleavings x {no injection, cancel-request at all 8 positions, cancel+drain at all 8}. Cancellation table has 13 phases x {state, outcome, committed count} plus balanced-ledger and committed-or-absent at every row.\n\nANNOTATIONS: PR-6-IMPL-05 (drain/finalize behavior) and PR-6-IMPL-06 (no orphan workers) annotated delivered. PR-6-IMPL-01 (task lifecycle) DECLINED — the stem says 'for daemon work' and continuumd's TaskTable is not rewired; the seam is designed, not done. DX-14's matrix row untouched (needs DPOR/solver/proof/synthesis engines, three of which do not exist).\n\nGATE: just check exit 0. Traceability regenerated, validator status=pass, program_status=ready."}	blake3:qBz9nSqTKHkkujAtZY06nN87FqZMxuMp0fLemW2YjUU
1785622849304392	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyowg		item.comment	bn-2gk	{"body":"Landed @ 7cabb7b. Lead ran all 6 continuum-task suites in-workspace (89 tests green). Ratified: the dependency-world resolution (three agreeing citations; Phase B deliverable not bought with a Phase A bone; no async runtime into the one-edge workspace); the terminal-not-quiescent finalize argument (a continuation into a dead scope); the both-or-neither CancelOutcome type; the three-independent-facts finalization report; the exhaustive-enumeration adaptation of the rendezvous house rules (interleaving-as-data). IMPL-01 DECLINE RATIFIED on the stricter reading — daemon work is in the stem; the rewiring bone delivers it. Concern 2 (Suspended cannot survive teardown) recorded as a design constraint for the rewiring bone — if a parked task must outlive its region, that is a design change to be surfaced BEFORE rewiring. Concern 3's Continuation-is-not-cont_* documentation accepted."}	blake3:5aIMIWA4lO-zyvH4qUJZ0M9Rc1c8HK1orQpnRHEXP7Y
