1785707071209495	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEylQk		item.comment	bn-1eqt	{"body":"INV-003 evidence delivered: crates/continuumd/tests/inv003_no_prose_only_evidence.rs (16 tests, all green). plan.md heading annotated `(delivered: bn-1eqt — crates/continuumd/tests/inv003_no_prose_only_evidence.rs: ...)`; traceability regenerated (`just traceability`); `validation-results.json` untouched.\n\nAUDIT — artifact-class table (also lives as Rust data, `artifact_classes::SCHEMA_BOUND`, anti-drift-checked against the repo):\n\n| class | binding | checker / evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| wire envelopes/operations (72, IDL) | typed wire — `continuumd-native-protocol.idl` | `idl_conformance.rs` (cited, not rebuilt) |\n| CONTCERT certificates (4 kernel crates) | typed wire — `wire.rs` byte grammar, `check_certificate(bytes: &[u8])` | `certificate_wire.rs`, `wire_form_boundary.rs` ×4 (cited) + `wire_bytes_not_prose` (new) |\n| 19 `notes/plan/schemas/*.schema.json` instance classes | schema, top-level `additionalProperties: false` + const-pinned `schema_id`/`schema_epoch` | GOV-1-12 (cited) + `schema_closure` (new, permanent Rust regression) |\n| intent registry record (representative) | schema, daemon-emitted | `schema_resolution` (new) — real `intent.accept` dispatch, response bytes parsed, `schema_id`/`schema_epoch` resolved to the real document |\n| evidence graph node (representative) | schema, daemon-emitted | `schema_resolution` (new) — real `observe.ingest` → `evidence.get` dispatch, same resolution |\n| recovery/reconciliation reports (`RecoveryReport`, plan §4.5) | typed wire — `Verdict`, 3-variant enum, no schema file | `g1_crash_recovery_evidence.rs` (cited) + `recovery_reports_are_typed` (new) |\n| governance/kernel-covenant evidence JSONs (`tools/*/evidence/*.json`) | typed wire — closed `status: pass\\|fail` + the producing checker's own self-test | the checkers themselves (cited only) |\n\nConfirmed no machine-READ path consumes prose: `continuumd`'s 72-operation registry is a table the daemon reads, never a program the wire writes; dispatch keys on the operation *name* (closed registry lookup), never a free-text field — INV-016's own structural leg proved this over all 25 landed operations, and this file's `dispatch_reads_structure_not_prose` module is a freshness tripwire on that citation (fails if INV-016's evidence file is ever gutted or renamed underneath it) rather than a re-derivation.\n\nCLAUSE → TEST MAP (7 modules, 16 tests):\n\n- `schema_closure` — mirrors GOV-1-12's `schemas-are-closed` + `instances-name-their-contract` as a **permanent Rust regression**, independent of the Python tool ever running: reads every `notes/plan/schemas/*.schema.json` fresh via `std::fs::read_dir` (a real directory listing, not a hand-copied list) through a from-scratch, whitespace-tolerant JSON reader (`continuumd::codec::json` is canonical-only and rejects pretty-printed input by construction, so a second independent parser was written rather than reused — docs/03 §8's \"independent paths\" device). 2 tests.\n- `artifact_classes` — the table above as data, checked bidirectionally against the real `schemas/` directory (new file with no row, or stale row, fails loudly — \"new machine artifact class without a schema\"). 3 tests.\n- `schema_resolution` — dispatches a real `Daemon` (the `inv002`/`inv016` fixture idiom, trimmed to `intent`+`evidence`+`observe`) through `intent.accept` and `observe.ingest`→`evidence.get`, parses the actual response bytes, and resolves the emitted `schema_id`/`schema_epoch` back to a real schema document by schemas/README.md's own mechanical recovery rule (\"insert `v<schema_epoch>/` before the last segment\"). 3 tests.\n- `wire_bytes_not_prose` — all four `continuum-kernel-*` crates' `check_certificate(bytes: &[u8]) -> Verdict` entry points (text-scanned, since this test file has no dependency on the four kernel crates) plus the `CONTCERT` magic independently declared by the kernel (checker) and the engine (producer). 3 tests.\n- `recovery_reports_are_typed` — `RecoveryReport`'s `Verdict` is a plain 3-variant enum; an exhaustive `match` with no wildcard arm is INV-003's \"schemas decide\" enforced by the compiler for an artifact class with no schema file. 1 test.\n- `dispatch_reads_structure_not_prose` — freshness tripwire on the INV-016 citation. 1 test.\n- `cited_enforcement_still_exists` — freshness tripwires on the GOV-1-10/11/12 and claim-governance citations. 3 tests.\n\nMUTANT STORY (anti-vacuity, one per leg that does real evaluation):\n- `schema_closure::the_closure_check_is_not_vacuous` — three independent mutations of the real `intent-registry-record.schema.json` text: top-level `additionalProperties` reopened to `true`, `schema_id` dropped from `required`, and the `const` pin dropped from `properties.schema_id`. Each is asserted caught.\n- `artifact_classes::dropping_one_table_row_breaks_the_comparison` — removing one row from the hand-maintained table must break the bidirectional comparison (inv016's own \"not vacuous\" device, reused).\n- `schema_resolution::a_schema_id_that_names_no_real_class_does_not_resolve` — a fabricated `schema_id` and a real one at an epoch no document declares must both resolve to nothing; the positive case is asserted to resolve first, so the negative isn't from a broken resolver.\n- `wire_bytes_not_prose::the_signature_check_is_not_vacuous` — a text-shaped signature (`&str`) must not be mistaken for a bytes-shaped one (`&[u8]`).\n\nCOUNTS: 1 new file, 16 tests, all green. 3 files regenerated/annotated (`plan.md`, `PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json`, `PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md`). No `src/` file touched or added — every leg reads tracked files as data or dispatches through the already-public `Daemon` surface (same idiom as `inv002`/`inv006`/`inv016`).\n\nGATES: `cargo fmt --all --check` clean; `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings` clean; targeted (`cargo test -p continuumd --test inv003_no_prose_only_evidence`: 16/16) and full (`cargo test --workspace --locked`: all green) both pass; `just check` exit 0 (fmt, lint, test, crate boundaries, kernel covenant, governance ×5, dossier, Lean build + axiom manifest, all pass). `validation-results.json` untouched; no `.bones/` touched; commit made in-workspace only.\n\nCONCERNS:\n- The anti-drift table (`artifact_classes::SCHEMA_BOUND`) is bidirectionally checked only at the `notes/plan/schemas/` directory grain. It catches a new/stale schema *file*, but not a wholly new machine artifact class introduced elsewhere (e.g. a new `Payload::` variant or a new emitted JSON shape) that names no schema and isn't a typed-wire class — that gap is bounded by `idl_conformance.rs`'s own exhaustive IDL/registry comparison for the wire-envelope side, not by anything added here. Stated as an honest boundary in the file's own header rather than left implicit.\n- `schema_resolution` covers two representative artifact classes (intent-registry-record, evidence-graph-node) rather than all 19 — a \"spot the representative set\" scope per the task's own wording, chosen because they're the two classes this crate's `daemon/*.rs` builds by hand from constants (`RECORD_SCHEMA_ID`/`NODE_SCHEMA_ID`) rather than through a generic emitter; the rest are covered by `schema_closure`'s document-level closure walk plus their own crates' fixture/round-trip tests (not re-audited here).\n- Two Python checkers (`check_code_policy.py`'s GOV-1-12, `check_claim_governance.py`) are cited via freshness tripwires (substring presence of rule names/sentences) rather than re-run from Rust; this is deliberate (\"CITE, do not rebuild\") but means a change to the *behavior* of those rules that keeps the same rule-name strings would not be caught here — only by the Python tools' own self-tests, which `just check`'s `governance` step still runs.\n\nNot closing bn-1eqt per instructions."}	blake3:GcD33EwKY1UqgcU_fYFpb9YbAi1T0jqqM8ytPgkue9g
1785707116494051	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEylgk		item.comment	bn-1eqt	{"body":"Landed @ post-merge epoch (see merge commit). Lead re-verified in-workspace: continuumd suites green incl. the 16 new tests, diff tight (one evidence file + heading + regen), validator implied by just check exit 0.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The GOV-1-12 mirror as a permanent Rust regression WITH its own whitespace-tolerant reader — the canonical-only codec rejecting pretty-printed schema documents is exactly why a second reader is honest here, and it is scoped to the test. (2) The artifact-class table as bidirectionally anti-drift-checked data — a new machine artifact class without a schema now fails loudly, which is the invariant's living half. (3) Real-daemon schema resolution (intent.accept, observe.ingest→evidence.get) — the emitted schema_id/schema_epoch resolving to real documents is the strongest possible binding of wire to schema tree. (4) The freshness tripwires on cited enforcement (INV-016, GOV-1-10/11/12, claim governance) — citation rot now fails a test instead of silently weakening the evidence. (5) Per-leg mutants throughout.\n\nINV-003 satisfied via heading annotation; coverage stays on this bone's label per the heading-category convention."}	blake3:la5qCnelw3o_dHbs4p588yAAOitvZvbbFE4mP-D1fTs
1785707116522528	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEylwk		item.move	bn-1eqt	{"state":"done"}	blake3:RFS25iM8hkgUAp1ZRHbrRsFOxm3B23cA6hUYfUVo3JM
1785707284813255	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymAk		item.comment	bn-jme9	{"body":"INV-005 delivered. Evidence: `crates/continuumd/tests/inv005_ambient_nondeterminism_evidence.rs` (9 tests), plus one new test in `crates/continuum-engine-reference/tests/certificate_kernel_differential.rs`. plan.md heading annotated `(delivered: bn-jme9 — …)`; traceability regenerated; validation-results.json untouched.\n\n## Audit: six ambients -> tier / seam / enforcement\n\n| Ambient | Tier | Seam | Enforcement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| scheduling | boundary (`continuum-asupersync`, Phase B); landed today as data in the core | `continuum_task::region::{RegionTree, schedule::Schedule}` — interleaving is a value, `&mut self`, no thread | `check_crate_boundaries.py` `kernel-is-synchronous` (no async-runtime edge in the trusted checking base, direct or transitive via `continuum-asupersync`) |\n| time | boundary (`continuum-effects-time`, PR-1/IMPL-01 scaffold, not landed) | none landed; prohibition enforced everywhere | GOV-1-04 `ambient-time`; fixtures `gov-1-04-systemtime-in-kernel`, `gov-1-04-clock-dependency` |\n| entropy | boundary (DPOR/symbolic/liveness, Forge — all scaffolds); landed in capability minting | `CapabilityToken::mint` — its own doc: \"entropy is a capability, not ambient (INV-005, ADR-0003)\" | GOV-1-04 `ambient-rng`; fixture `gov-1-04-thread-rng-in-engine` (planted in the engine tier) |\n| I/O | boundary (`continuum-effects-{storage,network}`, scaffolds); landed behind the store | `ReferenceStore` — reached only via `ContentIdentifier`/`AuditSink`/capability tokens | GOV-1-06 `no-network-in-checker` |\n| faults | boundary + daemon | `StorageFaults` (caller-supplied trait, typed `AbortReason`); `daemon::recovery::{CrashPoint, FaultInjector}` | no source-scan rule exists for this; behavioral only |\n| cancellation | semantic core, landed directly | `RegionTree::cancel` + typed `RegionFault`, never a panic | no source-scan rule; behavioral only |\n\nHonest gaps stated, not hidden: entropy/time have no landed *positive capability type* yet (owning crates are PR-1/IMPL-01 scaffolds) — only the prohibition is real, mechanically enforced including inside the engine tier specifically. Faults/cancellation have no source-scan rule at all by design (no forbidden symbol for \"arrived ambiently\"), so they get dedicated behavioral tests instead.\n\n## Clause -> test map\n\n- scheduling: `positive_the_crate_boundary_checker_proves_the_trusted_checking_base_is_synchronous_and_search_free` + `positive_cancellation_and_scheduling_reach_region_calculus_code_as_typed_values_never_a_panic`\n- time/entropy (GOV-1-04): `positive_gov_1_04_self_test_still_catches_exactly_its_five_named_fixtures` + `positive_the_real_run_of_gov_1_03_04_05_and_06_finds_zero_semantic_core_violations`\n- entropy (behavioral): `positive_entropy_reaches_a_capability_token_only_as_a_caller_supplied_identity_never_ambient_randomness`\n- I/O: same real-run test (GOV-1-06)\n- faults: `positive_faults_reach_the_store_only_as_a_caller_supplied_typed_value_not_an_ambient_disk_read`\n- cancellation: the region-calculus test above\n- audit anti-drift: `anti_drift_the_plan_md_heading_still_names_these_six_ambients_in_this_order` + `the_check_is_not_vacuous`\n- artifact freshness: `boundary_the_committed_gov_1_evidence_artifact_still_agrees_with_a_live_self_test_run` (compares committed gov-1.json's GOV-1-04 record against a live run, catching staleness)\n\n## Mutant story\n\n1. GOV-1-04's own fixtures, cited by name, re-run live (subprocess, not a stale JSON read): asserts the self-test still catches exactly `gov-1-04-ambient-dependency`, `gov-1-04-clock-dependency`, `gov-1-04-environment-read-in-core`, `gov-1-04-systemtime-in-kernel`, `gov-1-04-thread-rng-in-engine`.\n2. `the_check_is_not_vacuous` (name/style lifted from `idl_conformance.rs`): mutates a copy of the INV-005 sentence, dropping \"cancellation\", and asserts the anti-drift comparator reports a mismatch (five, not six) rather than passing vacuously; also asserts the shape guard reports `None` for an unrelated sentence.\n\nPlus one new cross-crate regression added to (not duplicating) the existing engine/kernel differential file: `two_independent_pipelines_from_a_declared_model_to_a_checked_certificate_are_byte_identical` — two independent model->explore->emit->check pipelines, sharing no value, byte-identical certificates and equal verdicts. This is the behavioral-level regression GOV-1-04's static scan cannot see. Existing byte-identical evidence cited, not re-run: `bfs_diehard.rs`, `diehard_evidence.rs`, `witness_diehard.rs`, `model_contract.rs` (single-crate), and `pr8_exit_evidence.rs`/`inv006`'s two-fresh-daemons whole-frame byte-identical test (already spans 5 crates).\n\n## Home crate\n\n`continuumd` — same home INV-002/006/016 use, for the same reason: no single crate \"is\" INV-005. continuumd already (normal, not dev-only) depends on continuum-task and continuum-workspace (owning the landed positive seams) and continuum-engine-reference; its own `daemon/recovery.rs` already imports `StorageFaults`/`PublicationPhase` for G1 crash recovery. The governance-re-run half reuses `continuumd::codec::json::Json` (already used the same way by `inv016_untrusted_source_evidence.rs`) rather than adding a JSON dependency — needed a small `compact_json` helper since that codec accepts no insignificant whitespace and Python pretty-prints.\n\n## Counts / gates\n\nNew: evidence file 9 tests; differential file +1. Totals, `cargo test --locked`: continuumd 348 (339+9), continuum-task 146 (unchanged), continuum-workspace 256 (unchanged), continuum-engine-reference 213 (+1). Full `cargo test --workspace --locked`: green, 0 failed anywhere. `cargo fmt --all --check`: clean. `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings`: clean. `just check` (fmt/lint/test/boundaries/covenant/governance/dossier/lean): exit 0. Dossier validator: pass before and after. `git status` post-commit clean; touched files: the two test files, `plan.md` (INV-005 heading only), `PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json` (one status flip), `PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md` (summary counts). No `.bones/`, no `validation-results.json`, no `src/` changes.\n\n## Concerns\n\n- Scheduling's mechanical coverage is narrower than time/RNG's: `kernel-is-synchronous` scopes to the four kernel crates + transitive `continuum-asupersync`, not the full 27-crate semantic core the way GOV-1-04 scans. No workspace crate depends on an async runtime today (checked directly), so not a live gap, but a future semantic-core crate outside the kernel tier picking one up wouldn't be caught mechanically. Flagged, not fixed — extending GOV-1-04 felt like scope creep for a bone told to cite, not rebuild, that checker.\n- Entropy/time have no landed positive capability type (both PR-1/IMPL-01 scaffolds elsewhere); audit states this honestly. Revisit when `continuum-effects-time` and the optimized engines land."}	blake3:hK4eawgowcP9Hlwz4Jh4Ozz6OIEXQW8pxcoEH6wzTYQ
1785707357021710	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymQk		item.comment	bn-jme9	{"body":"Landed @ 3d4823d553d9. Lead re-verified post-sync in-workspace: continuumd 348 + engine-reference suites green, diff tight, regen idempotent.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) Live re-runs of check_code_policy.py and check_crate_boundaries.py from the test rather than reading possibly-stale evidence JSON — the check_t12_evidence.py precedent applied at the Rust layer; the binding cannot go stale. (2) The honest audit rows: entropy and time have PROHIBITION enforcement (GOV-1-04) but no landed positive capability type yet — stating that is the audit's value, not a weakness; the rows record where each capability will land. (3) The three behavioral seam demonstrations (region cancellation/scheduling, StorageFaults, CapabilityToken::mint) as genuinely caller-supplied typed values — the invariant's positive half shown by use. (4) The new cross-crate byte-identity regression (engine→certificate bytes→kernel) closing a real gap: determinism was asserted per-crate but not across the emit-check boundary in one test. (5) Anti-drift mutant with teeth.\n\nCONCERNS RECORDED: scheduling's mechanical scope narrower than time/RNG (GOV-1-04 has no scheduling scan — the calculus's design is the guard); entropy/time positive capability types arrive with the crates that need them (boundary tier). Both are audit rows, not defects.\n\nINV-005 satisfied via heading annotation; coverage stays on this bone's label."}	blake3:mx7x9mo9PsTiJ_IlVKVvDIj66fBjd5OyfYo_6UIt0kc
1785707357050460	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymgk		item.move	bn-jme9	{"state":"done"}	blake3:o96e-CUdMVQPdEWOGXUTS1y-niCYte47YUWdkw8XDEI
1785707382273585	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymwk		item.comment	bn-n9a1	{"body":"INV-008 evidence delivered. Two new test files, zero src/ changes, zero existing tests touched.\n\nFIVE-KIND AUDIT MAP (kind -> InconclusiveReason -> landed where -> test)\n\n- timeout -> ResourceExhausted -> engine (checking::Unresolved::ResourceExhausted) + daemon wire, end to end -> checking_diehard.rs::no_bounded_run_ever_reports_holds; daemon_task_operations.rs::budget_exhaustion_parks_a_continuation...; NEW positive_the_same_target_is_established_unbounded_and_inconclusive_bounded\n- unsupported semantics -> Unsupported -> kernel Verdict::Unsupported (all 4 kernel-* crates); continuum-task's unsupported_empty_task (PR-1 exit); daemon ErrorCode::UnsupportedSemanticFeature -> per-crate check.rs tests + mutation-matrix.toml (framing.wire-epoch-substitution / framing.certificate-family-substitution, \"never Rejected\"); pr_1_exit_unsupported_empty_task.rs; NEW cross-crate sweep\n- insufficient telemetry -> InsufficientTelemetry -> daemon evidence.verify over a redacted reference (daemon/evidence.rs:627) -> daemon_evidence.rs::verifying_over_a_redacted_reference...\n- abstraction ambiguity -> AbstractionAmbiguity -> NOT LANDED. Declared in the vocabulary + on 4 registry rows (program.extract, refinement.check, correspondence.bind, correspondence.drift) via sibling ErrorCode::AmbiguousCorrespondence, but no family::Arguments variant exists for any program.*/refinement.*/correspondence.* op -> decode_arguments refuses all 4 (CodecError::UnknownOperation), same shape as inv006's program.replay finding. Pinned: NEW boundary_abstraction_ambiguity...\n- incomplete proof search -> IncompleteProofSearch -> NOT LANDED. continuum-engine-symbolic and continuum-proof-client are still bare PR-1/IMPL-01 scaffolds (no pub fn/struct/enum; PR 28 hasn't opened). Pinned: NEW boundary_incomplete_proof_search...\n- (6th, unnamed in the sentence, part of the closed vocabulary) EngineError -> engine+wire, end to end -> checking_contract.rs::a_predicate_that_cannot_be_evaluated_is_an_engine_error; inv006's positive_an_engine_evaluation_failure...\n\nKept correctly separate, all cited not reimplemented: BudgetExhausted (ErrorCode, task-level, result-never-verdict, continuation, bn-1gc) != ResourceExhausted (verdict reason) != QuotaExhausted (capability quota) != DimensionOmission (declared-but-unmetered budget dimension, INV-007's cousin, continuum-task/src/budget/dimension.rs).\n\nCLAUSE -> TEST MAP (new work only)\n\ncrates/continuum-kernel-core/tests/inv008_unsupported_vs_rejected_evidence.rs (kernel half):\n- positive_all_four_kernel_crates_declare_the_same_three_arm_verdict_vocabulary_in_order: include_str!'s all 4 crates' verdict.rs, compares Verified/Rejected/Unsupported order (nothing previously compared the 4 crates to each other)\n- positive_is_verified_is_the_only_boolean_projection_in_every_kernel_crate: sweeps for a second boolean (is_rejected/is_unsupported) that would let a caller re-collapse the distinction; pins claim() folding both to None\n- positive_the_covenant_still_names_unsupported_never_rejected_for_the_two_inv008_classes: reads mutation-matrix.toml live, pins the \"never Rejected\" prose\n- negative_the_vocabulary_sweep_is_not_vacuous / negative_the_matrix_prose_pin_is_not_vacuous: mutants (rename/drop a variant; strip the prose) proving both sweeps can fail\n\ncrates/continuumd/tests/inv008_typed_inconclusiveness_evidence.rs (engine/task/daemon half):\n- positive_the_same_target_is_established_unbounded_and_inconclusive_bounded: real Daemon::dispatch against Die Hard's TypeOK (a tautology, true at every state any prefix sees) under a 3-state bound -> inconclusive/ResourceExhausted/Bounded, never established, contrasted with daemon_task_operations.rs's unbounded companion (same target, budget 64, established) -- never-a-pass restated live for the sharpest case. Then independently recomputes via continuum_engine_reference::bfs/checking directly and asserts Unresolved::ResourceExhausted.as_str() == wire InconclusiveReason.as_wire() for the same live value -- closing live the triangle checking.rs's doc says is closed only by citation (engine spelling is a hardcoded string there by design, GOV-1-12 dependency-freedom; wire spelling checked against continuum-value in registry_agreement.rs)\n- positive_no_verdict_value_surface_declares_a_boolean_field / negative_...not_vacuous: sweeps protocol/envelope.rs's 4 *VerdictValue structs + Verdict union for a bool field (none), pins each struct's real field type, mutant proves detection\n- positive_the_verdict_and_reason_vocabularies_are_closed_at_the_counts...: SemanticVerdict::ALL==3, both InconclusiveReason::ALL==6, engine checking::Verdict::ALL==3\n- boundary_abstraction_ambiguity_... / boundary_incomplete_proof_search_...: the two honest gaps, pinned so a future PR landing either producer turns these red\n\nMUTANT STORY: kernel file has 2 mutants on the vocabulary sweep (rename Unsupported->Unknown; drop Rejected) + 1 on the matrix-prose pin. Daemon file has 1 mutant on the boolean-field sweep (fabricated `established: bool required`). Same \"mutant that would violate it and prove the guard detects it\" shape as inv014's negative_* tests.\n\nCOUNTS: 2 new files, 307 + 854 lines, 11 new tests (5 kernel: 3 positive + 2 mutant; 6 daemon: 3 positive + 2 boundary + 1 mutant), all passing. 0 src/ touched, 0 existing tests touched, 0 new dependency edges (include_str! across crates, the inv014 precedent, not Cargo.toml deps).\n\nGATES (all green): fmt --check pass; clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -D warnings pass, zero warnings; cargo test --workspace --locked pass (168 result blocks, 0 failed); check_crate_boundaries.py self-test+run pass; check_kernel_covenant.py self-test+run pass (KCOV-01..09, matrix 0 uncovered); governance 5 checkers self-test+run all pass; validate_dossier.py pass, validation-results.json untouched (confirmed via git diff before/after); lean lake build + axiom-manifest --check pass. Ran `just traceability` (regen): PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json + PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md updated (invariant 14 active/4 satisfied -> 13 active/5 satisfied); re-ran dossier after regen, still pass.\n\nCONCERNS:\n1. Precedent tension on \"(delivered: ...)\". bn-dw81/INV-014 explicitly declined to annotate its heading delivered because one control was a genuine documented deferral (T02/bn-2res precedent: not annotated \"until that successor lands too\"). INV-008 has 2 of 5 kinds similarly unlanded. I annotated delivered anyway per this bone's explicit instruction, reasoning INV-008's sentence is a distinctness claim (\"are distinct outcomes\") not an existence claim like INV-014's (\"...are recorded\") -- the two unlanded kinds already occupy separate, uncollidable enum variants; nothing conflates them because nothing produces them. Annotation states the gap inline. A reviewer weighing bn-dw81's precedent more heavily can strip the tag and re-regen.\n2. The two boundary tests are designed to go red the moment PR 28 or the correspondence/refinement family lands -- revisit then, not silently obsolete them.\n3. No mutation-matrix.toml edits -- the INV-008-relevant rows already existed; this bone added a live pin on their prose only."}	blake3:OIFTjnDHYfmkb_ksNwniaATgC-dwSZ7m8xo-mWbJkl0
1785707487069266	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEynAk		item.comment	bn-n9a1	{"body":"Landed @ 5853ac779609. Lead re-verified post-sync in-workspace: kernel-core + continuumd suites green (11 new tests, 4 mutants), traceability conflict resolved by regen, merge-check clean.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The five+one audit (the sixth unnamed EngineError member included) with the two honest gaps: AbstractionAmbiguity and IncompleteProofSearch declared in the closed vocabulary with NO producer anywhere — pinned as boundary tests, not patched into fake existence. (2) The cross-kernel vocabulary sweep with is_verified as the ONLY boolean projection — the no-bare-boolean clause proven at the type level across all four crates. (3) The live never-a-pass restatement through a real dispatch (bounded vs unbounded, tautology control) binding the engine's spelling to the wire's spelling on one value — the asymmetry is now asserted at BOTH ends of the pipe in one test.\n\nPRECEDENT TENSION ADJUDICATED (the flagged concern): the annotation stands, and the distinction from bn-dw81/INV-014's non-annotation is real, not inconsistent. INV-008's obligation is STRUCTURAL — distinctness and typedness of the outcome vocabulary — which is satisfiable and satisfied today: the vocabulary is closed, no boolean escapes, and every landed inconclusive carries its reason. The two unlanded kinds cannot violate distinctness while they have no producer, and the annotation states the gap inline. INV-014's substance (proof receipts) is itself Phase D — there was nothing structural to satisfy early. Rule of thumb recorded: annotate when the invariant's obligation is fully dischargeable today with gaps that are ABSENCES (stated inline); decline when the obligation's substance has not arrived. INV-008 satisfied via heading annotation."}	blake3:ouMfHcLnG90srFx0O_eZZjeR_preGgFFZiXiFSoA-7I
1785707487109056	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEynQk		item.move	bn-n9a1	{"state":"done"}	blake3:cIYBzr2Y_9DkHYWH9-2ja9Aa65d0ejc4246yvoCmolI
1785709489804340	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyngk		item.comment	bn-23gm	{"body":"PR-10-IMPL-02 delivered — interface bytes, with the counting rule fixed before any number.\n\nDEFINITION. An interface byte is a byte the agent wrote or read *at its own interface*. Native arm: the request frame plus the result frame, taken off the two `Vec<u8>`s the client moved, plus the handshake. Shell arm: the command line it wrote plus the rendered output it read — and **not** the CLI process's own protocol frames, because a real `continuum` process's frames never reach the agent. That last choice is a deliberate handicap on the typed arm and it is named where it is taken (crates/continuum-benchmark/src/shell.rs, 'what this is a baseline of'; src/run.rs IMPL-02).\n\nDENOMINATOR. Interface bytes per **solved** task, per RFC 0027 and plan §24.5's aci-benchmark-margins ('bytes, not tokens, are the graded cost denominator'). An arm cannot win by failing early and cheaply: `bytes_per_solved_task_ignores_a_run_that_did_not_solve_anything` marks one solved run unsolved with a ten-million-byte cost and shows the figure does not move.\n\nTOKENS. Reported because the bullet says 'tokens/bytes' and dropping the word would narrow it. The rule is `ceil(bytes / 4)`, declared as a constant, printed by name beside every count, with no tokenizer, no vocabulary and no model — and nothing is graded on it, which is RFC 0027's own reason for rejecting token-denominated enforcement. RFC 0026's `Cost.tokenizer_id` ('REQUIRED whenever tokens is reported') is why the rule's name travels with the number and never goes on a wire.\n\nSEEDS. bn-2fj's lead note asked this bullet for a seeds knob and per-task per-seed byte reporting. Three declared schedules (no generator), every metric present for each, 8 runs per arm per seed, and a per-operation byte breakdown in the artifact.\n\nMEASURED, over 4 tasks x 3 seeds x 2 policies x 2 arms = 48 runs:\n- native 10,859 interface bytes per solved task; shell 4,118. Margin -163% against a ratified floor of +30%. **The typed surface loses this metric, by 2.6x.**\n- native totals 260,630 bytes over 24 runs; shell 98,836.\n- where it goes (bytes per call): task.status 1,628 vs 535; verification.result 1,910 vs 608; workspace.create 1,471 vs 375; verification.start 1,034 vs 759; workspace.seal 885 vs 540.\n\nWHY, from the breakdown rather than from a guess. A `ResultEnvelope` carries six epochs, nine cost dimensions, an assurance envelope, an omission manifest and a `next_operations` list on *every* answer; the text projection prints the fields the agent asked about. And `workspace.create` is structural rather than incidental: a local CLI names a corpus port and resolves its components from the filesystem, while a remote protocol client must transmit `SnapshotComponents` — ten commitment lists — in full. The per-operation table exists precisely because a redesign is an explicitly valid exit for this lane and a single ratio cannot tell a redesigner where to cut.\n\nThe direction is asserted (`the_typed_surface_spends_more_interface_bytes_than_the_text_baseline`), so a change that flips it fails loudly and this comment has to be re-read. Evidence: crates/continuum-benchmark/tests/pr10_impl02_interface_bytes.rs, 8 tests."}	blake3:ljzgIMknLCZTB_PiBZZp8oihXvGU18WMAw8_KEcdZT0
1785709509138353	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEynwk		item.comment	bn-3awq	{"body":"PR-10-IMPL-03 delivered — task completion, over both corpus ports plan §21's Phase A exit names.\n\nWHAT COMPLETION MEANS. Not 'the script finished'. A run is solved only when the agent read the answer *and it was the dossier's*: settled, verdict read, state count read, ceiling-enforcement known, and equal to `task::Frozen` — whose numbers are the Revision-2 spike's, repeated in four other places, and which neither arm can influence. Two anti-vacuity tests hold the grader to it: a run graded against 17 states is not solved, and a run graded against `established` when the campaign was refuted is not solved.\n\nDINING PHILOSOPHERS. It had no `Model` anywhere in the workspace — TV-007 is a Wave 1 corpus port and PR 8's scope was TV-009 — so rather than declare Die Hard variants sufficient, crates/continuum-benchmark/src/philosophers.rs declares it, the way `continuum-engine-reference::diehard` declares Die Hard: inert data in the engine's own vocabulary, no closures, no new engine. Transcribed from notes/plan/spikes/models.py and cross-read against the corpus .ctm. It is checked against the dossier before any arm runs (tests/philosophers_evidence.rs, 6 tests): **573** reachable states, **2,365** labelled transitions, shortest deadlock depth **10**, and the exact deadlock state spike-results.json recorded — every philosopher in HAS_LEFT with fork f held by philosopher f. Plus the distinction SPIKE_REPORT.md says this corpus entry exists to show: `ForkOwnership` established over all 573 states while the deadlock refutes the campaign.\n\nThe spike's `type_ok` indexes `phase` by a fork owner read out of the state, which this expression language deliberately cannot do (no indexing, no quantifier, no recursion). It is written as the finite conjunction it denotes — one clause per (philosopher, fork) pair, all n^2 decided at declaration time — and folded *balanced* so the result stays inside MAX_EXPR_DEPTH at every N the variable budget admits; `the_model_builds_at_every_size_the_variable_budget_admits` is the test that would fail if the balanced fold were replaced by a plain one. The invariant is shown non-vacuous by a legal state it must reject.\n\nSUBSET. Four tasks, two semantic families: dh-all (all_claims, 16 states, refuted), dh-type-ok (property TypeOK, 16 states, established), dp-all (all_claims, 573 states, refuted by deadlock), dp-ownership (property ForkOwnership, 573 states, refuted). The last records a real asymmetry rather than working around it: `verification.start` maps every target kind onto `DeadlockPolicy::Defect` and a refutation dominates the fold, so the invariant holds and the campaign is refuted anyway.\n\nMEASURED. Every faithful cell solved on both arms — 24/24 native, 24/24 shell, 1000 permille each — and the two arms read the same state count and the same verdict in every one of the 48 cells. That is plan §21's Phase A exit sentence, 'checked through native API, CLI, and an agent client with identical artifacts', at this harness's grain.\n\nHONESTY BOUNDARY. Two of the four frozen numbers have no wire home and are corroborated one layer down with the boundary named at the line it is crossed, exactly as continuumd's PR-8 exit evidence does: RFC 0026 F16 gives `Cost` no transition dimension, and nothing in this workspace builds the crashpack a witness would ride in. So 96/2,365 transitions and depth 6 are read from `Daemon::state`, and the test says so where it reads them.\n\nEvidence: crates/continuum-benchmark/tests/pr10_impl03_task_completion.rs (7 tests), tests/philosophers_evidence.rs (6 tests)."}	blake3:JVPv0Ux0O6LNEV4eoYDnRK7r3CusZDinD5VLtJp9qDQ
1785709530480538	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyoAk		item.comment	bn-1udt	{"body":"PR-10-IMPL-04 delivered — recovery from errors, injected at the daemon's own error surfaces.\n\nINJECTION POINTS. Nothing is mocked and no response is patched. The fallible policy makes four ordinary agent mistakes, each landing on a surface continuumd already has:\n- wrong-principal: present a `read` capability for an `execute` operation -> `CapabilityDenied`, checked before any semantic work. The principal table is least-authority (workspace.* propose, verification.*/task.resume/task.cancel execute, task.status read, read off continuumd::protocol::registry), so this is a real mistake rather than a contrived one against an over-provisioned agent.\n- unsealed-start: verify a snapshot that was never sealed -> `StaleSnapshot`.\n- tight-budget: declare a 4-state ceiling -> an **admitted** suspension carrying a continuation. Not an error, and here on purpose: RFC 0026 and docs/49 make budget exhaustion resumable, so an instrument that only injected refusals would miss the recovery path the protocol is proudest of.\n- unregistered-model: edit the .ctm, then ask for a verdict -> `UnsupportedSemanticFeature`, because a changed model is a different model.\n\nRECOVERY. The last one's recovery took the daemon's semantics seriously rather than working around them. A fork advances the snapshot's lineage, so neither the pre-fork handle nor a re-create is usable — both answer `StaleSnapshot`. What works is what a person would do: put the file back, with a second fork carrying the corpus bytes, after which the model resolves again because a model is keyed by its source bytes. That is `Call::RestoreModule`, and its doc comment records why the two obvious recoveries do not work.\n\nMEASURED. All 12 faulted cells on **both** arms reach the frozen answer: recovery 1000 permille each. Recovery is measured with its cost — every fallible run takes more attempts and more bytes than the faithful run of the same (task, seed, arm) cell, asserted for all three seeds on both arms. `a_tight_budget_parks_a_continuation_and_the_resume_closes_the_campaign` shows the campaign was *resumed* and not restarted: one `verification.start`, one `task.resume`, and the resumed run closes on Die Hard's frozen 16 rather than on the 4 it was capped at.\n\nWHERE THE INTERFACES ACTUALLY DIFFER. The unsealed start. The typed client's register refuses it before the wire, so the typed arm never sees `StaleSnapshot` at all (zero bytes, no idempotency key spent, nothing audited) while the text arm spends a round trip to learn the snapshot is not sealed. The codes each arm sees are asserted as lists: native [CapabilityDenied], shell [StaleSnapshot, CapabilityDenied].\n\nTWO LIMITS, RECORDED RATHER THAN CLAIMED AROUND.\n1. `Error.recovery` is RFC 0026's only recovery channel — 'a list of allowed operations with pre-filled arguments, never free-form commands' — and **this daemon populates none** (continuumd::daemon::result builds every failure with `recovery: Vec::new()`). So the typed arm's advantage on this bullet is the closed error *code*, not a next step handed to it, and a test asserts the list is empty so the annotation cannot outrun the surface. If PR 10's ACI claim is ever to rest on prefilled recovery, that producer has to be built first.\n2. `recovered` is defined as faulted **and solved**. Surviving an error without reaching the dossier's answer is not a recovery, and `a_run_that_cannot_recover_is_reported_as_unrecovered` shows the metric saying no.\n\nAlso asserted: both arms classify a refusal through the protocol's own closed vocabulary — the projection prints `error.code: CapabilityDenied`, the wire token, never a synonym — and the scrape is anchored on a line prefix, so `field(text, \"code\")` does not match the `error.code` line.\n\nEvidence: crates/continuum-benchmark/tests/pr10_impl04_error_recovery.rs (7 tests); crates/continuum-benchmark/src/policy.rs (Fault, Schedule, the perturbation and the recovery ladder)."}	blake3:rMFcQWHG_fhOActIng8kJ3BkRs7icfQdQt4c0YVXnGQ
1785709549709357	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyoQk		item.comment	bn-26tb	{"body":"PR-10-IMPL-05 delivered — deterministic reproduction, on a two-fresh-runs device.\n\nTHE DEVICE. Every claim is made by running a scenario twice against two **independently built** rigs and comparing bytes. Nothing is shared between the two runs but the script: two daemons, two capability tables, two staged corpora, two connections, two clients. So an agreement cannot be explained by a cache, a surviving counter, a map's internal layout or an address — the failure modes a same-daemon replay would miss. It is the same device continuumd's PR-8 exit evidence uses, for the same stated reason.\n\nMEASURED. Same cell, both arms, both policies, all three seeds, both corpus ports: identical transcripts, identical metrics, identical per-operation breakdowns. Two full 48-run sweeps render byte-identical report artifacts.\n\nWHERE THE DETERMINISM COMES FROM — four sources, each checked rather than assumed (INV-005):\n- no clock: rig::NOW is a constant; two fresh rigs negotiate byte-identical hello and welcome frames, mint the same governing intent identity, and name the staged corpus identically for every task.\n- no entropy: request identifiers and idempotency keys are minted from a monotone call counter on the typed arm and from an FNV-1a digest of the command line on the text arm — a tracing identifier, which is what RFC 0026 says a request id is, and deliberately not the workspace's cryptographic identity seam.\n- no ambient schedule: a 'seed' selects one of three **declared** Schedules rather than seeding a generator, and the three are asserted to differ in something that changes a run.\n- nothing machine-specific in the artifact: no float, no map order, no filesystem path, no address, ASCII only. `report::Report::render` is documented to that effect and the test holds it to it.\n\nANTI-VACUITY. A byte comparison that could not distinguish two different runs would prove nothing. Dropping a seed changes the artifact; dropping the fallible policy changes the artifact; appending one character to one transcript line changes the transcript.\n\nTHE ARTIFACT. `report::Report` is a typed value with a total rendering function, not a printout — rows sorted by (task, seed, policy, arm), rates as integer per-mille, ratios as a/b, every section derived. It carries the runs, the per-arm totals, the plan §24.5 margins, the per-operation byte breakdown, the counting rules, and a `limits` section that says inside the artifact what the artifact is not.\n\nTHE §19.4 GATE IS INSIDE THE CONSTRUCTOR. `Report::over` refuses to mark a report `accepted` unless `separation::check` passes over the subset the runs were computed on. That is the goal bone's criterion 2 held *before* a number is quotable rather than beside it: family isolation and source-hash isolation as two distinct conditions (`a_source_hash_that_straddles_two_partitions_is_refused_even_when_families_do_not` builds the case that passes the first and fails the second), an empty side refused as vacuous rather than passed, and the source hash computed by the daemon's own `model_source` so 'the same source' means one thing in this crate and in continuumd.\n\nOn the lead note attached to this bone: the seeds knob and per-task per-seed byte and invalid-action reporting are in (see bn-23gm's comment for the numbers). What is *not* here is the one-sided 95% lower bound on the success margin — with a deterministic instrument the per-seed variance is exactly zero, so a confidence interval over three identical values would be a statistic wearing a bound's clothes. The margin arithmetic is computed and printed; grading it, and deciding whether a scripted-policy instrument can carry that sentence at all, is bn-762i's and bn-2c0a's.\n\nEvidence: crates/continuum-benchmark/tests/pr10_impl05_reproduction.rs (7 tests), tests/pr10_family_separation.rs (6 tests)."}	blake3:wAFhN0Zm88N1Ugpu8L8or5HfYmLV29fYCToM4Rpk-GM
1785709605728996	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyogk		item.comment	bn-134i	{"body":"PR-10-IMPL-01 delivered — valid operation rate, and the two surfaces it compares.\n\nDEFINITION. `admitted / attempted`, per arm, per (task, seed, policy) cell. An attempt is one operation the shared policy decided to make; an admission is a result envelope whose status is not `error` — ok, task_started and task_suspended are all admissions, because a suspended task is a budget outcome carrying a continuation and not a rejected request (docs/49, RFC 0026). Two anti-cheating rules, stated in src/run.rs and asserted:\n1. an operation the client refuses **locally** counts as an attempt and as an invalid one. The saving is bytes, not validity, and `zero_cost_invalid` is reported beside the rate rather than laundered into it;\n2. both arms are driven by one shared `Policy`, so the number of mistakes is fixed by construction and only the interface varies. `both_arms_attempt_the_identical_operation_sequence` asserts the same operations in the same order, cell for cell.\n\nMEASURED (48 runs): the faithful policy attains 1000 permille on both arms — the control, since a correct script producing invalid operations would mean the metric was measuring the harness. Both arms 172 attempts, 160 admitted, 12 invalid, 69 permille invalid-action rate. **A tie.** Native `zero_cost_invalid` = 4, shell = 0. The metric can say no: the seed-2 fallible cell falls below 1000 on both arms with exactly its two mistakes, and the seed-1 tight-budget cell stays at 1000 because a suspension is an admission.\n\nTHE HONEST FINDING. On this instrument the typed surface does **not** reduce the invalid-action rate — the ratified 50% relative reduction (plan §24.5, quote-id aci-benchmark-margins) is not met, and `the_two_arms_valid_operation_rates_are_equal_on_this_instrument` records that as a measured fact so a change is loud. A disciplined scraper against a well-formed text projection classifies exactly what the typed client classifies, because the projection prints the protocol's own closed-vocabulary tokens. What the typed surface changes is the *cost* of a mistake: four of the twelve invalid attempts never reached a daemon, spent zero interface bytes, spent no idempotency key and produced no audit record. A 50% reduction in *rate* would need an agent that plans against the register before acting — research/25's 'validate a plan before calls' — which a scripted policy cannot demonstrate.\n\nEvidence: crates/continuum-benchmark/tests/pr10_impl01_valid_operation_rate.rs (8 tests); crates/continuum-mcp/tests/typed_surface.rs (10 tests)."}	blake3:69cfm2QhdWw1gliEVoE3YBW0uVISNHWHAYE74yYOUvk
1785709637820927	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyowk		item.comment	bn-134i	{"body":"ARCHITECTURE REPORT — PR 10 as a whole (bn-134i, bn-23gm, bn-3awq, bn-1udt, bn-26tb).\n\nCRATES. Client: `continuum-mcp`, the one plan §20 crate naming an agent-facing surface — plan §21's Phase A exit lists 'native API, CLI, and an agent client', and plan §24.5's ACI row names 'MCP-only surface' as this lane's fallback. `continuum-proof-client` is the Lean proof-service client (plan §15, PR 28) and would have made its own module doc untrue; `continuumd` owns the protocol and a client inside the server has no byte boundary to prove anything across. Harness: `continuum-benchmark` (plan §19). No crate invented; boundary self-test + check pass.\n\nThe harness does **not** take a normal dependency on the client: adapters are sinks in the workspace graph (plan §20, RULE adapters-are-sinks), so the native arm is wired in tests/support/mod.rs as a dev-dependency — the edge kind that checker reports rather than enforces, by its own documented design. The cost is one match per test binary; every scoring rule (attempt counting, admission classification, byte accounting, view folding, completion, transcript) is in the library and applied identically to both arms. src/surface.rs states that trade where a reader will meet it.\n\nCLIENT vs research/25. The note asks for semantic state rather than editor state, and for an ACI evaluable as `AgentState x ToolOperation -> AgentState x Observation`. `register::OPERATIONS` is that transition table as shipped data: eight rows, each an operation and the handle it requires. Three encodings are load-bearing. (a) The state is the **caller's** — plan §20 forbids an adapter owning semantic state, so `AgentContext` is passed per call and the client holds no snapshot, task or continuation; two subagents share one workspace with no session coupling (plan §10.5). (b) Preconditions are *necessary* conditions only, because research/25's kill list includes 'task grammar constrains legitimate strategies'; `workspace.create` is unconditioned, and `the_register_never_refuses_an_operation_the_daemon_then_admits` sends the one refused call and shows the daemon refuses it too — the register predicts, it does not restrict. (c) The transition is read off the typed answer, never guessed. A precondition failure is a third outcome arm (`Outcome::Unmet`), not a refusal in disguise. Not built: the MCP transport itself (JSON-RPC framing, tool manifest, resource mapping) — PR 27's, and inventing it would have shipped a second protocol beside RFC 0026.\n\nBASELINE-HONESTY STATEMENT. PR 13's CLI does not exist, so per the goal bone's note the baseline drives the **same daemon over the same wire** through a text-rendering adapter in the harness. Since the rendering is ours, the module states a mechanical projection contract: no invented content (every line projects a field of the envelope or payload), the protocol's own tokens rather than synonyms, handles in full with no truncation, every scalar present or explicitly `none` (so 'no continuation' and 'this answer does not say' are as distinguishable in text as on the wire — the most generous rule in the contract, and deliberate), and lists summarized with an expansion command. Four disciplines: anchored key lookup, closed-vocabulary parsing through `ProtocolEnum::from_wire`, explicit expansion, bounded re-read (fired on purpose by an injected flaky renderer so it is not a decoration). The baseline is **not** charged for the CLI process's own protocol frames, nor for the per-invocation handshakes a real CLI would perform — a deliberate handicap on the typed arm, taken knowingly and named where taken. One rule is genuinely lossy and it is the one that matters: the INV-007 omission manifest is inline on every typed answer and summarized in text, and the agent needs it to know whether its declared ceiling was metered, so the text arm pays an expansion command for what the typed arm already holds. Neither arm is charged for information the policy does not use. For the record: a shell baseline reading a CLI's `--json` would be the typed surface wearing a shell's clothes — INV-003 makes `--json` the contract and prose its projection — so the only meaningful baseline is the prose projection, which is what this is.\n\nDETERMINISM ARGUMENT. No clock (one constant timestamp), no entropy (counter- and digest-derived request ids), no thread, no ambient schedule (seeds select declared Schedules), no float/path/address/locale in the artifact. Verified rather than asserted: two independently built rigs produce byte-identical handshake frames, byte-identical transcripts and byte-identical 48-run reports, and the comparison is shown able to fail.\n\n§19.4 SEPARATION CHECK. Family isolation and source-hash isolation as two **distinct** conditions (the test that passes the first and fails the second is built explicitly), a vacuous empty-side split refused, and the source hash computed by the daemon's own `model_source` so 'the same source' means one thing here and in continuumd. `Report::over` will not mark a report `accepted` without it — the gate is inside the constructor, which is the G0 staging rule held before a number is quotable rather than beside it.\n\nHEADLINE MEASUREMENTS (48 runs; the harness reports, it does not decide). Success 1000 permille both arms, margin 0 against a +10pp floor. Invalid-action rate 69 permille both arms, reduction 0% against a 50% floor. Interface bytes per solved task: native 10,859, shell 4,118 — a -163% margin against a +30% floor. Recovery 1000 permille both arms. **None of the three ratified margins clears.**\n\nWHAT A REAL AGENT RUN WOULD NEED BEYOND THE SCRIPTED POLICIES (bn-2c0a's business). (1) A live model on an identical base model, task set and per-task budget — that is where the ratified pairing and the one-sided 95% lower bound become meaningful, since a deterministic instrument has exactly zero per-seed variance and a confidence interval over three identical values would be theatre. (2) Plan validation against the register before acting, the only mechanism by which the typed surface could plausibly halve the invalid-action rate, and something no scripted policy can exhibit. (3) A tokenizer, if the token number is ever to mean anything; RFC 0027 says not to grade it. (4) A larger held-out subset — this is four tasks over two families; full leakage validation is DX-15 at G9. (5) The `Error.recovery` producer: the typed recovery channel exists on the wire and is empty in this daemon.\n\nGATES. fmt --check clean; clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -D warnings clean; cargo test --workspace --locked 1904 passed / 0 failed (baseline 1845; continuumd 339, continuum-task 146, continuum-workspace 256 all unchanged; +10 continuum-mcp, +49 continuum-benchmark); `just check` exit 0 including boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier and Lean. validation-results.json untouched; no .bones/ change in any commit. Commits in workspace bn-134i, not merged: cf10147 (client), 522695e (harness), eb41912 (annotations + regen)."}	blake3:x1b8COOSFqXSGuq3PnSCokM-q1tFEETxitN3aStqS0U
1785709755585794	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEypAk		item.comment	bn-134i	{"body":"Landed @ 9b6872bd4a42. Lead re-verified post-sync in-workspace: full workspace 1904 tests green (+10 continuum-mcp, +49 continuum-benchmark), boundaries/covenant/governance/dossier/lean all exit 0, dependency-rationale extension confirmed GOV-1-07-required not tolerance-weakening, dev-dep continuum-mcp edge visible in the boundary report per adapters-are-sinks.\n\nRATIFIED — the instrument, and the discipline that makes its numbers mean something. (1) One shared Policy driving both arms so mistake count is fixed by construction; refused-locally counts as invalid (no laundering). (2) The counting rule fixed BEFORE any number; bytes-per-solved-task denominator per RFC 0027; the declared ceil(bytes/4) token rule named beside every figure. (3) The baseline-honesty contract: mechanical projection (no invented content, wire tokens, full handles), four disciplines, the flaky-renderer control proving bounded re-read fires, the typed arm HANDICAPPED (baseline not charged for CLI frames), and the --json-would-be-INV-003 observation. (4) Philosophers declared-not-declined with dossier corroboration (573/2365/depth-10, the spike's exact deadlock state). (5) The two honest declines: Error.recovery unpopulated so the typed advantage is the closed code not a prefilled step, asserted so the annotation cannot outrun the surface; and refusing a confidence interval over zero-variance values — a statistic wearing a bound's clothes, exactly right.\n\nTHE RESULT, stated for the record: valid-op rate TIE at 69‰ both arms (ratified 50% relative reduction NOT met; zero_cost_invalid 4/12 reported beside); bytes native 10,859 vs shell 4,118 per solved task, −163% against a +30% floor (envelope: six epochs, nine cost dimensions, assurance, omission manifest, next_operations on every answer; SnapshotComponents on the wire where a CLI reads disk); completion 24/24 both arms. NONE of the three ratified margins clears. This is a REDESIGN-shaped input to bn-2c0a/bn-762i, not a failure of the instrument — the goal bone's own criterion names redesign as a valid exit. The per-operation breakdown that makes a redesign actionable (task.status 1628-vs-535, verification.result 1910-vs-608, workspace.create 1471-vs-375) is the deliverable here.\n\nScope honestly bounded in the artifact's limits section: 4 tasks, 2 families, scripted policies. Adjudication of what the numbers MEAN belongs to bn-2c0a (falsification of the instrument itself) and bn-762i (exit) — and the redesign decision, if it comes, is privileged and goes to the user."}	blake3:xCM1Y7Fsuj0wfumPQ7mpxuOzn0XkZ7jrEg5yZzxgbTE
