1785717097128221	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1Ak		item.comment	bn-1sdp	{"body":"PR-12 / IMPL-05 — observer event change classification. Delivered in `.maw/workspaces/bn-1sdp/`, commit `77de16c`. Not merged, not closed.\n\n## Design\n\nNew module `crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/observers.rs` — first real code in this crate (previously a PR-1 doc-only scaffold). Classifies RFC 0031's `observers` field over `continuum-intent`'s `Observer`/`ObserverSet` (PR-4/IMPL-03):\n\n- `classify_observers(before, after) -> Vec<ObserverChange>` — total: one record per unit key on either side, including `Unchanged`. Unit on `after` only → `Added`; on `before` only → `Removed`; on both → `classify_pair`.\n- `classify_pair` — componentwise over the four sets (`events`, `state_projection`, `knowledge_projection`, `security_projection`): tracks any-grew / any-shrank, then neither→`Unchanged`, grew-only→`Refined`, shrank-only→`Coarsened`, both→`Incomparable`. A `debug_assert_eq!` pins that `Unchanged` agrees with canonical-encoding byte equality (R2) on every call.\n- `ObserverChange` carries the `ObserverId` locator plus `continuum_intent::change_policy::Relation` (reused, not reinvented), with `to_classification_record()` for `PolicyTable::verdict`.\n\nOne RFC-reading call worth flagging: \"MUST classify `coarsened` even when other components grow — the mixed case blocks under the fail-closed rule regardless\" reads, taken literally as an amendment, as contradicting the componentwise rule stated one sentence earlier (growth anywhere rules out \"all four subsets\"). I read it as two sentences doing two jobs (plain case restated, then a concession the mixed case is handled elsewhere), and implemented the literal componentwise rule with no override — also the safer reading, since `Incomparable` is non-affirmative and blocks under every verb via P2 (including `unlocked`), while a bare `Coarsened` only blocks under a verb that names it, which no closed verb does for `observers`. Full reasoning in the module doc; flagging here for review rather than burying it in a comment.\n\n`unknown`/`unsupported` are never emitted: observer comparison is decidable outright (plain set inclusion, no solver), and `observers[]` carries no `fragment` member in the live schema — pinned by a test reading `intent-contract.schema.json` directly.\n\n## Clause → test map\n\n- refined / coarsened / unchanged / incomparable (the four componentwise outcomes) — `strictly_growing_every_component_classifies_refined`, `growing_one_component_and_holding_the_rest_still_classifies_refined`, `dropping_an_event_family_alone_classifies_coarsened`, `dropping_a_projection_element_alone_is_as_visible_as_dropping_an_event`, `shrinking_every_component_classifies_coarsened`, `equal_observers_classify_unchanged`, `one_component_shrinking_while_another_grows_is_incomparable`, `negative_a_same_size_event_swap_is_incomparable_not_unchanged`.\n- added/removed membership — `a_unit_present_only_after_classifies_added`, `a_unit_present_only_before_classifies_removed`.\n- R2 (unchanged only on canonical-encoding equality) — `debug_assert_eq!` in `classify_pair`, exercised by the whole suite.\n- \"hide observer events\" (plan §19.5) / \"coarsen observer\" (docs/50) — `positive_the_named_attack_on_a_real_corpus_fixture_classifies_coarsened`, `the_named_attack_is_reviewed_not_allowed_under_the_fixtures_own_policy`.\n- fail-closed rule / P2 (blocks under every verb incl. `unlocked`) — `incomparable_blocks_even_under_the_least_restrictive_verb`.\n- no `fragment` on `observers[]` → never `unsupported` — `the_live_schema_carries_no_fragment_member_on_observers_items`.\n\n## The hiding-is-never-benign argument\n\nINV-001/INV-011 make a coarsened observer a privileged weakening exactly like a weakened property — a claim's *verified* statement can weaken with its text untouched, invisible to a property-only diff. Three layers of evidence:\n\n1. **Direct attack, real fixture.** `positive_the_named_attack_on_a_real_corpus_fixture_classifies_coarsened` drops the one event the replicated-register corpus's `client` observer declares; the companion test carries that record into `PolicyTable::verdict` (already-landed PR-4 machinery) under the fixture's own `policy.observers = \"review\"`, asserting `Review`, never `Allow`.\n2. **Disguise sweep.** `property_no_attempted_disguise_of_a_dropped_element_ever_reads_as_unchanged_refined_or_added` runs seven disguises (drop-and-nothing, drop-and-decoy, drop-while-growing-elsewhere, rename, coarsen-then-rename, outright deletion...) and asserts the touched unit is always `Coarsened`/`Incomparable`/`Removed`, never `Unchanged`/`Refined`, never silently vanishing. Unit tests add the rename case directly (parallel to RFC 0031's claims-rename rule).\n3. **Anti-vacuity mutants.** Two deliberately wrong classifiers — `mutant_blind_to_shrinkage` (cardinality trend only, wrongly calls a drop-and-decoy `Refined`) and `mutant_by_total_cardinality` (summed sizes, wrongly calls a same-weight swap `Unchanged`) — run against fixtures the real classifier passes correctly, and the tests assert disagreement: proof the positive assertions are load-bearing, not tautological.\n\n## Counts and gates\n\n- New tests: 18 unit + 11 evidence = 29, all passing.\n- Full workspace `cargo test --workspace --locked`: 2,103 passed (2,074 baseline + 29 new), 0 failed.\n- `just check` (fmt-check, clippy --workspace --all-targets -D warnings, test, boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier, Lean): exit 0, zero violations in the log.\n- `validation-results.json`: untouched (confirmed via `git status`).\n\n## Files / commits\n\n- `crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/observers.rs` (new) — classifier + 18 unit tests.\n- `crates/continuum-semantic-diff/tests/pr12_impl05_observer_event_change_evidence.rs` (new) — 11 evidence tests.\n- `crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/lib.rs`, `Cargo.toml` — module wiring, honest \"what has landed\" doc, one new downward edge on `continuum-intent`.\n- `Cargo.lock` — the one new edge, nothing else moved.\n- `tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml` — added `continuum-semantic-diff` to `continuum-intent`'s `used_by`/rationale (GOV-1-07 was failing without this).\n- `notes/plan/notes/START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md` — `(delivered: bn-1sdp — ...)` on the \"observer event change\" bullet.\n- `PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md`, `PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json` — regenerated; PR-12-IMPL-05 flips `active` → `satisfied`.\n- Commit `77de16c` in the `bn-1sdp` workspace.\n\n## Concerns / honest scope\n\n- Library-level classifier only: no wire `intent_changes[]`/`diff_*` artifact assembly, no impact set — later bone's job.\n- P1 field-level completeness (a record for `observers` even when zero units changed) is the assembler's job, not this module's — stated in the module doc.\n- The RFC-reading call above is my resolved reading, not a raised RFC flag needing a revision — surfacing it for review rather than deciding unilaterally and staying silent.\n- The other five PR-12 bullets (`bn-b8ru`, `bn-8mlg`, `bn-7vg7`, `bn-ycn6`, `bn-3vxp`) are untouched, still open."}	blake3:gQsDdofmHdZdyee87dbBo-95fr6YThyf4TJbWexFjWk
1785717156842270	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1Qk		item.comment	bn-1sdp	{"body":"Landed @ 471a194bd96e. Lead re-verified: 29 new tests green (18 unit + 11 evidence), workspace 2,103/0, diff tight, GOV-1-07 edge recorded.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) Reusing continuum_intent::change_policy::Relation rather than minting a second classification vocabulary — one lattice, two consumers, no drift surface. (2) The R2 debug_assert pin on every comparison (canonical-encoding equality, never textual). (3) The real-corpus attack case closed into the already-landed PolicyTable::verdict — the diff and the policy now demonstrably compose on a genuine contract. (4) The 7-way disguise sweep as the hiding-is-never-benign adversarial half. (5) THE RFC 0031 READING: the observers section's 'MUST classify coarsened even when other components grow' read as two sentences doing two jobs rather than a special case, implementing the literal componentwise rule — accepted as the working reading BECAUSE it is the safer one (Incomparable blocks unconditionally; a bare Coarsened does not under an unlocked verb) and it is documented at the module. The RFC text itself still needs the correction — routed to bn-13nlo (comment following), which already owns RFC 0031 text repair.\n\nPR-12 IMPL-05 satisfied via bullet annotation."}	blake3:Iqjt_3qH1tuI1aZND2kqnRfaIA5JMcnkh_LiJ2HeREQ
1785717156882053	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1gk		item.move	bn-1sdp	{"state":"done"}	blake3:vlJ-jEcr5bnQGkh4opcuSS26NFJmURTqiwhSI62eDfg
1785717156912845	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1wk		item.comment	bn-13nlo	{"body":"Scope widening from bn-1sdp's landing (lead): RFC 0031's observers section contains an internally contradictory sentence — 'MUST classify coarsened even when other components grow' read literally contradicts the componentwise rule one sentence earlier. bn-1sdp implemented the literal componentwise rule (the safer reading: Incomparable blocks unconditionally, bare Coarsened does not under an unlocked verb) and documented the resolution in crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/observers.rs's module doc. When this bone repairs RFC 0031's example story, ALSO correct that sentence to state the componentwise rule unambiguously (or explicitly bless the two-jobs reading), citing bn-1sdp — the implementation and the spec text must stop disagreeing on the page."}	blake3:8BbpVc1U2227nCkhJM7AW39GCRuYucAV33RUz_Io2Qs
1785717511507758	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2Ak		item.comment	bn-16ek	{"body":"bn-16ek — PR-11 / IMPL-03 (source/model references): implementation report.\n\nDESIGN\nLanded crates/continuum-context/src/{selection,source,model}.rs — the first code in this crate (was a PR-1/IMPL-01 doc-only scaffold; no other IMPL-0X bullet in this crate had landed at claim time). RFC 0028's field-to-schema table maps \"relevant source spans and model actions\" onto selected[].kind in {source, model}.\n\n- selection.rs: SelectionKind, the schema's full closed 11-member kind enum (all 11, not just the 2 this bullet owns — same reasoning continuum-evidence's EdgeRelation gave for shipping all 13 edge kinds against a 7-word bullet: a partial closed enum can't round-trip a conforming instance), and SelectedItem, the shared {id, kind, summary, artifact} wire shape. SelectedItem::new is pub(crate); only SourceRef/ModelActionRef can build one.\n- source.rs: SourceSpan (mirrors the IDL's SourceSpan struct; file strengthened to continuum_workspace::snapshot::WorkspacePath instead of the wire's bare String) and SourceRef. artifact is always null (no src_* class in plan §4.4).\n- model.rs: ModelActionRef — a continuum_value::value::Name action name plus an optional model_* handle, class-checked against ArtifactClass::ElaboratedModel at construction (with_model refuses every other class).\n\nINV-016 is enforced structurally: the only two SelectedItem constructors take no string parameter, so summary can only come from the reference's own typed fields. A compile_fail doctest pins SelectedItem::new unreachable outside the crate.\n\nCanonical encoding reuses continuum_intent::canonical_json::Json (RFC 0037 ID5's discipline, already cross-crate-used by continuum-benchmark), not continuum_value::value::Value's CVNF-1: context-pack.schema.json is JSON-schema-normative exactly as intent-contract.schema.json is; CVNF-1 is the unrelated exact-finite-value domain's encoding and would have been a second, wrong-artifact-class writer.\n\nCLAIMED: typed non-stringly SourceRef/ModelActionRef; full closed SelectionKind vocabulary, fail-closed parsing; structural PartialEq/Ord on both refs and SelectedItem (satisfies rule ordering.deterministic once a future pack assembly sorts selected[]); canonical-JSON projection to the schema's exact item shape; INV-016 enforced at the type/signature level.\n\nDECLINED (honest scope):\n- Typed construction for the other 9 SelectionKind members — IMPL-01/02/04/05/06's scope.\n- Per-item content identity (ADR-0013) — schema gives no selected[] item its own content_hash, only the whole pack does; pack assembly is no single IMPL bullet's fence. Structural PartialEq/Ord stands in.\n- Expansion-handle integration (ExpansionRelation, expansions[], omission manifest) — IMPL-04's (bn-28jj), unopened at landing. source_span is one of the IDL's 11 ExpansionRelation members, exactly what a source item would anchor; SelectedItem::id is shaped as that future anchor, nothing more claimed.\n- Minting a real model_* handle — continuum-cml-elab (elaboration) is an unopened PR-1 scaffold; with_model accepts a real handle when supplied but this bullet cannot produce one.\n- Whole-pack assembly / context_id / top-level content_hash — no IMPL-0X bullet owns this seam alone; not claimed.\n\nCLAUSE -> TEST (condensed; full tables in each module's doc comment):\n11 kinds match schema order -> the_kind_set_is_exactly_the_schema_enum (+ integration test); fail-closed on bad token -> an_unknown_kind_token_does_not_parse (+ anti-vacuity: valid tokens still parse); artifact optional/nullable, always JSON null when absent -> artifact_is_present_as_json_null_when_absent; 4 keys, ID5-sorted -> canonical_json_has_exactly_the_schema_keys_in_sorted_order; SelectedItem::new unreachable externally -> compile_fail doctest; span validation (1-based, end>=start, point spans ok) -> line_or_column_zero_is_refused, an_end_before_start_is_refused + anti-vacuity accept cases; source item kind/artifact/summary -> a_source_ref_projects_to_the_source_kind, a_source_ref_never_carries_an_artifact, summary_is_exactly_the_spans_display; model item kind/artifact/class-check -> artifact_reflects_the_model_handle, action_only_has_no_artifact, with_model_refuses_the_wrong_artifact_class + anti-vacuity accept case; determinism -> identical_references_are_byte_identical_across_independent_builds, distinct_references_have_distinct_canonical_encodings, items_sort_into_one_stable_content_derived_order; JSON round-trip -> every_emitted_item_round_trips_through_the_canonical_json_reader.\n\nCOUNTS: 30 new tests — 22 unit (selection.rs 6, source.rs 9, model.rs 7) + 7 integration (tests/pr11_impl03_source_model_references.rs) + 1 compile_fail doctest. cargo test -p continuum-context: 30/30 pass. cargo test --workspace --locked (post-rebase, see CONCERNS): 2133 total passing, 0 failed.\n\nFILES: crates/continuum-context/{Cargo.toml, src/lib.rs (updated), src/selection.rs, src/source.rs, src/model.rs, tests/pr11_impl03_source_model_references.rs} (new except Cargo.toml/lib.rs updated); tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml (used_by + rationale for continuum-value/continuum-workspace/continuum-intent now include continuum-context — GOV-1-07 required this, caught by inv005_ambient_nondeterminism_evidence); notes/plan/notes/START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md (PR-11 IMPL-03 bullet annotated); PLAN_BONE_TRACEABILITY.md, PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json (regenerated); Cargo.lock (path-dep edges only).\n\nCOMMITS (workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-16ek, detached HEAD, not merged/pushed):\n- a287486 \"feat: Context Pack source/model references (bn-16ek)\"\n- 251c771 \"fix: resolve rebase conflict in dependency-rationale.toml (bn-16ek)\"\n\nGATES: cargo fmt --all --check pass; cargo clippy -p continuum-context --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings pass (workspace-wide clippy also pass via just check); cargo test -p continuum-context --locked 30/30 pass; check_crate_boundaries.py --self-test and plain run pass, 0 violations; just check (fmt-check, lint, test, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier, lean) exit 0; generate_traceability.py run (twice, see below); validate_dossier.py exit 0; validation-results.json untouched (confirmed via git status throughout).\n\nCONCERNS:\n- Mid-task, this workspace was automatically rebased onto a newer trunk epoch that had landed bn-1sdp's continuum-semantic-diff work concurrently. Both bones had independently added an entry to continuum-intent's used_by/rationale in tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml, and the rebase tooling committed unresolved structured-conflict markers (<<<<<<< / ======= / >>>>>>>) straight into commit a287486 rather than blocking. Caught this by re-running just check after the first green run and finding the file broken; resolved by hand (merged both used_by entries and both rationale sentences), regenerated traceability against the post-rebase bones state, and landed the fix as 251c771. Worth flagging to whoever owns the workspace-sync tooling: a structured conflict should probably block rather than commit silently.\n- \"Canonical encoding via the crate's existing writer\" was resolved by inference (RFC 0028 has no explicit ID5-equivalent clause the way RFC 0037 does) — inferred canonical JSON from context-pack.schema.json's JSON-Schema normativity plus continuum-intent's own module doc (\"Which layer owns which spelling\"). Worth confirming when IMPL-01/06 (pack assembly) lands.\n- ModelActionRef is scoped to \"actions\" only (plan §6.2's literal word), not the broader \"model elements\" PR 8's model core also declares — additive if ever needed, not a redesign.\n- SourceSpan's 1-based line/column convention and Display rendering are this module's own stated choice; the IDL fixes the field set but not a rendering convention.\n- No .bones/ changes made or committed in-workspace, per the workspace-fence rule."}	blake3:mVpntkSG0_qMNBVbgMJIfcY_WtLNKinVWauhDwmC7bI
1785717575345284	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2Qk		item.comment	bn-16ek	{"body":"Landed @ 5b5f2620a9d5. Lead re-verified post-sync: continuum-context 30 tests green, zero conflict markers in dependency-rationale.toml (the mid-flight rebase incident's fix confirmed), GOV-1 clean, merge clean.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The full 11-member SelectionKind over the bullet's subset — the round-trip-safety reading, now twice-precedented (EdgeRelation's thirteen). (2) SelectedItem's pub(crate) constructor reachable only through the two typed builders — reference well-formedness as a property of what compiles. (3) file strengthened to WorkspacePath (the schema's string, the workspace's type — the honest direction). (4) INV-016 structural (no caller-string path into summaries). (5) Canonical encoding reused from continuum-intent's writer with the schema-normative reasoning stated — no second encoding. (6) The four honest declines, each named with what it waits on (nine remaining builders, per-item identity absent from schema, IMPL-04's expansion handles, unminted model_* handles).\n\nINCIDENT NOTED for the loop: a concurrent sibling merge left committed conflict markers in the shared rationale file mid-rebase; the agent's second just check caught it and the fixup landed. The catch chain (maw labels the conflict, gates re-run, markers refuse the merge) worked — but shared-file appends across parallel workspaces (dependency-rationale.toml is now three-way hot) should be sequenced or the file made append-tolerant.\n\nPR-11 IMPL-03 satisfied via bullet annotation."}	blake3:S0hVl_uVqP5jrEhm5zSNs6s_Rea2atslr7ME3N_AWAU
1785717575376861	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2gk		item.move	bn-16ek	{"state":"done"}	blake3:O50by3qqWP51GCVwgg99-EuWuvQv9yP-RkFhfDANALo
1785718124353245	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy2wk		item.comment	bn-3sypm	{"body":"FOUR DECISIONS (RFC 0038, new 'Authority' extension + 'Edges: what a CHECKED_BY edge names').\n\nD1 — the to-handle names a `receipt`, and ONLY a receipt. F14 said 'there is no node kind for a check'; wrong — plan §11.2's ProofReceipt (format RFC 0024) is exactly the artifact a checker emits to record that it checked something. The other two lose on INV-004's own words: a `run` is search's own output, so an edge pointing at one lets the search machinery be its own check; a `certificate` is what CROSSES a checker, not the crossing, so it belongs on the `from` — the canonical sentence is `certificate CHECKED_BY receipt` — and an artifact offered TOWARD a claim is what SUPPORTS already means. One kind, not a union; direction fixed with it. In the library: CheckTargetRule::Undecided DELETED, Default IS the decision, so a graph nobody configured refuses the other nineteen kinds; the three candidates survive as data with the reason two lost.\n\nD2 — edge_id is the content identity of what the edge asserts, named through ONE seam: the deployment's ContentIdentifier at ArtifactClass::Evidence — same seam and class that names nodes, so one identity kernel names every evidence artifact and evidence.verify's re-derivation discipline extends to edges unmodified. Preimage = five length-framed parts (domain tag, kind, from, to, checker). Domain separation is a fact not a hope: the tag `evidence-graph-edge/v1` carries a `/`, outside the artifact-handle character class every Commitment this daemon derives belongs to, so no node preimage can spell an edge preimage's first part. EvidenceNaming needed NO change — it was built for exactly this.\n\nD3 — edge-creation authority, now explicit in RFC 0038's Authority section (which previously covered node creation and status promotion only). Twelve edge kinds are ordinary assertions; CHECKED_BY is the graph's record that an INDEPENDENT check happened, so it is the checker's own artifact. (a) the checker IS the caller — the admitted capability's actor, and there is NO checker request field (the device observe.ingest uses for provenance.actor and evidence.verify for status); (b) a checker is a `service:` actor — enforced by continuum-evidence's ServiceIdentity::parse, so agent:/human:/ci: cannot append at ANY level, CapabilityDenied; (c) no self-certification — InsufficientEvidence when the checker equals the from-node's provenance.actor, checked BEFORE the receipt is read, same order and reason as evidence.verify's dimension four. Two negatives: an edge is NOT a promotion (no status, no Promotion witness, cannot reach the CAS — RFC 0027's 'no evidence operation writes a status' survives verbatim), and evidence.verify MUST NOT append one as a side effect of promoting.\n\nD4 — the daemon's node-identity derivation is the WIRE's. Decisive reason is RFC 0038's own sentence: 'a replayed write returns the original node identity' is a claim ACROSS processes and producers, and the library preimage contains provenance — hence the producing actor and a wall clock — so under it two ingests of one trace under one profile mint two nodes and convergence depends on when and by whom the write arrived (the write-order resolution plan §11.7 forbids). The daemon's preimage is (artifact commitment, capture profile) and nothing else. Reconciliation is NOT a library rewrite: EvidenceIdentity never reaches a schema instance — Display is hex, and to_record takes the ev_ handle as a PARAMETER — so the library already accepts the wire's names as input and the two never compete. Pinned by a_records_wire_name_is_a_parameter_not_a_derivation. Same rule for edges: the wire's excludes provenance so a checker's retry converges; the library's keeps it so two agents asserting one relation keep two credits. Both stated in `rule evidence.edge_identity`."}	blake3:rxG_mfgfhuWLCSD-U3zpd06v0wr5L8AIWB_rxFu1TP4
1785718143730014	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3Ak		item.comment	bn-3sypm	{"body":"THE COORDINATED CHANGE (F13 precedent, one change across every layer). evidence.link — @mutation @audit_recorded @since(\"3.3\"), authority execute, verdict StructuralVerdictValue, errors [InsufficientEvidence]. Request: subject (EvidenceHandle), receipt (Commitment), checker_profile (String) — and notably NO checker field. Response: edge, receipt, checker. It appends TWO artifacts as one write, the receipt node and the CHECKED_BY edge naming it, and minting the node here is load-bearing rather than convenient: a receipt any producer could append would be a forgeable check artifact, and an edge surface with nothing appendable to point at would have re-created F14 one level down. INV-007: the operation accepts no budget and manifests no omissions; not @task_starting, so obligation demands none. Admission T1-T4 inherited unchanged; no new DataGrant (considered, declined — the gate is the actor scheme, which ServiceIdentity already types, not a grant).\n\nMAP. plan.md §10.2:1234 (`evidence.get / query / verify / subscribe / link`) + plan.md:2708 (72→73). RFC 0027: registry row; distribution table (execute 40→41, @mutation 44→45, @audit_recorded 6→7, total 72→73); the 'evidence is entirely read' rationale rewritten to read-except-link with the reason; a 3.3 versioning bullet; correction 30 given a disposition (still the rule — a decision RFC 0038 OWED is not a flag paid with a verb); six present-tense '72' claims. RFC 0026: F14 marked PAID with all three blockers' answers; the open-question entry struck through with the residual narrowing named; verdict distribution structural 25→26 and total 72→73; annotation and authority-level tables; a 3.3 versioning bullet; correction 47's F14 clause. IDL: the operation block, `rule evidence.edge_identity` (@since 3.3), revision history 1.4, version 3.2→3.3, idl_version 1.3→1.4, '72 operations in 18 namespaces'→73. continuumd: registry.rs (OPERATION_COUNT 73, PROTOCOL_VERSION 3.3, IDL_VERSION 1.4, the OperationSpec in IDL order), operations/evidence.rs (two anonymous bodies, so the named-struct count is unmoved), family.rs (Arguments+Payload variants), codec/operations.rs (three arms), transport/mod.rs (one arm), daemon/state.rs (EvidenceEdge holding continuum-evidence's EdgeRelation — so 'no check edge without a checker' is a fact about what compiles in the daemon too — plus append_edge/evidence_edge/evidence_edges), daemon/evidence.rs (handler, edge_identity, edge_record, library_kind/wire_edge_kind token bridges, FAULTS rows). evidence.get now resolves an edge and evidence.query returns real edges — both carried comments reading 'no operation in the 72 appends an edge', which had become false.\n\nBUNDLE INVENTORY (deferred-and-bundled discipline; 3.0→3.1 and 3.1→3.2 precedent). RODE 3.3: F14 only. STAYED FLAGGED, each with its reason recorded in RFC 0026's flags preamble rather than only here. F15 (snapshot component lists) — DECIDED AGAINST an additive fix: its own entry says paying it the same way is seven more optional fields for a gap nothing has reached and paying it properly is the major-version type change; the toolchain half is blocked on plan §4.2 and the schema, not the wire. F16 (Cost has no transition dimension) — DECIDED BLOCKED: 'cannot gain one at a minor' is in the flag's own title, the owner is SD-12, and a tenth dimension moves four artifacts, one of them rank 1. F17 (the NFC obligation) — UNDECIDED between two candidate repairs; the entry explicitly declines to choose ('raised for whichever is decided first'). Adding @pattern to Commitment/PageToken/FileComponent.path would technically be minor-compatible, which is precisely why forcing it would be the drift the flag discipline exists to prevent. F18 (validation_basis) — UNDECIDED and half BREAKING: relaxing required→optional needs a major, and landing only the vocabulary half would add a member for a status the node schema does not define the field at. F13 is not a flag to pay; it is the discipline this change followed."}	blake3:B-blEADxTgjKITId9t1NVH81ox6lqY0fODAz9m7bJjI
1785718169080762	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3Qk		item.comment	bn-3sypm	{"body":"INV-004 §2 HEADING: **DECLINED**, with the gap named (honest-decline convention).\n\nWhat this bone delivers is bn-24i's ground (b) — 'evidence-graph-edge requires checker on CHECKED_BY is unenforced, and NO operation among the 72 appends an edge' — now mechanically evidenced end to end: a_checker_appends_a_receipt_and_the_check_edge_that_names_it; a_checker_may_not_record_a_check_of_its_own_production (with its control — the identical call from a DIFFERENT service is admitted, so the refusal is about who checked and not about the request); only_a_service_actor_may_append_a_check_edge (cap_observer is the same `execute` level WITH the production-trace grant and differs only in the actor scheme, and its denial is byte-identical to the unknown-subject denial, so neither is an oracle); a_second_identical_check_converges_on_the_edge_already_held (a DIFFERENT idempotency key, so convergence comes from the identity rule and not from the ledger); an_edge_identity_is_domain_separated_from_a_node_identity (and the subject's status is unmoved — an edge is not a promotion). At the library layer: a_check_edge_points_at_a_receipt_by_default and the_check_target_is_a_receipt_and_the_alternatives_are_refused. Plus a new INV-016 leg-1 probe, positive_evidence_link_checker_profile_is_echoed_verbatim_never_interpreted.\n\nbn-24i's other two grounds are unchanged, and I verified both in-workspace rather than assuming. (a) 'Certificates cross an independent checker' is still evidenced by exactly one certificate family / one engine / one kernel: crates/continuum-certificate/src/lib.rs is still 23 lines with no types, and the four optimized engines are still 21-line scaffolds. (c) The Lean half's 'no sorry / empty axiom manifest' is still enforced by artifact-diff (lean/scripts/axiom-manifest.sh --check generates into a scratch dir and diffs) with nothing asserting theoremsWithAxioms == 0, so a sorry landed together with a regenerated manifest still passes. Annotating now would overstate on the same two grounds bn-11yx and bn-24i already declined for. Recommend the successor be scoped to (a): it is the largest of the three, and the crate plan §20 names as THE certificate checker is empty.\n\nCOUNTS. Workspace 2,081 tests green (baseline 2,074; +7 = five new daemon_evidence INV-004 edge tests, one INV-016 probe, one identity-seam test). Three tests renamed in place because they asserted the question was OPEN. Registry 72→73. Count assertions moved with the bone cited — all of them the ones that exist to force exactly this visibility: idl_conformance operations 72→73, OPERATION_COUNT 72→73, rules 37→38, a_removed_operation_is_reported 71→72; registry_agreement rows/total/verdict-sum 72→73; inv016 landed 25→26 (test renamed); version_window PROTOCOL_VERSION plus the implemented set gaining 3.3; inv003's freshness tripwire on the renamed inv016 test. One test-parser change: idl_conformance's independent IDL parser now filters `since` out of an operation's annotation list, because @since dates a declaration and is not one of the seven behavioural annotations the IDL §1 catalogue lists and RFC 0027's registry column reproduces — evidence.link is the first operation to carry one. Codec goldens are NOT per-operation (codec_canonical_form's six vectors are hand-listed representative exchanges and its version() is a literal ProtocolVersion::new(3,2), not PROTOCOL_VERSION), so no new vectors are required and none were invented.\n\nGATES. cargo fmt --all --check clean; cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -D warnings clean; cargo test --workspace --locked green; full `just check` exit 0 (boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier — spec debt still empty, program_status ready — and Lean). Traceability regenerated: two lines moved, both mine. validation-results.json untouched. Commits 5fad4bf (RFC decision + library half) and 0ce741f (the wire surface). NOT merged, NOT pushed, no .bones/ changes in the workspace.\n\nCONCERNS. (1) The canonical sentence `certificate CHECKED_BY receipt` is only half reachable over the wire: no operation appends a `certificate` node, so today's subjects are the `run` nodes observe.ingest creates. Recorded in RFC 0026's open-questions entry rather than hidden — it is a missing producer verb, not a defect in D1. (2) The receipt node's identity is (receipt commitment, checker_profile), so it CAN collide with an existing node of another kind if someone ingested a trace under the same commitment and profile string; the handler refuses that through CheckTargetRule rather than silently pointing a check edge at a run, and the bridge to the library's NodeKind is the schema token itself (NodeKind::from_token(kind.as_wire())), not a second twenty-row table. (3) The receipt node's evidence_kind is inherited from the subject because the wire has no field for it and inventing one would be inventing vocabulary — defensible, but the least-forced choice in the handler and worth a reviewer's eye. (4) F17 is the one flag with a plausible minor-compatible path (three @pattern additions); I left it because the flag itself declines to choose between its two repairs, but a lead who reads that differently could bundle it into a 3.4 cheaply. (5) The daemon's edge identity excludes provenance while continuum-evidence's includes it — deliberate and stated twice (RFC 0038 D4, edge_identity's doc comment), and the two never meet because the library identity is never rendered; but it IS a genuine divergence between two crates' notions of 'the same edge', and it would bite anyone who joined a library graph to the wire by identity rather than by handle."}	blake3:NwYBsO76_3FdaiAJqUgsuhY2CAw5L1DlqVjJwPhv8gc
1785718288617272	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3gk		item.create	bn-bou09	{"description":"# Context\nbn-3sypm declined INV-004's plan §2 heading annotation with the gap named: ground (b) — evidence-graph-edge requires checker on CHECKED_BY — is now mechanically evidenced (evidence.link, protocol 3.3, six tests: checker requirement, self-certification refusal, service: gate with byte-identical denial, convergence, domain separation). Grounds (a) and (c) remain: (a) crates/continuum-certificate is a 23-line scaffold (the caller-facing certificate crate — checked from wire form, no engine imports, the four optimized engines are 21-line stubs); (c) the Lean axiom manifest is artifact-diff only, nothing asserts theoremsWithAxioms == 0.\n\n# The work\nDeliver continuum-certificate per plan §20's assignment (read the crate's own scaffold doc + plan §20 + docs/33 trust boundary + the kernel crates' wire entry points — the crate composes the four kernel checkers behind one caller-facing surface, checked from CONTCERT wire bytes, INV-004's no-self-certification structurally: no engine/forge/asupersync imports, enforced by check_crate_boundaries). Ground (c): add the axiom-manifest zero-assertion (or record why artifact-diff is the honest gate and (c) is a reading — decide against ADR-0035's text). Then annotate INV-004's plan §2 heading delivered citing all three grounds (bn-11yx/bn-24i groundwork + bn-3sypm's (b) + this bone's (a)/(c)).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] continuum-certificate delivers its §20 responsibility with the lint walls and evidence tests the kernel crates set as the pattern.\n- [ ] Ground (c) resolved (assertion added or reading recorded against ADR-0035).\n- [ ] INV-004 heading annotated delivered; validator green; just check green.","kind":"task","labels":["kernel","phase-a","req:phase-a","req:inv-004"],"size":"m","title":"continuum-certificate crate delivery — INV-004 ground (a), heading annotation"}	blake3:Vx1W5NKAAPRhWNOAPnZonTzz0FrUN9jRdWwPz7_DYxg
1785718288658629	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3wk		item.comment	bn-3sypm	{"body":"Landed @ 1da173ae33db. Lead re-verified post-sync: continuumd + continuum-evidence green, 2,081 workspace tests, validator pass, registry 72→73 with every count-assertion moved bone-cited.\n\nALL FOUR DECISIONS RATIFIED. D1 receipt-and-only-receipt: the INV-004 argument is exactly right — a run-target lets search be its own check, a certificate is the FROM of the canonical sentence (certificate CHECKED_BY receipt); the F14 assertion 'no node kind for a check' corrected against plan §11.2's ProofReceipt; Default-IS-the-decision replacing Undecided means an unconfigured graph refuses the other nineteen kinds. D2 content-identity edge_id through the one existing seam, domain tag with / outside the handle-class alphabet — separation as fact not probability. D3 checker-is-the-caller with the service: gate and no-self-cert BEFORE the receipt is read, plus the two negatives (an edge is not a promotion; evidence.verify appends nothing) — the Authority section now covers its third verb. D4 the wire's derivation wins for the reason that matters: provenance-in-preimage breaks replayed-write-returns-original-identity across processes; the library accepting names as parameters means no rewrite.\n\nBUNDLE DISCIPLINE RATIFIED: only F14 rode 3.3, and each of F15-F18's reasons is recorded in RFC 0026's flags preamble where the next bump will read it — F15 wants a major, F16 self-excludes, F17 undecided (its cheap-minor alternative reading noted, left for the flag's own decision), F18 half-breaking. evidence.link minting the receipt node in the same write is load-bearing (a producer-appendable receipt is a forgeable check artifact; an edge with nothing to point at re-creates F14 one level down) — accepted.\n\nINV-004 DECLINE ACCEPTED, successor created: bn-<see next comment> scoped to ground (a) (continuum-certificate delivery) + ground (c) (axiom-manifest zero-assertion or recorded reading), carrying req:inv-004. Concerns 1-3/5 recorded (certificate-node appendability in RFC 0026 open questions; collision-refusal bridging; evidence_kind inheritance as least-forced; the two edge-identity scopes deliberate and stated twice)."}	blake3:R3QVL80bl-YmbF_FgqFBKZ8lKyY31cBdo1FuCCSvxqQ
1785718288688957	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4Ak		item.move	bn-3sypm	{"state":"done"}	blake3:J09qTN-_760kN0D6BtiYOV3BWV5-MxFrMfz9IfgzVNs
