1785718323889474	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5gk		item.move	bn-3tz60	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:7FZXBpFt0H84VAt3b3J1oOr-wvisGOEyGcuuyY_yRJ0
1785718323892912	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5wk		item.assign	bn-3tz60	{"action":"assign","agent":"continuum-dev"}	blake3:9s9lgQ8JpCfQPxT6SBkYTTWZhksvbFAxsjqltEpDbnQ
1785720325425459	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6Ak		item.comment	bn-bou09	{"body":"THE §20 READING (ground (a)). Plan §20 gives this crate exactly two facts: it is in the crate list, immediately above the four continuum-kernel-* crates, and \"proof/certificate checker does not depend on search engines\". Everything else follows from what is already law elsewhere, and it narrows the assignment rather than widening it.\n\nDirection is fixed, not chosen. check_kernel_covenant.py's KCOV-06 sets ADMITTED_KERNEL_DEPENDENCIES = frozenset() — a kernel manifest may declare NOTHING — so no kernel crate can depend on continuum-certificate. The edge can only run certificate -> kernel. check_crate_boundaries.py permits it: CHECKER = (*KERNEL, \"continuum-certificate\") and the forbidden targets are ENGINES + forge, which the kernels are not.\n\nThat kills the scaffold doc's own framing. It said this crate \"defines the boundary format rather than any evaluator\" — written at PR-1, before PR 9 landed four wire modules. The formats are now owned one per family by the kernel that checks them (CONTCERT/CONTSATC/CONTSMTC/CONTTMPC), each with a decoder sharing no code with any producer. Restating a grammar here would be a second authority over bytes the kernel already owns, and docs/03 §8's diversity argument makes the duplicate worse than useless. So the crate is DELIBERATELY THIN and the thinness is the reading: it owns DISPATCH, NOT SEMANTICS. This is less than \"a checker\" and I want that on the record — no obligation is evaluated here, no byte reinterpreted, no verdict minted.\n\nROUTING DESIGN. check_certificate(&[u8]) -> Outcome. Reads the leading 8-byte magic; Family::magic() returns the kernels' own wire::MAGIC constants rather than restating them, so the table cannot drift from the format (asserted against all four). Hands the WHOLE slice, magic included, to that kernel's check_certificate — no trimming, no reframing — so the kernel's decoder re-reads from byte zero and re-validates the magic it was routed by; the routing read is a dispatch hint and the kernel stays the only interpreter. Outcome::Checked(KernelVerdict) carries the kernel's own Verdict value in the kernel's own type; Outcome::Unroutable(RoutingFault) is the fourth outcome.\n\nThree refusals, each deliberate. (1) NO BOOLEAN on the surface — no is_verified, no -> bool, no Result<(), _>. The kernels offer is_verified \"never as a substitute for matching\"; here there are four outcomes, not three, and one bit is even less able to carry them. A caller writes the collapse where a reviewer sees it; family_routing.rs does exactly that in six visible lines and inv004 asserts src/ has no -> bool. (2) NO UNIFIED CLAIM ACCESSOR — sat reports propagations, smt an assurance class and its trusted theory lemmas, core states/transitions; a lowest common denominator drops exactly what docs/03 §2 and RFC 0005's proof-producing-solver policy insist a reader see. (3) A ROUTING FAULT IS NOT A VERDICT — never Rejected, never Unsupported. An unrecognised magic could be a corrupted CONTCERT, a family a later kernel will own, or a JPEG; deciding between those is what a decoder does and this crate has none. Answering either would be the INV-008 collapse. Also structural: CheckedClaim::new is pub(crate) in every kernel, so this crate CANNOT fabricate a Verified even in principle — it can only relay one.\n\nZero deps beyond the four kernels; nothing external. Kernel lint wall carried (forbid(unsafe_code) + the seven clippy denials), asserted by a test because check_kernel_covenant.py's scope is KERNEL_CRATES only — this crate's wall is asserted here or nowhere.\n\nEVIDENCE, +12 tests, every input built as bytes by a local encoder from the documented grammars (no crate's own fixtures). tests/family_routing.rs (8): routing table is the kernels' magics and they partition the input space, pairwise distinct, from_magic inverts magic exactly; each family's green certificate reaches its own checker and verifies; THE ANTI-DRIFT ONE — the relayed value EQUALS the routed kernel's own check_certificate on the same bytes, for green / trailing-byte-rejected / foreign-wire-epoch-unsupported artifacts of all four families, and the Unsupported arm is then named per family so a weakening into Rejected fails twice; the mutation sweep inverts every byte past the magic and asserts route unchanged + composition == kernel + verdict lost; short input and unclaimed magic are typed routing faults; a certificate stamped with another family's magic goes to the kernel it NAMES and is refused there, no sniffing and no fallback; every prefix plus 256 filler patterns never verify and never panic. tests/inv004_no_self_certification.rs (+4): exactly one public entry point and it takes wire-form bytes; MAGIC is the ONLY item taken from any kernel's wire module (so no decoded structure is ever held here — the INV-004 sentence cited is plan §20's \"a certificate is checked from its wire form, never from shared memory\", preserved THROUGH the composition, not merely at its far end); the lint wall; no -> bool.\n\nMUTATION SWEEP HONESTY. The sweep has survivors and I enumerated them instead of tolerating them: Core [165, 174..180], Sat [174,175, 203..205], Smt [230..232], Temporal [166, 175..181, 296..303]. Every one is a BOUND, a CAPACITY or an IDENTIFIER — a declared variable range widened, a declared variable count raised, a clause/step id renamed upward, rank(2) increased. Loosening a declaration makes a WEAKER claim about the same content, which is still true, so the kernel is right to accept it. The list is asserted by equality, with the failure message telling a future reader to read the difference before widening it. Everything else is decisive: 235/206/242/280 bytes per family, all asserted > 64.\n\nANTI-VACUITY OF THE TESTS THEMSELVES. Two injected mutants, both caught: routing Smt to the sat checker fails 4 of 8; making an unroutable input fall back to kernel-core fails 3 of 8."}	blake3:m-hZfuytpsPQ00r0v1Gcgu0WQ19ydX_oppF0dyGBt_8
1785720325464881	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6Qk		item.comment	bn-bou09	{"body":"GROUND (c) — DECIDED AGAINST THE TEXTS, ASSERTION OWED AND ADDED. ADR-0035's decision is machine-captured axiom output and its consequence is that UNDOCUMENTED axioms fail release gates. That is a documentation obligation and the existing artifact-diff discharges it completely (regenerate with Lean.collectAxioms into a scratch dir, diff the checked-in files). On ADR-0035's own text, NOTHING FURTHER WAS OWED — and I want that reading recorded, because it is not the whole story. The stronger obligation is stated elsewhere and was not mechanical: START_HERE PR-4a Exit and plan.md §21 Phase A both say T0/T1 compile \"with no sorry and EMPTY axiom manifests\", and AGENTS.md says \"no sorry, no axioms, no native_decide\". \"Empty\" is a fact about contents the diff cannot see.\n\nVerified by experiment, not argued. Replaced one T0 proof (Continuum.Reflect.andLeft) with sorry, regenerated the manifest — the DOCUMENTED workflow — and the old gate printed \"axiom manifest is up to date\" and exited 0 with 20 theorems depending on sorryAx. The hole was real and exactly as bn-24i/bn-3sypm described it. Source and artifacts restored afterwards; lean/ is byte-identical to HEAD.\n\nAdded after the diff, over the checked-in artifacts, as pure text needing no toolchain: three independent readings of the same fact — the generator's theoremsWithAxioms field; every row's \"axioms\": [] in the JSON read WITHOUT trusting that field; and the .txt transcript, which spells a non-empty row in different words. With the same sorry in place all three fire, the offending theorems are named, and the gate exits 1. Scope is stated in the script header and is deliberately the T0/T1 ladder, whose exit line says \"empty\" — NOT a project-wide axiom ban, since RFC 0012 permits documented foundations and a later rung using Classical.choice would carry its own manifest and its own bar. Generation stays total on purpose: an offending axiom must remain writable so a developer can see which theorem acquired it; the gate refuses, the generator does not. Justfile lean recipe comment updated to say why the diff does not imply the assertion.\n\nANNOTATION: TAKEN. plan.md §2 INV-004 heading now reads (delivered: bn-11yx, bn-24i, bn-3sypm, bn-bou09 — ...) citing all four with what each pays: bn-11yx the manifest-scoped dependency boundary, bn-24i the promotion boundary as a type, bn-3sypm evidence.link / CHECKED_BY, this bone the crate delivery and the axiom-manifest assertion. No inner parenthesis, because traceability.py strips (delivered:[^)]*) and would otherwise leave residue. Registry: INV-004 active -> satisfied; report's invariant row active 11 -> 10, satisfied 7 -> 8. Sixth §2 invariant heading annotated under this convention, after INV-002/003/005/006/008.\n\nPRE-EXISTING TEST CHANGED — ONE, DELIBERATELY, AND FLAGGED. inv004's the_manifest_declares_no_dependencies_of_any_kind_today was scoped by its own comment to \"while it remains a PR-9 follow-on scaffold\". Delivering ground (a) is the event it existed to make visible and it fired. Replaced by the STRONGER form of the same claim — an allow-list of exactly the four kernel crates, so a fifth dependency (forbidden or not) is still caught in this crate's own suite before the boundary/covenant/governance walk. The forbidden-family test is byte-identical. Nothing else pre-existing was touched.\n\nCOUNTS. cargo test --workspace --locked: 2,140 -> 2,152 (+12: 8 new in tests/family_routing.rs, 4 new in tests/inv004_no_self_certification.rs). Note the arithmetic: I measure the pre-change baseline at 2,140 on this HEAD (2,111 lib/integration + 29 doc-tests), not the 2,081 the brief quoted; I did not chase the discrepancy, but the DELTA is exactly my 12 and the diff is confined to crates/continuum-certificate, Cargo.lock/toml, dependency-rationale.toml, the lean script, the Justfile and the three dossier files.\n\nGATES. cargo fmt --all --check clean. cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -D warnings clean. cargo test --workspace --locked green. Full just check exit 0 twice (before and after the annotation): boundaries self-test 15 cases + real run 0 violations, kernel covenant, governance (code policy self-test 56 fixtures pass, GOV-1-06 and GOV-1-07 both green with the new edges), dossier validator status pass / program_status ready / spec debt empty, and lean (lake build + the diff + the new emptiness assertion). validation-results.json untouched. Commits be750ed (crate), b3e5415 (axiom-manifest gate), fba87d8 (annotation + regen). NOT merged, NOT pushed, no .bones/ in the workspace.\n\nCONCERNS. (1) HOT FILE: I edited tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml — continuum-kernel-core's entry moves dev-only -> trusted-checking-base with kinds [\"dev\",\"normal\"] and used_by gaining continuum-certificate (forced by GOV-1-07's checker-dependencies-are-tcb, not a preference), plus three new entries for sat/smt/temporal. Expect a rebase against bn-1mhcr/bn-3tz60; the edit is four contiguous [[dependency]] blocks. (2) The mutation sweep's survivor lists are frozen positions in THIS crate's fixtures but they encode facts about the KERNELS' slack. A kernel that tightened a check would turn this crate's test red — a correct and informative failure, but a cross-crate coupling a reviewer should agree to. (3) Outcome carries #[allow(clippy::large_enum_variant)] with the reasoning written out, following continuum_mcp::answer::Outcome and continuumd::daemon::family::Arguments: boxing would put an allocation on the arm that is supposed to be common and would insert an indirection of this crate's own invention between the caller and the kernel's value, when the whole claim of the type is that nothing comes between them. (4) NOT DELIVERED, deliberately: receipt composition. Each kernel's receipt takes that crate's own Seam of caller-supplied trusted inputs and the four are distinct types; unifying them is an RFC 0024 / ADR-0035 / INV-014 receipt-format decision, not a dispatch decision, and inventing the union here would invent vocabulary. A caller holding a KernelVerdict already has the kernel's claim and can call that kernel's receipt directly. Said so in the crate doc's \"Scope of this slice\". (5) The crate has no consumer yet — continuumd still does not route certificates through it, and evidence.verify still answers InsufficientEvidence for certificate-class nodes because \"the independent certificate checker\" was a scaffold (bn-24i's own note). That wiring is a daemon change, outside this bone's fence, and it is now unblocked. (6) A restamped artifact is refused by the kernel it NAMES rather than by the one that wrote its body: correct, and asserted, but the diagnostic will name the wrong grammar's fields. Honest and safe, worth a docs sentence if a caller ever surfaces those rejections to humans."}	blake3:X_wKnr1odAS-Rz7wwr951z1BSNoAe0KJ_EriM7Md2wU
1785720343045410	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6gk		item.comment	bn-bou09	{"body":"CORRECTIONS to my two landing comments, both mine and both arithmetic.\n\n(1) Decisive-byte counts in the mutation sweep: Core 235, Sat 209, Smt 242, Temporal 280 (green.len() - 8 magic bytes - the enumerated survivors). I wrote 206 for Sat; it is 209. The assertion in the test is 'decisive > 64' and is unchanged — only my prose was wrong.\n\n(2) INV-004 is the EIGHTH §2 invariant heading to carry a (delivered: ...) annotation, not the sixth: the seven before it are INV-002, INV-003, INV-005, INV-006, INV-008, INV-012 and INV-016. That is also why the traceability report's invariant row reads satisfied 7 -> 8, which the commit message states correctly."}	blake3:TRMkSdaMJw4ogd9STL29CvpiP6DXU4txeGs4Uov5YG0
1785720443012969	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6wk		item.create	bn-dtg61	{"description":"# Context\nbn-bou09 delivered continuum-certificate (thin dispatch: check_certificate(&[u8]) → Outcome routing by 8-byte magic to the owning kernel, verdict relayed as the kernel's own value, Unroutable never Rejected/Unsupported). Concern 5: the crate has NO consumer — continuumd does not route certificates through it, and evidence.verify still answers InsufficientEvidence for certificate-class nodes because the checker was a scaffold when the family landed.\n\n# The work\n- Wire evidence.verify's certificate-class path through continuum-certificate::check_certificate. Check plan §20 permits the continuumd→continuum-certificate edge (a daemon consuming the checker is the architecture's point, but verify §20 states it; boundary checker must stay green; GOV-1-07 rationale entry for the new edge).\n- The verdict surfaces with the kernel vocabulary intact (Verified/Rejected/Unsupported + Unroutable's typed spelling on the wire — check which existing ErrorCode/verdict field carries it; NO new wire shape without the flag discipline; if the existing evidence.verify answer shape cannot carry the four-outcome vocabulary honestly, record the gap as a flag rather than squeezing).\n- Evidence tests through Daemon::dispatch: a real CONTCERT artifact verifies; a mutated one is Rejected with the kernel's own reason; an unroutable one is typed as such; the InsufficientEvidence arm remains for genuinely-absent artifacts.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] evidence.verify on a certificate-class node runs the composition; all four outcomes reachable and typed.\n- [ ] Boundary + covenant + governance green; wire audit stated (existing fields vs flag).\n- [ ] just check green.","kind":"task","labels":["kernel","phase-a","req:phase-a","req:inv-004"],"size":"s","title":"continuumd routes certificate-class verification through continuum-certificate (evidence.verify wiring)"}	blake3:XZRVzaX1Jzag3lgHvp3ILUxcasLPRT3eTmJhAKST8RE
1785720443051913	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7Ak		item.comment	bn-bou09	{"body":"Landed @ ca4e72e3f45b. Lead re-verified post-sync: continuum-certificate 12 tests green, workspace green, validator pass.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) The thin-dispatch §20 reading with the direction PROVEN from KCOV-06's empty admitted-dependencies set — and the rejection of the scaffold doc's 'defines the boundary format' framing (a second authority over kernel-owned bytes). Dispatch, not semantics; recorded as deliberately LESS than 'a checker'. (2) The three refusals: four outcomes never three (Unroutable ≠ Rejected ≠ Unsupported — telling those apart is a decoder's job and this crate has none); no unified claim accessor (the four CheckedClaim types differ where docs/03 §2 demands a reader see); no boolean surface. (3) Whole-slice-including-magic handoff (no trimming = no second parse). (4) The pre-existing manifest test replaced per its OWN sunset comment — the event it existed to detect fired; the allow-list form is stronger. (5) GROUND (c): the sorry-injection experiment is the catch of the wave — 'no sorry, empty manifests' was asserted by three dossier texts and verified by NOTHING (old gate exit 0 with 20 theorems on sorryAx); three independent readings now fire on the same injection. Scoped to T0/T1, generation stays total. (6) INV-004 heading annotation ACCEPTED citing the four-bone set — the eighth §2 invariant delivered.\n\nCONCERNS: mutation-survivor lists as cross-crate coupling accepted (a kernel tightening turns this red — correct failure); large_enum_variant allow with reasoning follows precedent; receipt composition correctly declined (RFC 0024/ADR-0035 decision). CONCERN 5 ROUTED: bn-<next comment> wires evidence.verify through the crate — the no-consumer gap closes there."}	blake3:q5eHZaHbnvd3wBOYr-r2NLnpJ4QuVcfXT6RniY9xPjE
1785720443083361	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7Qk		item.move	bn-bou09	{"state":"done"}	blake3:i_JdJNwnwd8ZyJXq8A_jLbUwzOWAKL7JGTongi7Eamk
1785720453581500	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7gk		item.comment	bn-bou09	{"body":"Consumer-wiring successor: bn-dtg61 (evidence.verify through continuum-certificate)."}	blake3:lDynd1RuwRg5BjV8tBfWI5n5opxw1upwBN9fN6-We2U
1785720689029432	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7wk		item.comment	bn-1mhcr	{"body":"canonical_cbor landed, both encodings from one declaration. Committed in-workspace as 53381d5; not merged, not pushed.\n\nARCHITECTURE — one encoder, two document models (not two encoders from one macro)\nThe instruction suggested emitting the CBOR encoder from protocol_struct! so one declaration yields both. I went one step further and the macro needed no new arm: ProtocolValue::encode/decode are now generic over a new codec::Document trait, with one implementation per encoding (json::Json, cbor::Cbor). Two encoders generated from one text can still diverge if the generators are edited apart; one encoder parameterized by its output model cannot diverge, because there is nothing to edit apart. Which key, in what order, under which presence rule is decided ONCE. The encoding contributes only leaf spellings. Argued at codec/mod.rs \"Two encodings, one type layer\".\n\nCBOR-CANONICAL vs IDL DIVERGENCE (table also at crates/continuumd/src/codec/cbor.rs)\n| question | CBOR core deterministic | this protocol | who wins |\n| map key order | bytewise over the ENCODED key => shorter keys first (\"b\" before \"aa\") | ascending code point of the key (\"aa\" before \"b\") | THE IDL |\n| definite vs indefinite lengths | definite only | definite only | agree |\n| integer head | shortest | shortest | agree |\n| string/array/map length head | shortest | shortest | agree |\n| floating point | shortest, canonical NaN | absent — protocol declares no float | agree, vacuously |\n| tags | permitted | absent — no tag is load-bearing | narrower here |\n| major type 1 (negative) | permitted | absent — no signed integer declared | narrower here |\n| simple values other than 20/21/22 | permitted | absent — \"undefined\" is not a second named absence | narrower here |\nThe one real divergence is key order and the IDL wins; pinned by the_cbor_key_order_is_the_idls_and_not_the_cbor_specifications, which asserts the exact bytes a262616102616201 and shows the length-first spelling is rejected with KeyOrder.\n\nWHY THE IDL NEEDED NO EDIT (criterion 6)\nI did NOT extend the IDL, and the reason is a finding rather than a shortcut. Every rule of the canonical subset follows from the rule's own first sentence — \"one message has exactly one byte spelling under the negotiated encoding\". CBOR gives a writer more freedom than JSON and every one of those freedoms is a second spelling of a message that already has one: 9f01ff and 8101 are both [1]; 1801 and 01 are both the integer 1; c06161 and 6161 both denote \"a\" to a reader that ignores tags. the_canonical_subset_is_derived_from_one_spelling_not_chosen proves each derivation the only way it can be proved — by exhibiting BOTH spellings and showing they denote the same message. The derivation table is at cbor.rs. Editing the IDL would also have forced idl_version 1.4->1.5 (its own doc says \"bumped on every change to the file\"), stranding RFC 0026's prose recording 1.4 — a file outside my fence. Recorded as a deliberate non-edit, not an omission.\n\nPRESENCE-DISCIPLINE MAPPING\nNothing to map: presence is decided in codec/mod.rs's nine put_*/take_* helpers, which are now generic over Document, so CBOR runs the SAME code. required => key present and not 0xf6; nullable => key present, MAY be 0xf6; optional => key absent, or present and not 0xf6. Missing => MissingField, optional-present-and-null => UnexpectedNull, nullable-absent => MissingField — identical variants, identical struct/field names, because it is one function. presence_is_three_valued_in_cbor_exactly_as_in_json asserts all four ways to conflate them.\n\nREADER REJECTIONS — every JSON one has a twin or a recorded reason (full table at CborError)\ntwins: NotUtf8, Truncated, TrailingBytes, TooDeep, DuplicateKey, KeyOrder; Signed=>NegativeInteger; FloatingPoint=>FloatingPoint; LeadingZero=>NonShortestHead (same rule, one spelling per value, in the head instead of in decimal digits); Unexpected splits by major type into Reserved/SimpleValue/Tag/NonTextKey.\nrecorded reasons for NO twin: IntegerRange — JSON's 2^53-1 bound exists because a number beyond it is one some conforming readers round; CBOR major 0 is exact over the whole u64 range, so there is no boundary to police. RawControl and BadEscape — CBOR text strings have no escape layer, so a control character already has exactly one spelling.\nCBOR-only, no JSON counterpart: IndefiniteLength, Tag, NonTextKey.\nevery_cbor_reader_rejection_is_reachable_and_named exhibits an input for all 13 variants.\n\nTWO LEAVES DIFFER, AND ONLY TWO (IDL section 3)\nBytes: base64url text in JSON, major type 2 here; a TEXT string offered to a Bytes field in CBOR is a second spelling and is rejected. U64: number-or-decimal-string in JSON, one unsigned integer here; a decimal string offered to a U64 field in CBOR is rejected. bytes_and_a_large_u64_are_the_only_leaves_the_two_encodings_spell_differently pins both with literal bytes and round-trips 0, 1, 23, 24, 255, 256, 65535, 65536, 2^53-1, 2^53, u64::MAX in both.\n\nGOLDEN-SET INVENTORY — 24 literal vectors x 2 encodings = 48 byte sequences (JSON as text, CBOR as hex, in tests/codec_canonical_cbor.rs)\n6 exchange vectors: handshake.client_hello, handshake.server_reject, workspace.create.request, evidence.link.request (the 73rd operation, new in 3.3), denial.result, task.result — all at protocol 3.3.\n18 body vectors, one per IDL namespace (workspace, intent, verification, model, program, proof, evidence, observe, query, context, task, debug, repair, forge, benchmark, correspondence, refinement, failure), each the namespace's first operation's request body.\nPlus the mechanical registry sweep: all 73 operations' request AND response structs (146) plus all 46 named structs = 192 shapes, each decoded from a document synthesized out of its own FieldSpec list and re-encoded, in both encodings, with field sequences compared across them and the bytes shown canonical in each. That sweep is reached through StructSpec's own round-trip closures (added to StructSpec::of::<T>()), so the swept set IS the registry's set — there is no second list of 146 type names to drift.\nHONEST BOUNDARY, stated in the file's own header: the 24 are literal byte sequences a second implementation can be tested against; the 192-shape sweep is a mechanical claim, not a literal vector, and does not pretend to be one.\n\n(1/2 — inventory, mutation counts, doc fix, counts, gates and concerns follow in 2/2.)"}	blake3:A6I9OYs0emoKKY8Ss-txkQDahLBjhOWyXJN0ZA6YYw0
1785720689070710	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8Ak		item.comment	bn-1mhcr	{"body":"(2/2 — continues the bn-1mhcr delivery comment.)\n\nMUTATION SWEEP\n11,074 mutants over the 24 CBOR vectors (every byte position x {0x00, 0xff, b^0x01}, skipping no-ops). 4,849 rejected outright (43.8%); the other 6,225 parsed to a DIFFERENT document and each was its own canonical form. Zero decoded back to the original — that is the injectivity half, the one a permissive CBOR reader fails immediately because it would read 1801 and 01 as the same integer. Bounded and linear: one mutant allocated at a time, no capacity reserved from any declared length. Separately, an_inflated_length_costs_a_bounds_check_and_not_an_allocation pins the CBOR-specific OOM hazard JSON does not have (a nine-byte head claiming 2^64-1 items answers Truncated, reserving nothing).\n\nTHE DOC/TEST MISMATCH (bone comment)\nDid both halves. codec/operations.rs cited every_operation_the_families_serve_round_trips_through_the_codec in tests/codec_round_trip.rs — neither the name nor the file existed. I wrote the test under exactly that name (in tests/codec_canonical_cbor.rs) and corrected the file reference. It is driven from the registry: all 73 operations are offered to the tables, 26 decode (asserted) and 47 answer UnknownOperation (asserted), each decoded variant names its own operation, and each round-trips through BOTH encodings. The doc's claim is now true rather than merely repaired.\n\nWIRE VISIBILITY\nNo flag, no feature gate. Server::answer now reads the request frame and writes the result frame in Negotiated::encoding — that is what having negotiated one means. a_cbor_connection_serves_a_whole_exchange_in_cbor drives a real exchange and asserts the frames are CBOR maps and are NOT parseable as canonical JSON. Client-side _in::<D> spellings added throughout the transport; the plain names stay canonical_json so no existing caller moved.\n\nCOUNTS\nworkspace tests 2140 -> 2159 (+19, all new, 0 failures, no pre-existing test edited). New: crates/continuumd/src/codec/cbor.rs (623 lines), crates/continuumd/tests/codec_canonical_cbor.rs (1570 lines). Touched: codec/mod.rs, codec/operations.rs, protocol/spec.rs, protocol/scalar.rs, transport/mod.rs, lib.rs. tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml NOT touched — no CBOR crate; the canonical subset is by hand exactly as the JSON codec was, and pulling a crate would have been strictly worse here because the one thing this protocol needs is the ONE rule no general CBOR library implements (code-point key order instead of length-first). A library would have had to be fought, not used. validation-results.json not touched. No dossier lines changed, so no regen was needed.\n\nGATES\ncargo fmt --all --check: clean. cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings: clean. cargo test -p continuumd --locked: green. just check: exit 0 (twice, before and after the final tightening).\n\nCONCERNS / FOLLOW-UPS\n1. Handshake frames are still canonical_json unconditionally, and this is a genuine IDL gap rather than laziness: a ClientHello is the frame that OFFERS the encodings, so it cannot already be in the one the offer has not selected, and rule handshake.negotiation says which encoding is chosen and not which encoding the choosing is spelled in. I refused to invent a bootstrap rule. Recorded in transport/mod.rs's module docs; it belongs in the IDL and is worth a bone.\n2. Cross-encoding TYPED-value equality does not hold for any message carrying an Opaque, and that is the contract, not a defect — rule encoding.opaque_payloads says the payload is \"a canonical value of the NEGOTIATED encoding\", so its bytes are the encoding's. Asserted explicitly: the two workspace.create envelopes differ in \"arguments\" and in nothing else, and the decoded bodies are equal. Anyone writing a cross-encoding test for intent.propose_revision (whose IntentChangeSet.changes is an Opaque) will hit this; documented at codec/mod.rs.\n3. StructSpec gained two fn-pointer fields and a hand-written PartialEq that compares name+fields only (the closures are derived from the type, not part of the description). Worth a glance from whoever owns the conformance tests, though idl_conformance and registry_agreement are both green.\n4. The registry sweep's synthesizer resolves alias @patterns from a small hand-written table (there is no regex engine in the workspace to generate a matching string from a pattern). If an alias's pattern ever tightens, that table is what needs to move — it will fail loudly, not silently."}	blake3:elcAo7_TGvl5H-5Ko-oJM8Tpj6XgXqNat-CFbOqqJsE
1785720784337448	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8Qk		item.create	bn-1h158	{"description":"# Context\nbn-1mhcr's finding: handshake frames are canonical_json unconditionally, and that is currently an UNWRITTEN rule — ClientHello is the frame that OFFERS the encodings, so it cannot be spelled in the one not yet selected, and rule handshake.negotiation says which encoding is CHOSEN, not which encoding the choosing is spelled in. The agent correctly refused to invent a bootstrap rule; the fact is recorded in crates/continuumd/src/transport/mod.rs.\n\n# The work\nRecord the bootstrap rule in the IDL's handshake section (canonical_json is the bootstrap encoding for hello/negotiation frames; negotiated encoding applies from the first post-negotiation frame — or whatever reading RFC 0026 supports), idl_version 1.4→1.5 with revision-history note, RFC 0026 prose aligned, conformance test pinning the bootstrap bytes. Doc-grade wire clarification of existing behavior — audit whether it needs a flag (it describes what every conforming daemon already does; the bn-23j7s no-F-flag rule likely applies).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The bootstrap rule is written where the IDL owns it; conformance suite pins it; transport doc cites the rule instead of apologizing for its absence.\n- [ ] just check green.","kind":"task","labels":["protocol","spec-debt","req:phase-a","req:pr-5"],"size":"xs","title":"Handshake bootstrap encoding — ClientHello cannot ride a not-yet-negotiated encoding (IDL gap from bn-1mhcr)"}	blake3:x0j2ppLrMwjCuksayTIzyk0wPyEJhv65qCfIMKhvYug
1785720784375381	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8gk		item.comment	bn-1mhcr	{"body":"Landed @ 29e4829d192c. Lead re-verified post-sync: continuumd suites green, workspace 2,159, validator implied by just check.\n\nRATIFIED. (1) THE DESIGN FINDING: one encoder parameterized by codec::Document rather than a second emitter arm — two generators edited apart can diverge, one encoder with two output models cannot; key/order/presence decided once, encodings contribute only leaf spellings (exactly two differ, both IDL-mandated). Better than the brief asked for. (2) The IDL-needed-no-edit finding with each canonical-subset rule DERIVED from 'one message has exactly one byte spelling' and proven by exhibiting both spellings — the derivation test is the strongest form of not-editing. (3) The golden discipline: 48 literal byte sequences + the 192-shape mechanical sweep reached through StructSpec's own closures so the swept set IS the registry's (no second name list to drift), with the literal/mechanical boundary stated not blurred. (4) 11,074 mutants, zero decoded back to original. (5) No CBOR crate — the one rule this protocol needs (IDL order over length-first) is the one no general library implements; fighting a dependency is worse than writing the subset. (6) The doc/test mismatch fixed by making the doc's claim TRUE (test written under the promised name). (7) Transport serves negotiated encoding — no flag, conformance behavior.\n\nFINDING ROUTED: handshake bootstrap encoding is an unwritten rule → bn-<next comment> (IDL handshake section, idl_version 1.5, conformance pin). StructSpec's fn-pointer fields + hand-written PartialEq accepted (name+fields equality is the honest comparison for a description type carrying closures).\n\nPR-5's canonical_cbor debt is PAID — the conforming-daemon golden set exists across all 73 operations in both encodings."}	blake3:BBXRlD-n6ZZSDQJrSrRl6CWUqD1Zkv-12rnfmEm63gw
