1785724526660106	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEykwo		item.assign	bn-13nlo	{"action":"assign","agent":"continuum-dev"}	blake3:HtIj18_suFoOepBk7XOaGXctIaz2X_vNDpFSC2PmgnY
1785725607736518	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEylAo		item.comment	bn-13nlo	{"body":"RFC 0031 repairs delivered in `.maw/workspaces/bn-13nlo/`, commit 019466d. Not merged, not closed.\n\n## Repair 1 — example violated the unchanged shortcut\n\nBefore: `semantic-diff.example.json` had `before_intent == after_intent == \"in_ack_v1\"` with a non-empty `intent_changes` (one `properties`/`unchanged` record). The unchanged-shortcut rule requires `intent_changes` empty whenever the two identities are equal — the example instead spelled the equality out as a record, which the shortcut forbids.\n\nAfter: `after_intent` → `in_ack_v2` (distinct identity). `intent_changes` now carries two records: `bounds`/`expanded` (no unit — bounds has none per the schema's own comment — protected: true, permitted under `no-decrease`) plus the pre-existing `properties`/`unchanged` record for `AckImpliesDurable`. `policy.decision` stays `allow` (verified against `PolicyTable::verdict`'s rules: `no-decrease` doesn't deny `expanded`, `locked` doesn't deny `unchanged`); `policy.reasons` rewritten to name both facts instead of the now-false \"protected intent unchanged\". Demonstrates R2 on both sides: an actual equality and an actual inequality driving a direction.\n\nPicked \"mint a second identity\" over \"delete the demo\" per the ACs. `bounds`/`expanded` specifically because `diff_demo1` (this artifact) is also cited by `repair-transaction.example.json` and `promotion-receipt.example.json`, and RFC 0032 gate 3 `intent_integrity` = \"passed\" iff the diff's recomputed decision is `allow`; both siblings show it passed/promoted, outside this bone's fence to edit. `bounds`/`expanded`/`no-decrease` was the only genuinely-directional, unconditionally-`allow` field/relation pair available, so it's the only change that fixes the shortcut without staling those two RFC-0032 examples. `promotion-receipt.example.json` needed no edit: its `intent` field is documented as the *base* intent (still `in_ack_v1`); `semantic_diff`/`intent_diff` still both name `diff_demo1` (correction 10 holds).\n\nRan 6 ad-hoc schema mutant checks on the pair: properties record missing required `unit` → rejected; bounds record with non-enum relation → rejected; bounds record `protected:false` → rejected (const:true); protected `incomparable` + `policy.decision:allow` → rejected (fail-closed conditional); corrected baseline → accepted; `after_intent` off-pattern → rejected. All six behaved as expected.\n\n## Repair 2 — observers sentence was self-contradictory\n\nBefore: \"...any mixed movement is `incomparable`. Dropping an event family... MUST classify `coarsened` even when other components grow — the mixed case blocks... regardless.\" Read literally, sentence 2 amends sentence 1 into contradiction (growth anywhere rules out \"all four subsets\").\n\nAfter: \"...any mixed movement is `incomparable` — the componentwise rule admits no exception for this case. Dropping an event family..., with no other component growing in the same comparison, ... classifies `coarsened` under the rule just stated. If another component grows in the same comparison, the movement is mixed: it classifies `incomparable`, not `coarsened`, and still blocks... exactly as every other non-affirmative relation does.\" States the componentwise rule unambiguously (the \"two jobs\"/safer reading bn-1sdp already implemented). Added correction 15 (cites bn-1sdp, bn-13nlo); correction 14 records repair 1 (cites bn-13nlo).\n\n`observers.rs`: updated the verbatim RFC blockquote to match; collapsed the ~26-line \"Reading...\" disambiguation essay to a short citation of correction 15 (doc-only, no code/test touched). Left `crates/continuum-intent/src/observers.rs` / `tests/observers_group.rs` alone despite also quoting the old sentence — that's bn-32kpe's fenced territory (RFC 0037/continuum-intent); flagged below.\n\n## Pinning-test moves\n\nNone. No Rust test `include_str!`s or otherwise pins `semantic-diff.example.json`'s bytes (unlike the W7 precedent for `intent-contract.example.json`); `continuum-semantic-diff`'s tests build fixtures inline. Nothing to flip.\n\n## Counts and gates\n\n- `cargo fmt --all --check`: clean.\n- `cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- `cargo test -p continuum-semantic-diff --locked`: 29/29 (18 unit + 11 evidence), 0 failed — unchanged from bn-1sdp's landing, no test added/removed.\n- `cargo test --workspace --locked`: 2,269 passed, 0 failed. (Dossier's \"baseline 2,261\" predates several intervening bones landing on trunk before this workspace forked; this bone adds zero tests, so its own before/after is identical by construction — I touched no `#[test]` fn.)\n- `just check`: exit 0.\n- `generate_traceability.py` + `validate_dossier.py`: both clean, `\"status\": \"pass\"`, 19 schema pairs, spec-debt open list empty, traceability regen produced no diff.\n- `validation-results.json`: untouched (confirmed via `git status`).\n\n## Files / commit\n\n- `notes/plan/rfcs/0031-semantic-and-intent-diff.md` — observers sentence rewritten; corrections 14, 15 added.\n- `notes/plan/schemas/examples/semantic-diff.example.json` — `after_intent` → `in_ack_v2`; new `bounds`/`expanded` record; `policy.reasons` rewritten.\n- `crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/observers.rs` — module-doc-only.\n- Commit `019466d` in the `bn-13nlo` workspace.\n\n## Concerns\n\n1. `crates/continuum-intent/src/observers.rs` (~L16-24) and `tests/observers_group.rs` (~L10) still quote the pre-correction (already-truncated — they drop the \"blocks... regardless\" clause) RFC sentence verbatim. Now stale. Left untouched: bn-32kpe's fenced territory, not mine.\n2. RFC 0031's F1 flag is stale (already paid by bn-1lfek's schema sweep) — already routed to bn-3l5la, just noting adjacency.\n3. The minted `bounds`/`expanded` change has no paired numeric detail anywhere (the diff schema carries no raw before/after values, and a full `in_ack_v2` contract fixture was out of fence and not ACs-required). Ready for a future bone to reference if one materializes `in_ack_v2`."}	blake3:TIXRxXotiI3y63FB_m-s2c6ygjT6OPI0Xk0cx5tD13o
1785725625958406	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEylQo		item.comment	bn-32kpe	{"body":"DECISION: (b) — the two-call contract is RATIFIED. RFC 0037 gains \"Acceptance obligations that are not well-formedness\" (AO1–AO4), correction 17, flag F12, four rejected alternatives, and an acceptance-suite bullet. check() keeps exactly five rules (W2, W3, W7, W8, W9) and gains none.\n\nTHE WEIGHING, in the order the arguments actually fell.\n\n(c) DIED FIRST, and killing it is what decided the bone. The candidate narrow W-rule — flag the incoherent pair (policy.non_vacuity = no-removal while optimization.non_vacuity = []) and leave bare emptiness to acceptance policy — does not survive generalization. `no-removal` is admissible on four fields (faults, non_vacuity, optimization, security_policy), and the corpus's own die-hard fixture carries the identical pair TWICE: `faults: no-removal` over `enabled: []` + `profiles: []`, and `optimization: no-removal` over empty hard/soft. Those are ordinary dormant policy — a standing statement about whatever the set comes to hold — not defects, and no principle distinguishes them from the non_vacuity case. So what actually bothers us is the empty SET, not the pair; the pair is only how bn-2z0b noticed it. Worse, the narrow rule is evadable in the wrong direction: an author who writes `non_vacuity: unlocked` beside an empty set passes it, so the rule would reward removing the lock along with the obligation. Recorded as a rejected alternative and pinned by a new corpus test.\n\nThe W7 ANALOGY, which optimization.rs raised and this bone had to adjudicate, does not hold. A `review` verb naming no reviewer is a block that FIRES on the first change and CANNOT BE DISCHARGED — a live contradiction in the policy table alone. `no-removal` over an empty set is a block with NOTHING TO GUARD YET — dormant, and it becomes live the moment a member is added. Different defects; only the first is ill-formedness. The flag is answered rather than left standing.\n\n(a) LOST ON TWO INDEPENDENT GROUNDS, neither of which is blast radius (there are NO production callers of check() today — grep: only tests and contract.rs's own unit tests; the daemon's intent family never calls it — so a flip would have been cheap). First, SCOPE: INV-012 is a synthesis obligation — \"Forge objectives include required progress/availability behaviors\" (plan.md), \"Every synthesis task includes positive behaviors or progress scenarios\" (plan §14.5) — and whether a contract will govern a synthesis task is not a fact the DOCUMENT carries. A verification-only contract that requires no behavior to remain possible is weak, not malformed. Second, PROXY WEAKNESS, which is the one that decides it: the obligation is a PRESENCE COUNT over opaque strings classified by membership (RFC 0031), and the file's own boundary_the_guard_counts_behaviors_it_does_not_judge_their_content already pins that \"x\"/\"true\"/\"noop\"/\"placeholder\" discharge it. Putting that in a surface whose other four rules are exact would make the verdict CLAIM non-vacuity on the strength of a one-character string. The trap moves; it does not close. docs/50 cuts the same way on inspection: its Intent-attacks list is (weaken property, strengthen assumptions/fairness, shrink bounds, remove faults, coarsen observer, lower assurance) — vacuity is NOT there; \"disable progress\" sits under Overfitting attacks, and \"non-vacuity/positive scenarios\" is a DEFENSE listed SEPARATELY FROM \"protected Intent Contract\". docs/50 itself treats them as two layers.\n\n(d) CONSIDERED AND REJECTED, the strongest form of (a) and not in the bone's frame: a W-rule conditioned on claim kinds (all-safety claims MUST declare a non-vacuity behavior). It dodges the scope objection and dies on soundness — claims[].kind is authored metadata this RFC deliberately does not derive from the expression (correction 7: a kind change with an unchanged expression classifies `incomparable`), so a safety claim labelled `liveness` discharges it, and the rule would put a safety gate on an unverified label. The sound version — deciding whether the contract's claims are satisfiable by a system with no behaviors — needs the fragment's decision procedure, which S2/S3 keep out of the cross-field checker.\n\nTHE COMPROMISE ALSO REJECTED, and recorded: an opt-in CheckEnvironment fact (\"this lane assembles synthesis\"). It looks natural — CheckEnvironment already carries the outside facts W8 and W9 need — but it is exactly the \"skip this rule\" spelling the surface's own documentation says it does not have. Always-on is (a); opt-in makes the verdict's meaning depend on how it was requested and weakens the four unconditional rules. AO3 forbids it by name.\n\n(b)'S COST — the trap stays loaded — IS PAID, not waved: AO3 states the rule normatively; contract.rs states it at three levels (module \"What a verdict does not decide\", check()'s doc, is_well_formed()'s doc — \"a caller that treats this as full acceptance accepts a contract that requires no behavior to remain possible\"); and a new test makes the surface's own rendering (\"the contract satisfies W2, W3, W7, W8, and W9\") a tripwire, so a sixth rule joining or a rule leaving lands the next reader on this reasoning instead of drifting."}	blake3:KiZY-OLKjy73bxlZqBMBn6iqJGxIQ6y3pipeeTfMwx8
1785725625998934	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEylgo		item.comment	bn-32kpe	{"body":"(bn-32kpe, part 2 — what landed.)\n\nRFC TEXT ADDED. §Well-formedness preamble gains one sentence pointing at the new section. New \"### Acceptance obligations that are not well-formedness\" with AO1 (an empty non_vacuity set is well formed; no W-rule rejects it and a checker MUST NOT add one), AO2 (the caller with standing is the lane assembling a synthesis task; it MUST require non-emptiness and refuse with a typed refusal naming INV-012 — and a lane that assembles no synthesis task MUST NOT impose it), AO3 (a verdict is not an acceptance decision; no per-caller flag; and the obligation is never discharged by a count — judging content is Forge's, plan §14.5/RFC 0033), AO4 (a dormant verb is not an ill-formed one; W1–W10 / AO1–AO2 / P1–P7 answer three questions about three objects — the document, the task, the revision). Correction 17 records the disposition, quotes the flag it answers, and states the direction. F12 records honestly that NO landed lane discharges the obligation (continuum-forge is still a PR-1/IMPL-01 scaffold; nothing outside tests calls require_non_vacuity) and hands the surface to RFC 0033. Four \"Rejected alternatives\" bullets. One acceptance-suite bullet.\n\nTEST MOVES. crates/continuum-intent/tests/inv012_nonvacuity_evidence.rs only; NO test in any other file or crate touched. negative_the_cross_field_checker_alone_would_accept_the_disabled_contract RENAMED to ratified_the_cross_field_checker_decides_five_rules_and_non_vacuity_is_the_second_call (it asserted an OPEN concern; it now asserts the ratified contract), same assertions plus the positive control. THREE NEW: ratified_a_vacuous_contract_can_be_wholly_well_formed_and_the_verdict_names_the_rules_it_decided (the sharp form the old test could not make — the die-hard fixture always fails W9, so it compared two one-finding verdicts; the dossier's validated example is clean on all five, and emptying its non_vacuity leaves it clean, verdict byte-equal, rendering pinned); ratified_a_directional_verb_over_an_empty_set_is_dormant_not_ill_formed (AO4's corpus evidence — the two dormant no-removal verbs in die-hard, checked against the real document rather than asserted, since the whole argument rests on that shape being ordinary); the_disposition_this_file_pins_is_the_one_rfc_0037_records (reads RFC 0037 at compile time and proves each quoted anchor is present AND capable of going missing, so a withdrawn disposition breaks the tests that rest on it). The file's \"src/ is untouched\" house rule was FALSE after this bone and is corrected in place to say what each bone changed.\n\nCODE. Documentation only — no rule added or removed, no behavior change, no schema touched, no epoch moved. src/optimization.rs: the flag paragraph becomes \"# The two-call contract, and the flag this module used to raise\", quoting AO1/AO2 and keeping the W7 reasoning as the record of why it lost; require_non_vacuity()'s doc gains the ratification. src/contract.rs: module \"## What a verdict does not decide\", check()'s doc (\"These five are the whole surface\"), is_well_formed()'s doc.\n\nCOUNTS. continuum-intent 435 -> 438 (+3, all new; the fourth change is a rename). Workspace 2,269 -> 2,272. NOTE: the brief's baseline of 2,261 is the trunk's; this workspace's base commit (26a8ae4) is ahead of it, and I measured 2,269 by stashing rather than assuming.\n\nGATES. cargo fmt --all --check clean; cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -D warnings clean (workspace); cargo test -p continuum-intent --locked green (438); cargo test --workspace --locked green (2,272); full `just check` exit 0 (boundaries, kernel covenant, governance, dossier — program_status ready — and Lean). Traceability regenerated: NO diff. notes/plan/validation-results.json untouched. Commit 356e564 in .maw/workspaces/bn-32kpe, NOT merged, NOT pushed, no .bones/ in the workspace commit. No caller test moved, because there is no caller.\n\nCONCERNS. (1) F12 is the real residual: the obligation is implemented, ratified, and CALLED BY NOTHING outside tests. (b) is only as strong as the lane that will make the second call, and that lane (RFC 0033 / continuum-forge) is a scaffold. If Forge lands without wiring require_non_vacuity, this bone will have written the rule that nobody runs — the flag exists so the next reader sees that, but a successor scoped to \"the Forge lane makes the second call\" is the honest follow-up. (2) AO2 says the lane MUST refuse with \"a typed refusal naming INV-012\" and does NOT name a wire error code, because RFC 0033/RFC 0026 own that surface and inventing one here would be the vocabulary drift the flag discipline forbids. (3) The sharp new test supplies the schema example's declared handle as the minted one (the literal \"in_ack_v1\", exactly as pr4_exit_evidence does) so W9 is held fixed across the mutation; that is deliberate variable isolation and is stated in the helper's doc, but it does mean the mutated bytes are checked against a handle they would not mint. (4) AO4's \"dormant is not ill-formed\" reading legitimizes `faults: no-removal` over an empty fault model. I believe it (the verb governs what the set comes to hold), but a reviewer who thinks a verb should be denied where it can never fire would have to take that up as a general rule across all four no-removal fields — not as a non_vacuity special case, which is precisely what this bone rejected. (5) The die-hard fixture's policy table is now load-bearing for a recorded RFC argument; editing those two verbs would break ratified_a_directional_verb_over_an_empty_set_is_dormant_not_ill_formed, which is intended but means the fixture is harder to change than it was."}	blake3:kalhkYNL6oY3DjfhKwOlmwX8AKHDTNXfl-YyMAamk08
1785725657904309	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEylwo		item.comment	bn-13nlo	{"body":"Lead: reviewed and merged @ epoch 2bc1d036. Both repairs ratified: (1) example minted in_ack_v2 with a genuine bounds/expanded record — the unchanged-shortcut MUST now holds, decision stays allow so docs/41 narrative intact; (2) observers sentence rewritten to the exception-free componentwise rule — mixed movement is incomparable, which blocks unconditionally under P2 (stronger than the bare coarsened the old sentence promised), matching the reading bn-1sdp implemented. Corrections 14/15 recorded in RFC 0031 revision history. Doc-only + example-only; no pins flipped; 2,269 tests green in-workspace."}	blake3:-mRnUBFpTT4zW-LJ-2s9bcawS_OS0CmWxbnfME2mWGw
1785725657938583	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymAo		item.move	bn-13nlo	{"state":"done"}	blake3:CRWrXKjSyVpMoyn9Phd_8wRLbwkToLsYwZOwzGr_sEA
1785725788906774	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymQo		item.create	bn-1dsih	{"description":"RFC 0037 correction 17 ratified the two-call contract (bn-32kpe): IntentContract::check() decides the document's five cross-field rules; the lane that assembles a synthesis task MUST additionally call Optimization::require_non_vacuity and refuse the task with a typed refusal naming INV-012 when optimization.non_vacuity is empty. Flag F12 records the residual: continuum-forge is a PR-1/IMPL-01 scaffold, so nothing outside tests makes the second call today. This bone is the discharge: when the Forge task-assembly lane lands (PR 22 surface, RFC 0033 owns the wire refusal), it makes the second call, and the AO2 obligation gains its production caller. Flip the inv012_nonvacuity_evidence.rs no-production-caller observation when done.","kind":"task","title":"Forge synthesis lane makes the non-vacuity second call (RFC 0037 F12)"}	blake3:3uZMNKN90TytuRWJkHGA3NSs6QHbgTG43tDyQxs9NpM
1785725792134156	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymgo		item.update	bn-1dsih	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:inv-012"}}	blake3:x-aqjY3dJzrypqgaxhg-EC-D8RRqUrTpgs5w0NAr_H0
1785725808505190	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEymwo		item.comment	bn-32kpe	{"body":"Lead: decision (b) RATIFIED and merged @ epoch 5a56c6ff. The reasoning that carried it: (c) died on the corpus's own evidence — die-hard-contract.json holds the identical no-removal-over-empty dormant shape twice (faults, optimization), so a non_vacuity-only rule names INV-012's concern by governance coincidence, and the unlocked-beside-empty evasion rewards removing the lock with the obligation. The W7 disanalogy is exact: undischargeable-on-first-change vs dormant-until-member. (a) died on scope (INV-012 is synthesis-scoped by its own text; the document doesn't carry whether it will govern synthesis) and on proxy weakness (a presence count over opaque strings that a one-char string discharges would make an exact surface lie). AO3 correctly forbids the per-caller-flag compromise. F12 residual routed to successor bn-1dsih (req:inv-012): the Forge task-assembly lane makes the second call when it lands. Concerns 2-5 noted and accepted as recorded."}	blake3:kpzBKzVdzUiU0x4BupsnZyVEGeshhz8o4l7uWk09NBU
1785725808543283	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEynAo		item.move	bn-32kpe	{"state":"done"}	blake3:pIbVB5vwuYE3taBUxzXo7nuHH9rSxJjVUP6NrvDLW8U
1785726196293030	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEynQo		item.comment	bn-10wdo	{"body":"bn-10wdo — three dispositions, decided against RFC 0026's own text, no wire byte changed.\n\n## Concern 1 — LineageError::Unknown: CapabilityDenied vs StaleSnapshot\n\n`check_current` returns `Stale` (a provable earlier lineage point) or `Unknown` (the Fork's\n3-identity memory can't place it, \"never a false claim the identity was never part of any\nlineage\"). `daemon::workspace`'s `lineage_fault` maps `Unknown` -> `CapabilityDenied`\n(workspace.fork/seal); `daemon::task::resume`/`daemon::verification::start` collapse both arms\nto `StaleSnapshot`.\n\nInvestigated whether this is principled: in every call site, including workspace.fork/seal, the\nidentity check_current sees is *already* resolved via `state.workspace(handle).ok_or_else(\nFault::denied)` one line earlier — never foreign or out-of-scope by the time Unknown is reached.\n\"You named a thing you cannot see\" isn't literally what's happening anywhere. The real\ndistinction is call *shape*: workspace's two ops check a caller-declared `expected_head`\nimmediately before spending a **write** on it (a compare-and-set guard — X2 genuinely applies to\na token about to authorize a mutation); task.resume/verification.start check an already-pinned\nidentity before a **read/run**, where the only open question is currency, matching RFC 0026's\nown StaleSnapshot definition (\"not current, or not sealed\").\n\nDisposition: ratified as two rules keyed to call shape, not aligned — attack 7\n(dx03_falsification.rs) already exercises both codes for the identical scenario in one test run,\ndeliberately, so aligning either direction moves a pinned wire byte. Written into RFC 0026, new\nsubsection \"An unplaceable lineage identity: one condition, two codes, and why that is a\ndecision\" (after \"Semantic separations that MUST NOT be blurred\"), with the forward-looking rule\nfor future operations. Cross-referenced from workspace.rs's module doc and both\n`check_current(...)` call sites. Doc-only; no code changed.\n\n## Concern 2 — Continuation::bounds/frontier unread on resume\n\nConfirmed: `task::resume` never reads `continuation.bounds`/`frontier`; it re-derives the bound\nfrom `TaskEntry::bounds()`, and `bfs::explore` has no partial-state entry, so resume re-explores\nthe whole model from initial states under the new bound. RFC 0030 settles the semantics: \"the\ncommitted frontier is part of *output identity*\" and \"frontier inclusion is checkable ... without\nre-running the engine\" — a claim about the *produced artifact*, not an engine-seeding\ninstruction.\n\nDisposition, written into RFC 0026 as \"What a `cont_*` handle means, and what `bounds`/`frontier`\nare pinned for\" (end of \"Continuations and resume admissibility\"): a `cont_*` means \"advance this\ntask\", not \"resume from this point\"; bounds/frontier are pinned *provenance* (RFC 0030's budget\nrule), checked against, not fed into, the engine. Sound today only because canonical BFS\ndeterministically makes a larger bound's reachable set a superset of a smaller bound's — stronger\nthan inclusion requires — an accident of the one engine, not the wire contract or\n`Continuation`'s type. A future engine (real partial resume, non-canonical order, a portfolio\nsolver) could lose this silently while the fields stay unread — the concern's silent-break.\n\n**Guard added**: `daemon::verification::run` takes `prior_frontier: &[State]` and\n`debug_assert!`s every parked-frontier state is in the resumed exploration's reachable set\n(`Reachable::contains`). Threaded from `task::resume` (holds the old `Continuation` locally)\nthrough `advance` -> `run_in` -> `run`; `&[]` on a fresh `verification.start`. `debug_assert`, not\na release-mode refusal, because today's single engine makes this re-derivable rather than a live\nrisk; RFC text states a future non-BFS binding is where it becomes release-mode, not removed.\n\nVerified live, not dead code: a temporary `#[should_panic]` fixture called `run()` directly with\na well-formed but genuinely-unreached Die Hard state (`[5,3]`, 1-state bound) as `prior_frontier`\n— confirmed the panic — then removed before committing. Attack 16 (resume of a superseded\ncontinuation) now exercises this guard on the actual production path; passed unchanged.\n\n## Concern 3 — task.resume's local decision table vs code\n\nThe doc had one row: \"pinned snapshot not held, not sealed, or superseded -> StaleSnapshot\". The\ncode: `state.workspace(&continuation.snapshot).ok_or_else(Fault::denied)?` returns\nCapabilityDenied for \"not held at all\", distinct from `stale()` for \"not sealed\"/\"superseded\".\n\nRFC 0026's own StaleSnapshot definition (\"not current, or not sealed where required\")\npresupposes a *named*, resolvable snapshot — it doesn't cover \"not held at all\", which is X2's\n\"a handle this daemon does not hold is CapabilityDenied, not a not-found\", the same rule\nworkspace.rs's own doc already states family-wide. The code was right; the doc's elaboration\n(folding three conditions into one row) was wrong.\n\nDisposition: aligned DOC to CODE. Split into \"not held at all -> CapabilityDenied\" and \"held but\nnot sealed, or superseded -> StaleSnapshot\", noting the correction and that the path is\nunreachable via wire today (no operation evicts a WorkspaceRecord a continuation has pinned) —\nno wire audit exercised either reading; doc-only.\n\n## What moved\n\n- Doc only: RFC 0026 (two new subsections, ~110 lines), workspace/task/verification module and\n  function docs (cross-refs), dx03_falsification.rs module doc + attack 7/16 doc comments + two\n  assertion messages (concerns 1 & 2's citations).\n- Code, no wire change: verification::run/run_in/advance gained `prior_frontier: &[State]` and\n  one `debug_assert!` (concern 2); the two call sites updated to match.\n- Concerns 1 & 3: documentation only, no LineageError mapping/error code/wire byte moved.\n\n## Tests, gates\n\nPre-existing tests unchanged except dx03_falsification.rs's comments/messages citing these\nconcerns — no assertion weakened or expected value changed (attack 7's codes and attack 16's\nadmission-not-refusal are byte-identical to before). validation-results.json untouched.\n\n2,269 tests pass. `cargo fmt --all --check` clean, `cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D\nwarnings` clean workspace-wide, `cargo test -p continuumd --locked` all green, full `just check`\n(fmt-check, lint, test, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier, lean) exits 0. `notes/plan`'s\ndossier validator reports `\"status\": \"pass\"` after the RFC 0026 edits.\n\nCommitted in-workspace as `fix: refusal-surface and continuation-semantics dispositions\n(bn-10wdo)` (46add07, .maw/workspaces/bn-10wdo). Not merged, not pushed. Bone not closed."}	blake3:nwIrJOc8oCx7FPVeSUCZdBszWwRH5pxjlUYNT_WZLVk
1785726265677980	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyngo		item.comment	bn-10wdo	{"body":"Lead: all three dispositions ratified and merged @ epoch 7c1ff1e. (1) Unknown-lineage two-code split ratified as two rules keyed to call shape — CAS-guard-before-write answers CapabilityDenied (X2), eligibility-check-on-already-resolved-identity answers StaleSnapshot; the rule for future operations is now stated in RFC 0026 instead of left to rediscovery, and attack 7 already pins both codes in one run. (2) cont_*-means-advance: bounds/frontier are pinned provenance per RFC 0030's budget rule; the superset property that makes this sound is now CHECKED via the prior_frontier debug_assert in verification::run (verified to fire against a genuinely-unreached state before commit), with the promote-to-release-refusal condition recorded for any future non-BFS engine binding. (3) resume decision-table row split doc-aligned to code (not-held → CapabilityDenied, held-but-stale → StaleSnapshot), path wire-unreachable today. No wire byte changed — no F-flag per the wire-visibility rule. 2,269 tests green in-workspace."}	blake3:q717GRhEjSVlveFTBTFN8H-leFhb3K1YluDCxk8KwP0
