1785868556022844	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyuAo		item.comment	bn-ycn6	{"body":"PR-12/IMPL-04 delivered: crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/bounds.rs (classify_bounds, BoundsChange) + crates/continuum-semantic-diff/tests/pr12_impl04_bound_change_evidence.rs. lib.rs edit kept to one `pub mod bounds;` line plus one doc sentence, no reflow.\n\nDESIGN: continuum-intent's bounds.rs already implements the componentwise order (Bounds::relation_to -> BoundsRelation, with its own tests pinning the RFC text). classify_bounds() does not reimplement that logic — duplicating an RFC rule already correctly implemented is itself a hazard — it calls Bounds::relation_to and applies a total, no-wildcard 1:1 name-preserving map (to_relation) from BoundsRelation's five variants onto change_policy::Relation's five same-named tokens. The crate is closed over Relation only; BoundsRelation never leaves the module.\n\nCOMPONENTWISE RULES, with governing quotes:\n- Order: componentwise partial order on (values, nodes, faults, depth). \"null denotes unbounded and is the top of that component's order, so n -> null contributes an increase and null -> n contributes a decrease. An absent values or depth key MUST be read as null.\" (RFC 0031, \"bounds\"; RFC 0037 \"Absence, null, and defaults\" states the same for the contract shape.)\n- Directional relations are `expanded`/`contracted`, never `shrunk`/`weakened`/`strengthened` — RFC 0031 correction 2 fixes this naming explicitly against plan §5.3's informal \"bound increase/decrease.\" \"A decrease in any component with no increase elsewhere is `contracted`; an increase in any component with no decrease elsewhere is `expanded`.\"\n- Equality: `unchanged` iff no component moved (all four canonical-equal) — R2's equality-of-canonical-encoding rule, same discipline as observers.rs.\n- Mixed movement (growth in one component, shrinkage in another, same or different component): \"mixed movement is `incomparable`\" — no exception, stated identically in both the \"bounds\" section and the \"Classification lattice\" summary bullet.\n- Declaredness (`unknown`, fails closed unconditionally): \"An absent `nodes` or `faults` key has no null form and no schema default; a change in the declaredness of either MUST classify `bounds` as `unknown` and fail closed.\" This overrides every other component's movement — verified by a dedicated test (declaredness_change_is_unknown_even_when_every_other_component_grows) where every other component grows and the result is still Unknown, matching continuum-intent's own `Bounds::relation_to` behavior and its comment \"does not let an increase elsewhere excuse a declaredness change.\"\n- `unsupported`: RFC 0031's field table row for bounds lists only {unchanged, expanded, contracted, incomparable, unknown} (unsupported absent); it becomes admissible only via correction 13's blanket non-affirmative grant (\"the non-affirmative three are admissible on every row\"), not via the row itself. classify_bounds() never emits it: unsupported is RFC 0031's answer for a unit whose *fragment* (scope.fragments) doesn't cover it, and `bounds` carries no `fragment` member in intent-contract.schema.json at all (confirmed by a live-schema test, the_live_schema_carries_no_fragment_member_on_bounds, mirroring observers.rs's identical proof for the same absence) — there is no fragment for a bounds comparison to fall outside of.\n- One unit, not a keyed set: bounds has no per-item key (RFC 0031's \"Unit of classification\" = \"the tuple\" itself; semantic-diff.schema.json's own $comment lists bounds among the four whole-field units with \"no sub-unit to name\"). So classify_bounds() is total and single-valued: no added/removed case, exactly one BoundsChange per call.\n\nEVIDENCE: tests/pr12_impl04_bound_change_evidence.rs, 13 tests — real-corpus positive (fixture nodes 3->2 classifies Contracted, closes into PolicyTable::verdict under the fixture's own bounds verb no-decrease -> Block), two adversarial disguised-shrink sweeps (simultaneous shrink+grow across components, both directions of which component \"looks like\" the headline move -> always Incomparable, never Expanded/Contracted/Unchanged), declaredness-change -> Unknown -> fails closed (three variants: isolated, with every other component growing, and closed into verdict under both no-decrease (-> Review) and a synthetic locked verb (-> Block), plus the P2-unconditional case under all_unlocked (-> Review)), two anti-vacuity mutants each asserted in both directions (mutant_blind_to_declaredness treats Undeclared as 0 and would read a fail-closed case as Expanded forward / Contracted backward; mutant_by_total_magnitude nets component totals and would read a genuine mixed movement as a clean Expanded forward / Contracted backward), plus baseline and boundary tests. src/bounds.rs itself carries 15 unit tests (5 componentwise+declaredness outcomes, admissibility, record-conversion, live-schema fragment-absence check). Total for the crate: 53 passed, 0 failed (29 src unit + 13 evidence + 11 pre-existing pr12_impl05 evidence + 0 doctests).\n\nGATES (all exit 0): cargo fmt --all --check; cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings (workspace-wide, zero warnings); cargo test -p continuum-semantic-diff --locked (53 passed, 0 failed); full `just check` (fmt-check, lint, test, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier, lean — all pass, Lean axiom manifest up to date).\n\nRFC GAP RECORDED FOR THE LEAD: none found specific to bounds beyond what's already recorded as correction 13 (non-affirmative-admissible-on-every-row) and RFC 0037 correction 15 (absence-as-null, citing bounds.values/bounds.depth as already-settled precedent) — both pre-existing, already-landed corrections, not new gaps. The bounds text itself reads as internally consistent between the \"bounds\" section and the \"Classification lattice\" summary (unlike the observers section pre-bn-13nlo). One minor observation, not a contradiction: RFC 0031's field-by-field table (the five-column table under \"Field-by-field classification rules\") lists bounds's admissible relations as exactly {unchanged, expanded, contracted, incomparable, unknown}, omitting unsupported even though bounds is not one of the five rows the correction-13 paragraph names as having omitted \"the non-affirmative relations\" (that paragraph names faults/assurance/optimization/non_vacuity/security_policy specifically) — bounds sits in between (two of three non-affirmatives already listed, one omitted). Correction 13's closing sentence (\"Read every row's admissible set as its listed relations plus incomparable, unsupported, and unknown\") already covers this reading generically, so no action needed, but a future editing pass tightening the correction's scope to name bounds/trust_boundaries (which have the identical partial-listing pattern) explicitly rather than leaving it to the generic closing sentence would remove the need to infer it.\n\nWorkspace: .maw/workspaces/bn-ycn6, committed (feat: bound-change classification for the intent semantic diff (bn-ycn6)). Bone left in doing state; no merge/close/push performed."}	blake3:8IleGxjTvLaKBkfXLHIFEvBNKJVCNYNyo3bZ3x1LgM4
1785868602576560	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyuQo		item.comment	bn-ycn6	{"body":"Lead: reviewed and merged @ epoch 66363bd. The right structural move: classify_bounds wraps continuum-intent's own Bounds::relation_to (which already carries the componentwise order) with a total, no-wildcard, name-preserving map onto change_policy::Relation — one authority, no duplicated order. Rules ratified: null=unbounded=top, absence≡null for values/depth, expanded/contracted only (correction 2), mixed movement incomparable with no exception, nodes/faults declaredness→unknown unconditionally, unsupported admissible-but-never-emitted (no fragment member, live-schema pinned per the observers proof). The bounds/trust_boundaries table-row tightening observation noted — editorial, riding the next RFC 0031 pass, not boned. 53 semantic-diff tests green."}	blake3:-VZFjgX8R3h0Javuw5-f5HxVk6OjVJ26lJ3gkychWho
1785868602609667	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyugo		item.move	bn-ycn6	{"state":"done"}	blake3:NR8vMbH1I9UvrKsXFBVPLxVM9eZyc6GO9V85BdNTgJU
1785869708870913	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyuwo		item.comment	bn-38p2	{"body":"PR-11/IMPL-06 landed in .maw/workspaces/bn-38p2 (85b4c22), both halves.\n\nPACKER (crates/continuum-context/src/budget.rs, new). RFC 0028's second budget branch: an over-budget expansion now publishes a smaller child instead of refusing. Selection is a PREFIX of the declared item order (SelectedItem's canonical order — there is no stage-9 ranker in this workspace, and RFC 0028 names 'plain causal slice + expansion, stage 9 disabled' as the lane's own fallback, so the packer says so rather than inventing a utility order); every dropped candidate is recorded under 'budget', expandable, naming the query that retrieves it; the whole disposition runs through bn-28jj's closed accounting, so |selected| + Sum(counts) = candidate set holds by construction and ClosedAccounting::reconcile re-checks it from the two published halves before a document is written. The UNPACKED path goes through the ledger too, so fitting the budget is not a way around the accounting. continuumd/src/daemon/context.rs calls it and projects the PACKED manifest onto the envelope.\n\nWHICH BRANCH IS WHICH (the row reads two ways; two other RFC sentences settle it). 'Expansion is not paginated. […] an expansion too large for the budget is packed and its remainder recorded in the manifest, which is the same mechanism as any other pack' + rejected alternative 'The answer is a smaller pack with a larger manifest, OR BudgetExhausted with a continuation'. So a packed child is an ANSWER (status ok, shortfall visible as a budget omission on pack and envelope); BudgetExhausted is what is left when no conforming child exists.\n\nBOUNDARY: the smallest conforming child is the complete manifest beside an empty selection — 'the omission manifest, whose completeness is reserved before any optional content' and INV-007's 'never the thing a budget squeezes out'; shrinking past it would mean dropping a record or shrinking a count ('Counts are exact'). At that child's own measured size the answer is a pack selecting nothing that still accounts for every candidate; one byte below, nothing is published (typed non_resumable_reason, SD-13). The boundary is measured in the tests (binary search over published answers), never assumed.\n\nMEASUREMENT (correction 17). content_budget.bytes is now the child's own measured size in BOTH places (ChildPack::to_json; continuumd's ceiling() no longer writes the ceiling back). Reading fixed in pack.rs docs: 'measured' = byte length of the WHOLE published canonical encoding — the bytes a caller receives and the bytes ADR-0013 compares ('Two packs are the same pack iff their canonical encodings are byte-equal') — because that is the only reading under which 'Expansion accounting''s sum over a pack family is what the family cost (tested by summing a two-generation family against the documents' lengths). Self-reference closed like content_hash's: the published value is the LEAST solution of N = C + digits(N), reached by iterating from zero; least is pinned because at a decimal boundary two values are self-consistent and either would give one answer two encodings. ChildPack.budget_bytes renamed to ChildPack.ceiling, read only by admission; the daemon's fallback ceiling still reads the parent's recorded value, now with the reading that justifies it ('an expansion may not cost more than the pack it expands unless the caller asks for more').\n\nTOKENS: kept ABSENT. Advisory, model-relative, and the schema requires tokenizer_id beside them (F4); no tokenizer exists in-tree — the same refusal daemon::budget already makes for Cost.tokens. Tested as 'content_budget carries exactly one key'.\n\nSURPRISE WORTH FLAGGING: because 'budget is not key material', one question under two ceilings is ONE ctx_* and TWO documents, so a shortfall record's recoverable_by is the child's OWN handle (the same question with more room). That is admitted, not a defect: RFC 0028 requires the two to be frontier-ordered rather than conflicting (INV-009) and states determinism per (key, budget) — both asserted as tests (prefix inclusion + non-increasing counts, same ctx_*, different content_hash).\n\nPERFORMANCE: pack size is NOT monotone in items kept (admitting a kind's last item deletes a record worth more than the item), so the maximal prefix cannot be bisected for. Each candidate is encoded once, the document's fixed part once, and the scan runs over arithmetic (a prefix sum + <=11 exact counts); the chosen prefix is then built and measured for real, so the arithmetic chooses and never decides. Maximality is asserted against the real next child, which also pins the arithmetic to the encoder. 2,000-candidate linear fixtures in both test files.\n\nPINS: no protocol/IDL/schema change; no new operation, so landed-operation counts and INV-016 inventories do not move; bn-3tz60's CLI tests untouched (they state no ceiling). bn-28jj's a_budget_smaller_than_the_answer_refuses_rather_than_truncating is RENAMED a_budget_below_the_minimal_child_refuses_rather_than_truncating with assertions unchanged — 64 bytes is below that fixture's minimal child, so the pin did not need to flip; its comment now cites this bone and names the boundary.\n\nFILES: continuum-context/src/{budget.rs (new), pack.rs, lib.rs}; continuumd/src/daemon/context.rs; tests continuum-context/tests/pr11_impl06_byte_and_token_budgets.rs (10) and continuumd/tests/daemon_context_budget.rs (8, twelve-item fixture because bn-28jj's two-item one is too small to pack); +15 unit tests; START_HERE bullet marked delivered and traceability regenerated (PLAN_REQUIREMENTS.json PR-11-IMPL-06 active -> satisfied).\n\nGATES from the workspace root, all exit 0: cargo fmt --all --check; cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -D warnings; cargo test -p continuum-context (80 unit + 7 + 10 evidence + 1 doctest); cargo test -p continuumd; full just check. Workspace tests 2,304 -> 2,337 (+33). Bone left in doing; not merged, not pushed."}	blake3:DXenalkD6UYtlVI_LQCq-RI2bRPOfbMLzBt0In1Ho6I
1785869776572292	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvAo		item.comment	bn-3vxp	{"body":"PR-12/IMPL-06 delivered: fault/fairness/assurance classification in continuum-semantic-diff, following bn-1sdp's observers.rs precedent. Files: src/{faults,fairness,assurance}.rs (new), src/lib.rs (minimal: 3 `pub mod` lines + 1 doc bullet), tests/pr12_impl06_fault_fairness_assurance_evidence.rs (new, 14 tests).\n\nRULES PER FIELD:\n\n`faults` — pure set membership: \"Both are classified by membership: one record per class or profile added or removed. `removed` is the 'removing crash-after-submit' attack and is what `no-removal` blocks.\" (RFC 0031 \"`faults`\"). classify_faults visits FaultModel::units() union; Unchanged/Added/Removed only — no fragment on fault_model (schema-checked), membership always decidable, so Unknown/Unsupported/Incomparable never arise. AO4 (RFC 0037 correction 17, \"no-removal over an empty fault model is dormant, not ill-formed\"): no special case needed — empty-vs-empty visits the empty union and returns nothing, verdicting Allow on die-hard's dormant lock (test included).\n\n`fairness` — three movements, keyed by (kind, action) (W1): \"Membership: a constraint present on one side only is added or removed... `kind`: weak → strong on the same action classifies strengthened... (A kind change is equivalently expressible as one removed plus one added record; a classifier MUST choose one encoding and MUST NOT emit both.)... `condition`... antitone... `null` means unconditional and is the weakest condition, hence the strongest constraint: C → null classifies strengthened... incomparable/unknown pass through the duality unchanged.\" (RFC 0031 \"`fairness`\"). RFC 0031 Open Questions fixes single-record kind encoding as normative. Absence≡null for condition is settled text (\"An absent `condition` key MUST be read as `null`\", RFC 0037 correction 15 cites it as already-stated) — continuum-intent's FairnessConstraint::from_json already collapses absence/null to one `None`, so there's no separate declaredness case here (unlike bounds/assurance). classify_fairness: pass 1 = antitone condition rule per same key; pass 2 detects kind-flip (same action, complementary kinds, exactly one before-only + one after-only unit) and collapses to Strengthened/Weakened ONLY when conditions are structurally identical; a simultaneous kind+condition move falls back to removed+added (RFC-sanctioned alternate encoding) rather than guessing a combination rule — see RECORDED GAP #1.\n\nOracle gap: condition-content comparison (two unequal, both-declared conditions) needs Finite-fragment behavior-set inclusion, computed \"over CPNF-1 exactly as for claims\" per RFC 0031 — not implemented anywhere in continuum-intent yet (continuum_intent::property's own doc: \"Classification is not here... RFC 0031's question (PR 12)\"; bn-8mlg/IMPL-02 territory, still open). formula_relation classifies that case Unknown (S2 fail-closed), never guessed, never Incomparable (no double-refutation discharge exists here). Only equality and the null extremum (decidable by RFC 0031's own text) are computed without an oracle.\n\n`assurance` — total order, three relations, fully closed over continuum-intent: \"The classified unit is the requirement triple... Fail closed in both directions... `incomparable` MUST NOT be emitted for `assurance`.\" (RFC 0031 \"Assurance movement\"). Declaredness: \"a change in the declaredness of either [checker flag]... MUST classify assurance as unknown and fail closed\" (RFC 0037 \"Absence, null, and defaults\"; RFC 0031 correction 14 states the same). classify_assurance delegates entirely to AssurancePolicy::change_to, mapping CheckerDeclarednessChanged -> Unknown and Ok(AssuranceChange) -> Relation via Relation::from_wire(change.as_str()) — bridging vocabularies by wire spelling rather than importing continuum_value::assurance::AssuranceChange, so this crate's production deps stay exactly continuum-intent (one edge). evidence_class_changes (already in continuum-intent) covers accepted_evidence_classes membership; not re-wrapped. debug_assert_ne! + an exhaustive 2025-combination battery test pin that Incomparable is never constructed.\n\nRECORDED GAP #1 (RFC 0031 fairness text, a silence not a contradiction): the field table keys fairness by (kind, action) — so weak:A/strong:A are distinct units — yet the `kind` bullet treats weak→strong \"on the same action\" as one edit to one constraint, matching ACROSS keys by action alone, with no rule for when a same-action removed+added pair should NOT read as a kind change (condition also edited; an action legitimately carrying both kinds that drops one). fairness.rs resolves conservatively (identical-condition-only collapse); full writeup in its module doc, \"A recorded tension in RFC 0031's text\".\n\nRECORDED GAP #2 (continuum-intent, mechanically pinned, not fixed — read-only for this bone): correction 13 says \"incomparable excluded from `assurance` alone, per its own total-order rule\", but `PolicyField::admits_relation` is `!relation.is_affirmative() || classified_relations().contains(...)` with no field override, so `PolicyField::Assurance.admits_relation(Relation::Incomparable)` currently returns `true` — contradicting the parenthetical. Pinned live in assurance.rs (admits_relation_currently_over_admits_incomparable_on_assurance...). No live consequence here (classify_assurance never constructs Incomparable regardless); worth routing to whoever owns change_policy.rs.\n\nDESIGN NOTE: continuum-value is NOT a dependency anywhere (prod or dev) in the final Cargo.toml (diffs clean vs. pre-bone). Draft attempt added it under [dev-dependencies] only (boundary checker only *reports* dev edges) but tools/governance/check_code_policy.py's GOV-1-07 reads dev deps too and failed `just check` (dependency-rationale.toml's continuum-value used_by list didn't list this crate); removed the edge entirely rather than editing shared governance TOML. Tests build assurance levels via continuum_intent::assurance_policy::level_from_wire(\"validated\") etc.\n\nEVIDENCE (real corpus: replicated-register-contract.json — 6 fault classes+2 profiles, 1 fairness constraint, full assurance requirement; die-hard-contract.json for AO4): positive removal of \"crash\" -> Removed, Blocks under no-removal; positive kind-strengthen weak:Recover->strong:Recover -> Strengthened, Reviewed with named reviewer (W7); positive minimum validated->bounded -> Downgraded, Blocks under no-downgrade; AO4 dormant empty fault_model -> Allow; adversarial fault-class swap and fairness-action-rename never silent; assurance declaredness drop -> Unknown, Reviews (not Block, not Allow); pin sweep assurance never Incomparable (5 mutations); 3 anti-vacuity mutants both directions (cardinality-blind faults; checker-ignoring assurance, over-lenient; any-inequality-is-downgrade assurance, over-strict) each shown wrong on its target case.\n\nGATES (all exit 0, bn-3vxp workspace): cargo fmt --all --check clean; cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings (whole workspace) clean; cargo test -p continuum-semantic-diff --locked: 73 unit (12 faults/17 fairness/15 assurance/29 pre-existing) + 13+11+14 evidence = 111 tests, 0 failed; full `just check` (fmt/lint/test/boundaries/covenant/governance/dossier/lean) exit 0.\n\nCommitted in-workspace: \"feat: fault/fairness/assurance change classification for the intent semantic diff (bn-3vxp)\". No merge/close/push; bone left in doing state."}	blake3:Ncs_oUqqhzVsZX29t7GznjizwLsksK-s2476VgxVqg4
1785869817926355	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvQo		item.comment	bn-38p2	{"body":"Lead: reviewed and merged @ epoch 28f9a07. Ratifications: (1) branch semantics — packed child is an ANSWER (ok + budget omissions on pack and envelope), BudgetExhausted only below the minimal child, and the minimal child = complete manifest beside empty selection per INV-007's never-the-thing-a-budget-squeezes-out; boundary measured by binary search over published answers, not assumed. (2) measured bytes = whole published canonical encoding, self-reference closed as the LEAST solution of N = C + digits(N) with the decimal-boundary ambiguity pinned. (3) tokens honest-absent (exactly-one-key test) — no tokenizer in tree, F4 requires tokenizer_id beside tokens. (4) one-ctx_*-two-documents under budget-is-not-key-material ADMITTED with frontier-ordering (INV-009) and per-(key,budget) determinism both asserted — recoverable_by naming the child's own handle is the honest consequence, recorded not hidden. (5) non-monotone pack size: arithmetic chooses, real encoding decides, maximality asserted against the real next child. bn-28jj pin renamed with assertions unchanged, cited. Worker annotated its own bullet + regenerated traceability in-workspace — the convention future briefs should name explicitly. 2,337 workspace tests."}	blake3:aP8MyDcSh5sTnD54zkiHOPFAcNf0S7_mwKAGpvc_w5E
1785869817964554	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvgo		item.move	bn-38p2	{"state":"done"}	blake3:mDdc_NqLTQ7T5XFVxYpnDSOOKiLhyhtfMWvGl0C7GUc
1785869885115673	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyvwo		item.create	bn-1fik9	{"description":"bn-3vxp's fairness classifier found a genuine ambiguity in RFC 0031's fairness kind bullet: it matches units across the two sides by action alone (units are keyed by (kind, action) per W1, so a weak<->strong swap on one action changes the unit key), but states no rule for the case where the swap coincides with a condition edit. bn-3vxp resolved conservatively: a CLEAN weak<->strong swap on one action collapses into RFC 0031's fixed single-record encoding (Strengthened/Weakened); when the condition ALSO moved, it falls back to removed+added (never an affirmative single record). The resolution is documented in crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/fairness.rs. This bone dispositions the ambiguity in RFC 0031's own text (correction-ledger entry stating the rule, per the bn-13nlo repair precedent): either ratify bn-3vxp's conservative fallback or state a sharper rule and flip the classifier + tests with it.","kind":"task","title":"RFC 0031 fairness kind-bullet: no stated rule for weak/strong swap with simultaneous condition edit"}	blake3:XgTFOLW8h_GpQVxQO_V4tD9hyNnUGj6qmuoGbnIGzr8
1785869885305616	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywAo		item.create	bn-2sngz	{"description":"bn-3vxp found and mechanically pinned (live test in crates/continuum-semantic-diff, plus the classifier never constructs it): continuum-intent's PolicyField::admits_relation admits Incomparable on the assurance row, but RFC 0031 correction 13 excludes incomparable from assurance alone, per assurance's total-order rule ('the non-affirmative three are admissible on every row, with incomparable excluded from assurance alone'). Fix: tighten admits_relation's assurance row to refuse Incomparable, flip bn-3vxp's pin from divergence-record to regression guard citing this bone, and check no caller relied on the over-admission (assurance_policy.rs never produces Incomparable, so this should be consequence-free). Falsification-discipline shape: pin exists green, fix bone flips it.","kind":"task","title":"PolicyField::admits_relation over-admits Incomparable on assurance vs RFC 0031 correction 13"}	blake3:PyBt-_k2lOywNJnb8-MNijpII9XmXb_Yom27wXMEOEk
1785869895978649	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywQo		item.update	bn-1fik9	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-12"}}	blake3:NMonBP4FvbWs-FAdkcaFBzVKuTXQ-0F8ZdV-bPI-q04
1785869896010836	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEywgo		item.update	bn-1fik9	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"spec-debt"}}	blake3:GgN_qkz_r-_B89GaxamwNk-ysVetVVEnRASw_YAna6g
