1785870679573146	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyzQo		item.comment	bn-2sngz	{"body":"Fixed. `PolicyField::admits_relation` (crates/continuum-intent/src/change_policy.rs) now refuses `Incomparable` on `assurance`, per RFC 0031 correction 13's exact parenthetical: \"the non-affirmative three are admissible on every row (with `incomparable` excluded from `assurance` alone, per its own total-order rule)\" (notes/plan/rfcs/0031-semantic-and-intent-diff.md, \"Corrections recorded by this RFC\", item 13).\n\n**The fix.** `admits_relation` previously implemented the rule's general half unconditionally (`!relation.is_affirmative() || classified_relations().contains(&relation)`), so every non-affirmative relation short-circuited to `true` on every field before the field-specific set was consulted. It now special-cases the carve-out: affirmative relations go through `classified_relations()`; non-affirmative relations are admitted everywhere except `Incomparable` on `Assurance`. Doc comments on `admits_relation` and `ClassificationRecord::new` were rewritten to quote correction 13 and explain why `assurance`'s total order (three affirmative relations dispose of every comparable pair) leaves no \"both inclusions refuted\" case for `incomparable`, while `unknown` correctly stays admissible for the unrelated checker-flag declaredness case.\n\n**Pin flip, confirmed green before and after.** Ran `cargo test -p continuum-semantic-diff --locked admits_relation_currently_over_admits_incomparable_on_assurance` on frozen src first — green, confirming bn-3vxp's pin. Flipped it in assurance.rs: renamed to `admits_relation_now_refuses_incomparable_on_assurance_per_correction_13`, negated the assertion, and rewrote the doc comment recording the full cycle — found/pinned by bn-3vxp, closed by bn-2sngz — citing both bones and the old test name.\n\n**Caller sweep (every use of `admits_relation`):**\n- faults/bounds/fairness/observers.rs each call `admits_relation` only on their own field, never `Assurance` (grepped across `crates/`, confirmed). Untouched. fairness.rs is bn-1fik9's territory anyway.\n- assurance.rs's `AssuranceChange::new` also carries an independent `debug_assert_ne!(relation, Relation::Incomparable, ...)`, so tightening is redundant-safe there. `every_relation_this_module_can_produce_is_admissible_on_the_assurance_field` only tests `{Unchanged, Upgraded, Downgraded, Unknown}` — unaffected.\n- `ClassificationRecord` has no public field-literal constructor (private fields, `new()` is the only path), so `verdict()` could never have received an `Assurance`+`Incomparable` record pre-fix except via `new()` — the tightening is enforced once, at construction.\n- **Two continuum-intent integration tests DID rely on the over-admission** — the reliance the bone asked me to hunt for:\n  - `change_policy_contract.rs::p2_a_non_affirmative_relation_forbids_allow_on_every_field_under_every_verb` looped `Assurance × Incomparable` through `records_with()` → `ClassificationRecord::new(...).expect(...)`, which now panics on that cell. Fixed by skipping it (comment citing correction 13) and adding `p2_excludes_assurance_incomparable_per_correction_13_rather_than_silently_skipping_it`, asserting the skip is a typed `InadmissibleRelation` rejection, not an untested hole.\n  - `a_relation_the_fields_order_cannot_produce_is_a_typed_rejection` asserted `.is_ok()` for all fields × all three non-affirmative relations, including `Assurance × Incomparable` — now `Err`. Fixed with an explicit `assert_eq!(..., Err(InadmissibleRelation{...}))` for that pair and excluded it from the general loop.\n  - `change_policy_adversarial.rs` mentions `PolicyField::Assurance` once, for an unrelated `MissingField` case.\n- `assurance_policy.rs` / `continuum-value/src/assurance.rs`: grepped both for `Incomparable` — zero hits, confirmed consequence-free.\n\n**Applicability matrix / RFC 0037 — confirmed disjoint.** RFC 0037's \"Verb applicability\" table and the schema's `$defs/policy_verb_*` restrictions govern admissible *verbs*, a different table from `admits_relation`'s *relations*; `assurance`'s verb row is untouched. Checked the bone's aside that \"RFC 0037's correction 13 cites the same rule\": it doesn't. RFC 0037's own correction 13 is \"The 'Policy joins' illustration was narrower than its own definition\" (unrelated — `locked ⊔ v = locked`). RFC 0037 has no correction discussing the per-field admissible-*relation* table at all; that gap (and correction 13's fix) is RFC 0031's alone. RFC 0037 correction 14 covers a different rule (checker-flag declaredness → `unknown`). Flagging this for the lead — the bone's aside assumed a match that isn't there; not a defect, just worth a wording fix if repeated elsewhere.\n- `semantic-diff.schema.json`'s `relation` enum is flat (no per-field conditional); `intent-contract.schema.json` has no relation-admissibility construct (only the verb matrix again). No schema disagrees, and none could — the per-field relation table was never schema-structural, only RFC prose transcribed and checked in Rust.\n\n**Wire-visible reachability — confirmed.** Grepped `crates/continuumd/src/` for `PolicyTable`/`.verdict(`: `continuumd::daemon::intent` imports only `PolicyField`/`PolicyTable`/`PolicyVerb` (governance-table plumbing), never `Relation`/`ClassificationRecord`, never calls `PolicyTable::verdict`. The P1–P6 path this bone touches is exercised only by the intent/semantic-diff test suites today, not any live daemon request path — not wire-visible, beyond what was already stipulated.\n\n**Gates**, all in-workspace at `.maw/workspaces/bn-2sngz/`:\n- `cargo fmt --all --check` — exit 0, no output.\n- `cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings` — exit 0, no warnings, full workspace.\n- `cargo test -p continuum-intent --locked` — all green, 0 failed (144 unit + integration suites + 2 doctests).\n- `cargo test -p continuum-semantic-diff --locked` — all green, 0 failed (73 unit + integration suites).\n- `just check` (fmt/lint/test/boundaries/covenant/governance/dossier/lean) — exit 0: `\"program_status\": \"ready\"`, `\"status\": \"pass\"`; 195 `test result: ok` blocks summing to 2420 passed, 0 failed; Lean build all 19 jobs, axiom manifest clean (T0/T1 axiom-free).\n\n**Commit** (in-workspace, not merged): \"fix: admits_relation refuses Incomparable on assurance per RFC 0031 correction 13 (bn-2sngz)\" — 3 files (change_policy.rs, change_policy_contract.rs, assurance.rs), all inside the declared fence. No RFC or fairness.rs touched.\n\nTwo things for the lead: (1) the two continuum-intent tests that silently depended on the over-admission — production code (assurance.rs) was clean, but test code wasn't, so \"consequence-free\" needed the sweep rather than the assumption. (2) the bone's \"RFC 0037 correction 13\" aside doesn't hold as written — see above.\n\nLeaving the bone in `doing`. No merge/close/push performed."}	blake3:OYKjy2vpDEZb8FWkAq9VKYVMR7n_9TFUKwtqHzx60OM
1785870719967759	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyzgo		item.comment	bn-2sngz	{"body":"Lead: fix ratified and merged @ epoch 542cba3. admits_relation's blanket non-affirmative grant now carries correction 13's one carve-out (Assurance+Incomparable refused, typed InadmissibleRelation). The caller sweep earned its keep: two change_policy_contract.rs tests RELIED on the over-admission and were flipped to assert the typed rejection — this is why sweep-verify-don't-assume is in every fix brief. Pin flipped to regression guard with the found-by/fixed-by cycle recorded. Also accepted the correction to MY OWN bone description: the per-field admissible-relation table is RFC 0031's alone (RFC 0037's correction 13 is an unrelated policy-joins item) — good catch, the fix targeted the right authority. Nothing wire-visible reachable (continuumd doesn't call PolicyTable::verdict yet). 2,420 workspace tests green."}	blake3:DmyL9wjMdAbyOlmq9xtRU_d5010s5LLZMIYDOj6OCMM
1785870720001500	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyzwo		item.move	bn-2sngz	{"state":"done"}	blake3:O89oYf-2_HK3Rf9PgTvf6JG0JRkU1jgHPJzmXKDOC0A
1785870720345069	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0Ao		item.comment	bn-1fik9	{"body":"Disposition: RATIFIED bn-3vxp's conservative fallback (removed+added when kind swaps AND condition moves), per the lead's strong prior. Wrote it into RFC 0031's own text rather than leaving it a bone-comment argument, because the text does more than fall silent here — it actually supports the conservative reading once you trace the general rule.\n\nGoverning quotes (RFC 0031, \"Field-by-field classification rules\"):\n- Unit key: \"`fairness` | `fairness[]` | one constraint, keyed by (`kind`, `action`)\" — kind is part of the key, confirmed by RFC 0037 W1: \"no two `fairness` items may share a (`kind`, `action`) pair. RFC 0031 keys classification by exactly these.\"\n- General default: \"**One record per classified unit.** ... The classifier MUST emit one record per classified unit that is not `unchanged`, and MUST NOT collapse two units with different relations into one record.\"\n- The narrow exception: \"`kind`: `weak → strong` on the same action classifies `strengthened`... (A `kind` change is equivalently expressible as one `removed` plus one `added` record; a classifier MUST choose one encoding and MUST NOT emit both.)\"\n\nReading these three together: under the stated unit key, a weak:A -> strong:A swap is literally two different units (one removed, one added) by default, and the general rule forbids collapsing units with different relations into one record. The `kind` bullet carves out exactly one named exception to that default, phrased as \"equivalently expressible\" — i.e. the same semantic event, just re-encoded. That equivalence only holds when nothing but `kind` differs: a `strengthened`/`weakened` record carries one scalar direction, and once `condition` also moves, the `kind` axis and the `condition` axis can disagree in sign, so a single record can no longer state \"the same event\" without inventing a net direction the classification lattice never defines. A condition-coincident swap therefore falls outside the named exception and back under the general \"MUST NOT collapse\" default — i.e. removed+added is not merely the safe conservative guess, it is what the general rule already prescribes once the narrow exception's scope is read correctly. This is a real textual argument, not just silence-so-be-cautious, which is why I ratified rather than flagged for the lead.\n\nCorrection number used: 16 (RFC 0031's ledger held 15 corrections before this bone; 16 is the actual next number). Note: the bone brief said \"18 corrections so far\" — that did not match the file on disk (verified 15, matching both trunk and workspace before I started). Flagging the mismatch in case it signals a stale reference elsewhere, but proceeded with the file's real state.\n\nChanges made (all inside .maw/workspaces/bn-1fik9/, fence honored — change_policy.rs and bn-2sngz's test-file work untouched):\n- notes/plan/rfcs/0031-semantic-and-intent-diff.md: extended the `kind` bullet in-place to state the exception's scope (mirrors how correction 15 extended the `observers` section's own text, not just the ledger), plus new correction-ledger entry 16 stating it normatively with Normative:/Direction:.\n- crates/continuum-semantic-diff/src/fairness.rs: module doc's \"recorded tension\" section converted to a \"flag -> citation\" writeup quoting correction 16 verbatim, exactly the bn-32kpe/optimization.rs pattern; top summary blockquote of the `fairness` RFC section extended to stay a verbatim quote of the now-longer `kind` bullet; three remaining \"recorded tension\" cross-references (classify_fairness's doc comment, the inline fallback-branch comment, and the negative test's comment) updated to cite correction 16 instead of describing an open tension. No behavior change: classify_fairness, formula_relation, dualize, and all FairnessUnit/FairnessChange logic are untouched.\n\nGates, all exit 0:\n- cargo fmt --all --check: clean, no diff.\n- cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings: 0 warnings.\n- cargo test -p continuum-semantic-diff --locked: 111 passed, 0 failed (73 unit + 13 pr12_impl04 + 11 pr12_impl05 + 14 pr12_impl06 + 0 doc-tests).\n- just check (fmt-check, lint, full workspace test, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier, lean): exit 0. Full workspace test suite: 2419 passed, 0 failed across 195 test-result blocks; dossier validator \"status\": \"pass\"; lean build 19/19 jobs, axiom manifest up to date, every T0/T1 theorem axiom-free.\n\nCommitted in-workspace as 5347d9c: \"docs: RFC 0031 fairness kind-bullet disposition — conservative fallback ratified (bn-1fik9)\". Bone left in `doing`; no merge/close/push performed."}	blake3:fD83lLCso-Hv5PYoGyeqG6D0nvs9F90obigHyOa4xfU
1785870762829338	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0Qo		item.comment	bn-1fik9	{"body":"Lead: disposition RATIFIED and merged @ epoch 1aaf363. Better than ratify-the-safe-guess: the agent showed the conservative fallback is what RFC 0031's text ALREADY prescribes — the general one-record-per-unit rule governs by default, and the kind bullet's single-record exception ('equivalently expressible') is scoped to clean swaps only, because the Strengthened/Weakened record carries one scalar direction that a coincident condition move could contradict in sign. Correction 16 states the scope; fairness.rs flag→citation per the optimization.rs pattern. (My brief's 'correction ~19' was wrong — RFC 0031 held 15; noted.) No behavior changes; 111 semantic-diff, 2,419 workspace tests green."}	blake3:K9gM-sIBp8GR6REgxFQcTuE3DxnISYBPdYACruAgd3k
1785870762866799	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0go		item.move	bn-1fik9	{"state":"done"}	blake3:IEx8Kn0sSl6OX8deFd8kppc9y8o3TSZC3R6mxv0jTAs
1785872292033018	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy0wo		item.comment	bn-3rqvm	{"body":"PR-13 read/verify group landed in crates/continuum-cli (commit 8c9df35, in-workspace).\n\nREGISTRY MAPPING (noun-verb; every CLI verb names the registry verb it drives, the discipline the landed groups follow):\n  snapshot create/fork/seal -> workspace.create/fork/seal\n  check start/result/await -> verification.start/result/await\n  explain compile -> context.compile\nThere is no workspace.import in the IDL; the PR-3 family is create/fork/diff/seal, so 'import' maps onto workspace.create. workspace.diff is deliberately NOT a snapshot verb: it is the RFC 0031 semantic-diff read (PR-12's lane), refused typed-unsupported by daemon::workspace, and a 'snapshot diff' whose depth is unsupported in every buildable deployment belongs beside PR 12, not inside the family that makes snapshots. Stated in snapshot.rs's module doc.\n\nCHECK, BOTH ANSWER SHAPES: verification.start declares task/result both optional, so render_start derives a named AnswerShape token {task-shaped, result-shaped, neither, both} from the RESPONSE BODY -- never from the envelope status, which is a different statement. Task-shaped renders the handle, the status wire token and the envelope continuation; result-shaped renders the cached VerificationResult (task/target/fragments/evidence/crashpack/context/continuation). Both live from one fixture: first submit task-shaped, second submit under a distinct idempotency key hits the daemon's cached lane and is result-shaped; both pinned in text and JSON. RFC 0026's A12 disposition honoured explicitly -- the rendering claims nothing about work beginning, and a test asserts no 'started work'/'running'/'in progress'/'launched' appears.\nFLOW DECISION: submit-and-print-handle, never submit-then-poll. One wire call per invocation; check result takes task_* positionally. A poll loop needs a remembered task (INV-002 ambient state) and a client-side stopping rule with no daemon counterpart (violates acceptance criterion 1). verification.await is the protocol's own bounded wait; its bound is the caller's required --timeout-ms, which sets both the body's timeout_ms and the envelope's wall_ms ceiling. A suspension is PRINTED (status + cont_* + typed facts), never waited on.\nVERDICT, NOT RE-DERIVED (criterion 2): wire::Admitted gained verdict: Option<Verdict> and assurance: Option<AssuranceEnvelope>, read straight off ResultEnvelope -- the same field set continuum_mcp::Admitted already carries, so the two adapters do not disagree about what an answer is. render::verdict_lines/json flatten all four union members into one seven-key set (kind/verdict/inconclusive_reason/assurance_class/decision/outcome/gates) so a parser never branches; render::assurance_lines/json render all nine dimensions x four keys on both union arms (rule envelope.assurance_required: 'hiding uncertainty to save tokens is prohibited'). INV-008 pinned live: a PARK_BUDGET Die Hard campaign renders inconclusive + ResourceExhausted + bounded verbatim in all three formats, and nothing infers a reason from a budget.\n\nEXPLAIN: explain compile renders the Context Pack PROJECTION -- 18 schema-normative scalars plus guarantees/evidence/selected/omissions/expansions/redactions unabridged, read out of the pack document; JSON embeds the same document verbatim. INV-003 pinned by a test asserting every pack.* value the text format prints is a substring of the JSON answer. The pack's own RFC 0028 manifest (pack.omissions) and the envelope's INV-007 manifest (omissions) render separately, neither folded into the other. An absent array reads none, not 0 (an empty manifest is an assertion under RFC 0028). Unparseable pack reads (none, N) vs absent (none, none).\n\nDEPTH / WIRE GAPS (all recorded; no protocol change, no IDL edit):\n 1. context.compile -- registered, namespace SERVED by ContextFamily, compiler unserved ('no Context Pack compiler is served by this daemon'). explain compile reports Depth::Unsupported over a real frame; its projection is proven renderer-first, per the bn-3tz60/bn-1g7e4 precedent.\n 2. workspace.diff -- registered, refused UnsupportedSemanticFeature by daemon::workspace. Not surfaced (see above).\n 3. workspace.fork's patches argument -- refused UnsupportedSemanticFeature by the served family. Surfaced via --patch and pinned as the boundary case that gives 'depth' content INSIDE a served namespace: patches -> unsupported, same base without -> refused.\n No operation I needed was missing from the registry.\n\nDEFECT FOUND AND ADDRESSED: Connection's generated idempotency key was 'cli-idem-{counter}' -- not a function of the operation or the request -- so two DIFFERENT mutations by one actor collided with IdempotencyKeyReused (latent before this bone: only task.cancel/resume and context.expand were mutations and no test drove two). Now 'cli-idem-{operation}-{counter}', plus --idempotency-key as a caller override, because rule idempotency.replay makes 'these are the same request' a statement only a caller can make. RESIDUAL, deliberately not fixed here: a generated key is still not a function of the request BODY, so two invocations of one command with different arguments present the same key and earn the daemon's typed IdempotencyKeyReused. Content-deriving it needs a hash and this crate has exactly one dependency edge (continuumd re-exports no hasher). Pinned as evidence (two_different_creates_need_two_idempotency_keys_and_say_so_when_they_share_one) and documented on Connection::with_idempotency_key. Candidate for a follow-up bone.\n\nREUSE: render::Depth, render::project, render::Projection and the golden harness reused as-is. project's success closure now takes &Admitted<T> instead of &T -- half of what check renders is on the ENVELOPE (status, task, continuation, verdict, assurance), and a body-only closure would have forced check out of the shared shape; evidence/debug/repair updated mechanically, behaviour unchanged. New shared helpers: verdict_lines/json, assurance_lines/json, handle_lines/json. Scan gained repeats (Scan::all) for list<> fields (--overlay/--patch/--guarantee) and a --seal boolean; single-valued reading unchanged. clippy::large_enum_variant allowed on wire::Outcome with the justification continuum_mcp::Outcome already carries for the identical shape.\n\nTESTS: 3 new suites, all live over LocalPair against real provisioned Daemons. snapshot_family.rs 13 (create->seal->fork end to end where each command consumes the handle the previous one PRINTED; X2 no-existence-oracle denial; unsupported-vs-refused contrast; JSON pinned byte-for-byte; text pinned line-for-line; pretty = text + one line; 64 KiB overlay; INV-002 usage). check_verdict.rs 12 (both answer shapes; A12; refuted/validated closed; inconclusive/ResourceExhausted/bounded; nine assurance dimensions; await; denial; JSON pinned; usage). explain_pack.rs 10 (live family-level unsupported in all three formats; JSON pinned; full projection; both manifests; INV-003 property; unparseable and minimal packs; totality; 64 KiB question; usage). Plus 6 new lib unit tests (verdict key-set totality, assurance four-keys-both-arms, handle lists, repeated flags, --seal, and the unknown-command test which used 'snapshot' and would have gone on passing for the wrong reason).\n\nGATES from workspace root, all exit 0: cargo fmt --all --check (0); cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings (0); cargo test -p continuum-cli --locked (0) -- 99 tests passed, up from 58 (30 lib + 13 + 12 + 10 + 18 + 6 + 5 + 5); full just check (0) -- 2460 tests across 198 suites, boundaries/covenant/governance/dossier/Lean green.\n\nFiles: src/{snapshot,check,explain}.rs new; {cli,wire,render,lib,evidence,debug,repair,bin/continuum}.rs modified; tests/{snapshot_family,check_verdict,explain_pack}.rs new; tests/{context_expand,debug_repair_surface}.rs touched for the two new Admitted fields. Nothing outside crates/continuum-cli/. START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md untouched. Bone left in doing."}	blake3:iR2-w4deKhTCdETlTU1mDSHI3xvtd3eybkAfRHwTXP8
1785872363849116	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy1Ao		item.create	bn-jmx97	{"description":"bn-3rqvm found and partially fixed a latent defect: continuum-cli's Connection generated idempotency keys as cli-idem-{counter} — not a function of the operation or request — so two different mutations by one actor collided with IdempotencyKeyReused. Fixed to cli-idem-{operation}-{counter} plus a --idempotency-key caller override. RESIDUAL (this bone): the generated key is still not a function of the request BODY, so two different requests to the same operation in one session share a key-space that only the counter separates — replay/retry semantics across CLI invocations are therefore caller's responsibility via --idempotency-key. Content-deriving the key needs a hash and continuum-cli deliberately has exactly one production dependency edge (continuum-intent) — decide: reuse continuum-intent's canonical hashing (ID5 discipline) if it is honestly reachable through the existing edge, accept a second edge, or ratify the current shape with the override documented as the replay mechanism. Evidence pin exists in bn-3rqvm's tests.","kind":"task","title":"CLI generated idempotency key is not a function of the request body"}	blake3:AbaUWd1dOUQ0fc0AN-h2np7Q6MUxkWHcMEZZ5HpR6rE
