1785875383096972	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy9Ao		item.comment	bn-l4kmc	{"body":"docs/36, docs/46, docs/55 regenerated against IDL 3.3 (73 ops, protocol 3.3) and RFC 0026's Corrections section (24-36), plus RFC 0027's docs/36-specific corrections (19-21) and its docs/55 authority-annotation requirement (24) since they target the same three docs and are precise/mechanical. Verified mechanically, not by eye: scripted diff of all 73 operation/authority pairs, all 19 handle prefixes, and all 20 ErrorCode members against the IDL -- zero mismatches.\n\ndocs/36 (AGENT_PROTOCOL_AND_TOOL_CONTRACTS): error taxonomy 15->20 codes (added AcceptanceChainInvalid/StatusConflict/QuotaExhausted/EpochUnsupported/PublicationAborted); 'recovery may carry' -> 'recovery is required'. Result-envelope example fixed on all five counts correction 25 names: status completed(TaskStatus)->ok(ResultStatus); verdict bare string->tagged union {semantic:{...}}; next_operations bare names->NextOperation structs with pre-filled arguments; artifacts[].kind context_pack->ctx (plan section 4.4 prefix minus underscore); empty epochs{}->all six epochs plus engine named. Worked example's relation=source -> source_span (correction 26). context.expand's budget.max_nodes argument removed; node ceiling is output_policy.max_nodes, an envelope field (correction 27). Protocol-conformance list: IDL tech choice is decided (custom textual IDL, JSON Schema generated) and clients are Rust+TypeScript not Python (correction 28). Also applied RFC 0027's docs/36 corrections: 'Useful relations' prose list -> the closed 11-member ExpansionRelation vocabulary verbatim (RFC 0027 correction 19); tool-design rule 10's 'Unknown' token removed and replaced with the actual inconclusive/unknown-token distinction (correction 20); repair-authority may/may-not lists reframed as illustrative, with the actual per-operation authority levels stated (correction 21).\n\ndocs/46 (IDE_CLI_DAP_MCP_AND_SARIF): was missing the 'Non-normative projection' provenance blockquote entirely (36/55 have it) -- added. Four non-conforming adapter surfaces fixed (correction 29): DAP continuum/fairnessLedger removed (no backing operation -- fairness is an assurance-envelope dimension, not a standalone op); MCP get_context_pack -> compile_context (context.compile), alongside existing expand_context (context.expand), since there is no context.get; MCP verify_repair -> evaluate_repair (repair.evaluate, not evidence.verify -- different op and authority level); MCP start_forge -> create_forge (forge.create). Versioning section rewritten: was tracking a native+adapter protocol version, a semantic epoch, a schema version, and a fused proof/checker epoch; now states the real six compatibility epochs plus engine identity, that adapters have no protocol version of their own, that there is no checker epoch (correction 17), and that schema_epoch is a per-payload header not a tracked epoch (correction 30). 'Generic artifact path' fallback for an unrenderable epoch replaced with the real rule: EpochUnsupported, never best-effort rendering (correction 31).\n\ndocs/55 (AGENT_API_REFERENCE_SKETCH): coverage 64/72 -> 73/73 operations (added the whole correspondence namespace [bind/status/drift] and observe namespace [ingest/classify/result], task.update_budget, evidence.subscribe, and the new 73rd op evidence.link). Handle classes 17/19 -> 19/19 (added IntentBundleHandle inb_ and DefectHandle defect_, reordered to IDL declaration order). Added RFC 0027 correction 24's requirement: every operation now states its authority level (read/propose/execute/revise-intent/promote), scripted-verified 73/73 against the IDL. intent.lock fixed from 'applies an intent lock' to what it does: edits the plan section 5.4 field-to-verb policy map from RFC 0037's closed verb set (correction 33). intent.accept's 'drafts gain protection' fixed -- there is no draft status; intent.accept is the only transition out of Proposed (correction 34). Error taxonomy 15->20, same fix as docs/36. Common-result-rules list fixed: removed snapshot/intent/budget (request-side only, never in ResultEnvelope), fixed the 'semantic/proof/engine epochs' phrasing that grouped engine identity under 'epochs' -- docs/55 repeated the same mistake correction 35 found in docs/35 -- now names all six epochs plus engine as a separate identity, and kept/clarified audit correlation, the one field docs/55 asked for that the protocol went on to add at 3.1 (correction 32).\n\nNormative-text findings for the lead: none found. The scripted cross-checks (73 operation/authority pairs, 19 handle prefixes, 20 ErrorCode members) found RFC 0026's stated counts and tables agree with the IDL exactly -- no drift or internal contradiction surfaced during this pass.\n\nGates: full 'just check' (fmt-check, lint, test, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier, lean) exits 0 from workspace root. Dossier validator's gate_citation_hygiene (148 files) passes; the claim-governance checker (scans notes/plan/docs/*.md for controlled/uncontrolled verbs) passes with zero violations against the new prose. Committed in-workspace at 356b6d6: 'docs: regenerate docs/36/46/55 against IDL 3.3 and RFC 0026 (bn-l4kmc)' (3 files changed, 471 insertions, 87 deletions). Bone left in doing; no merge/close/push performed."}	blake3:fcVCiHO-As91QuSjt6ize3JRtfOxIvb4ajvG3V8IzyM
1785875427395269	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy9Qo		item.comment	bn-l4kmc	{"body":"Lead: reviewed and merged @ epoch 5553e2d. Projection-doc regen ratified: docs/36 (20-code taxonomy, envelope example fixed on all five counts, output_policy.max_nodes, real conformance list), docs/46 (four nonexistent-operation fixes, real six-epochs-plus-engine-identity versioning, EpochUnsupported), docs/55 (73/73 operations incl. evidence.link, 19/19 handle classes, per-operation authority levels added everywhere). Script-cross-verified against the IDL with zero mismatches — the right method for projection regen. Folding in RFC 0027's docs-targeted corrections was correct scope (same docs, same mechanical checklist). No normative findings — RFC 0026's own counts agree with the IDL exactly, which is itself worth having verified."}	blake3:gNNDqL45tbQU-0NVJE8_CimDLeTzd_uBThssEI0n0jw
1785875427426439	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy9go		item.move	bn-l4kmc	{"state":"done"}	blake3:dFECbqJWHuAj8NUVAd8mFWzLm77FpTo55uTpksxJGuQ
1785876774009656	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy9wo		item.comment	bn-ybh1z	{"body":"Delivered — `crates/continuum-cli/src/contract.rs` is the crate's one output contract, and `crates/continuum-cli/tests/output_contract.rs` holds all sixteen command renderers to it over three arms and three formats. Commit `6cc9e5f` in the workspace.\n\n## The contract as ratified\n\n**1. `--json` is the daemon's answer, verbatim.** Three rules govern what may appear: *no dropped fields* (lists travel as lists in the machine channel — it has no terminal to overflow); *echoed subjects appear once* (a response field repeating a request field of the same name is carried under the request's key — `repair.review`'s `repair`, `task.cancel`'s `task`); *no invented fields* (every key is a request field, a response field, or a derived token, and a derived token is admissible only when it is a total documented function of the answer adding no fact the answer does not carry — `depth`, `answer_shape`, `outcome` are the three).\n\n**2. The purity statement, exactly as pinned.** `contract::project_text(document, keys)` computes the whole terminal body from the machine document and an ordered key list *and nothing else*. The correspondence has **one rule and no exceptions**: a key is a path (`a.b`, `a[0]`, `a.b[2].c`); traversal into anything that is not the matching container yields `null`; and a value renders as `null → none`, bool → `true`/`false`, integer → decimal, string → itself, **array → its length**, **object → its field count**. `contract::divergence(text, document)` recovers the key order from a rendered text block, re-renders it from the JSON alone, and compares byte for byte. `the_terminal_body_of_every_command_re_renders_from_its_own_machine_document` applies it to every command × every arm. This is the strongest statement the existing `render::Projection` design supports — the renderers already compute both channels from one closure over one `Outcome`, and this proves the results of that one closure agree — and it is falsifiable: `the_projection_check_reports_a_real_renderers_drift` perturbs a real command's real output (`node_bytes` → `node.bytes`) and asserts the check names the key. I also confirmed by hand that reverting the `node_bytes` rename in `evidence.rs` fails the matrix test.\n\n`pretty` is pinned separately and exactly: `pretty.text == \"command  <name>\\n\" + text.text`, for every command, every arm. The label is the only string in the crate that is not a projection of the document; it carries no fact.\n\n**3. The exit-code taxonomy.** One number answers one question: *did I get an answer, and if so was it yes?*\n\n| Code | `Exit` | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `0` | `Success` | The daemon answered, and no verdict it carried says no. |\n| `1` | `Declined` | No answer: the request was typedly refused, or the command line did not parse. |\n| `2` | `Fault` | No answer: the connection failed below the protocol. |\n| `3` | `Refuted` | An answer, and it is **no** — a decided negative verdict. |\n| `4` | `Inconclusive` | An answer, and it is **unknown** — INV-008's typed inconclusiveness. |\n\n`0`/`1`/`2` keep exactly the meanings cli-conventions.md assigns; `3`/`4` extend that table rather than reinterpret it, so a script testing `$? == 0` keeps working. `Exit::of_verdict` is total over all four `Verdict` members and over `verdict: null` — `SemanticVerdict::Refuted`, `EvaluationVerdict::{Refuted,Deadlock}`, `PolicyDecision::Block`, `StructuralOutcome::Rejected` are the decided negatives; `PolicyDecision::Review` is inconclusive (a gate that reached no decision); everything else is success. It reads the verdict off the answer and never off a cost, a budget, or an assurance class.\n\n**bn-1g7e4's reading stands, and I documented why.** `unsupported` and `refused` still share `1`. The line: *the exit code says what happened to the question; a typed token in the output says what happened to the request.* Splitting refused/unsupported would add a number duplicating `depth`, which is lossless in every format. Splitting refuted/inconclusive adds a number **nothing else conveys to `$?`** — the failure it removes is `continuum check result … && deploy` deploying a refuted build.\n\n**4. Non-color completeness (G8-06).** The audit found **zero** ANSI, colour, box-drawing or graph rendering anywhere in the crate — it is a property of the code, not a flag: there is no `--no-color`, no `NO_COLOR` read, no TTY-conditional styling, because there is nothing to switch off. Now pinned: `no_command_emits_an_escape_a_colour_or_a_box_glyph` scans every command × arm × format for control characters (other than the line feed ending a line) and for U+2500–U+259F, and `pretty_is_text_plus_one_label_line_and_nothing_else` proves a reader who never sees the pretty format loses a label and no fact.\n\n*(divergences, pins, files, gates and findings in the next comment.)*"}	blake3:QbaC0BSMI_ANVjMvtlgDKa1lqEP01qmo5HcF865MoEY
1785876774045271	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy-Ao		item.comment	bn-ybh1z	{"body":"**bn-ybh1z, part 2 of 2** — divergences, pins, gates.\n\n## Divergences found and fixed\n\nEleven, all citing this bone in the code:\n\n1. **`next_operations` was text-only** — a count in the terminal and *absent from the machine channel*. RFC 0026's allowed-next-steps list, and the thing plan §3.1's five-minute path renders, reachable only by prose. Now a JSON array (operation, arguments embedded verbatim, `arguments_bytes`, rationale).\n2. **`error.recovery` was a count in JSON** — RFC 0026 calls it \"the only recovery channel\"; a length is not one. Now the list.\n3. **`request_id`, `cost`, `artifacts` reached no format at all** — envelope fields on every one of sixteen commands, dropped. Added centrally in `render::project`, machine-channel only (documented: prose is a *selection* of the document, never a superset, and ten cost lines under every `snapshot seal` would make §13.4's \"terse by default\" untrue).\n4. **`TaskRecord.epochs` was dropped entirely**, and `milestones` reached the machine channel as a count. Epochs are what a `ContinuationEpochMismatch` refusal is read against, so `task status` was the one command that could explain a stale continuation and could not. All seven epoch fields and every milestone now render.\n5. **`task status`/`task cancel` JSON collapsed the whole response key set on the refusal arm** to `{error, omissions, advice}` — a parser had to branch on which keys existed. Now they use `Projection::absent_json` like everything else.\n6. **`task resume` carried an invented `refused: bool`**, and **`context expand` an invented `status: ok|refused`** — both restating what `depth` says typed and finer. Removed.\n7. **`task`/`context` had no `operation` and no `Depth` at all** — the INV-008 unsupported/refused distinction was unavailable on four of sixteen commands. All four now go through `render::project`.\n8. **`context expand` text printed `pack.bytes` with no JSON counterpart** — prose carrying a fact the machine channel lacked, INV-003's failure in its smallest form. Now `pack_bytes` in both.\n9. **`evidence show` text printed `redacted  false` where JSON said `null`** — a claim this crate made about a field the daemon never sent, and the two channels disagreed for the same answer. Now `redacted` reads the object (`4`/`none`) and the stub's own boolean is `redacted.redacted`.\n10. **`snapshot`'s `diagnostic[i].span` rendered a file name where JSON carried the five-field span object.** Now `diagnostics[i].span.file`.\n11. **Key names that were not paths into the document**: `omission[i].*`→`omissions[i].*`, `diagnostic[i]`→`diagnostics[i]`, `gate[i]`→`gates[i]`, `verdict`→`verdict.kind`, `{node,edge,frontier,state,pack}.bytes`→`*_bytes`, `target.kind`/`target.id`/`budget.states`/`budget.bytes`/`components.intent`/`components.files`→ the flat spellings JSON already used. `pack.bytes` was the worst: the document reads it as the pack's own `bytes` field, which is a real key in `content_budget`.\n\nTwo reserved-key collisions were resolved rather than shadowed (`contract::RESERVED_KEYS`): `TaskRecord.operation` renders as `record.operation` (the operation the *task* runs), and `ContextExpandRequest.depth` as `expand_depth` (an expansion distance, not a `Depth`). The three task commands render their response bodies under `record.` for that reason.\n\n## Pins flipped, with reasons\n\n- `check result` on a refuted campaign: `0` → **`3`**. `check result`/`check await` on a parked (inconclusive) campaign: `0` → **`4`**. Three assertions in `tests/check_verdict.rs`, each naming the reason at the assertion. This is the taxonomy's whole point and the only behavioural change a caller can observe.\n- `Depth::exit_code` **removed** (it could not see an admitted answer whose verdict says no). Its unit test became `both_refusal_depths_still_earn_the_same_declined_code`, which pins the part that did not change.\n- Five byte-for-byte JSON golden documents updated for `next_operations`, `recovery` as a list, and `request_id`/`cost`/`artifacts`.\n- `task resume`'s `refused  true|false` assertions → `depth  refused|served`; `evidence show`'s `redacted  false` → `redacted  none` + `redacted.redacted  none`.\n\n## Files\n\n- new: `crates/continuum-cli/src/contract.rs`, `crates/continuum-cli/tests/output_contract.rs`\n- changed: `src/{render,task,context,check,explain,evidence,debug,repair,snapshot,error,lib,bin/continuum}.rs`; `tests/{check_verdict,context_expand,debug_repair_surface,evidence_show,explain_pack,snapshot_family,task_lifecycle}.rs`\n- The taxonomy is documented in the crate: `contract.rs`'s module doc (four numbered sections, with the plan §13.4/§3.1, INV-003, G8-06 and cli-conventions.md citations), the exit-code table restated in `bin/continuum.rs`, and `lib.rs` pointing at it first.\n\n## Gates\n\nFrom the workspace root, all exit 0:\n\n- `cargo fmt --all --check` — 0\n- `cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings` — 0\n- `cargo test -p continuum-cli --locked` — 0, **121 tests** (baseline 99, +22)\n- `just check` — 0, workspace **2,557** (measured baseline at this commit: **2,535**, +22; the brief's ~2,502 is stale)\n\n## Surprising\n\n- **Every renderer lined up under the one rule after the key renames alone** — no renderer body had to be restructured to make `divergence` hold. The `Projection` design was already computing both channels from one closure; what was missing was that the *key names* were not paths into the document. That is why the exception-free rule was affordable.\n- **`unsupported` vs `refused` was the wrong axis to argue about.** bn-1g7e4 framed it as \"should unsupported get its own code\"; the real gap was one layer up — an *admitted* answer whose verdict is a `no` exiting `0`. Nothing in the crate could see that, because `Depth` classifies the request and not the answer.\n- **`explain compile`'s pack projection was already a path projection** of the embedded document; its key names were correct by accident of good design. The only fix it needed was `pack.bytes` → `pack_bytes`.\n- `explain.rs`'s `render_scalar` had to stop answering `none` for containers: on an adversarial pack whose `parent` were an object, the text would have read `none` while the machine channel read `1`, and the two channels would have disagreed about a field neither invented. It now follows the contract's uniform count rule."}	blake3:bIB_GN9E-zox4SehUCUi9zPWJMeplkwwsLhfuVxshVY
1785876830909491	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy-Qo		item.comment	bn-ybh1z	{"body":"Lead: reviewed and merged @ epoch cb05e67. Ratifications: (1) the five-class exit taxonomy (0 success / 1 declined / 2 fault / 3 refuted / 4 inconclusive) with bn-1g7e4's unsupported-refused-share-1 STANDING under the crisp rule — exit code says what happened to the QUESTION, typed token says what happened to the REQUEST; splitting refused/unsupported would duplicate depth, splitting refuted/inconclusive conveys what nothing else can to $?. (2) The refuted→3 / inconclusive→4 pin flips are exactly the bone's acceptance criterion — the real gap was an ADMITTED answer with a refuted verdict exiting 0 because Depth classifies requests, not answers; well found. (3) Mechanical purity via contract::divergence (recover key order from text, re-render from JSON, byte-compare, falsifiability tested BOTH directions) is the strongest cannot-diverge statement the design supports. (4) Eleven INV-003 divergences fixed incl. fields reaching NO format — this is why the contract needed one owner. (5) G8-06 zero-color finding now PINNED not just true. 16 renderers × 3 arms × 3 formats through one table; 121 CLI tests, 2,557 workspace."}	blake3:zIPSIhv8J_RxUlID-175uf_PcwwM7ooAtpZmsWJQglM
