1785878930594118	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyggs		item.comment	bn-ymw	{"body":"Delivered on workspace bn-ymw, commit 2a08a1b (base 718cc72). Both acceptance criteria met; every arm of `just check` green (fmt, clippy -D warnings, `cargo test --workspace --locked`, boundaries, covenant, five governance checkers, dossier, Lean T0/T1 + empty axiom manifest).\n\n## What landed\n\n**`crates/continuum-security/src/injection.rs`** — the corpus, as inert data (1197 lines, 15 in-crate shape tests). Its shape is not invented: plan §24.5 quotes research/35's ratified promotion gate verbatim, and that sentence fixes it — three cases for each of the ten red-team classes (one per prohibited outcome in the kill criterion), plus one case for each of the seven docs/49 intent-policy blocks and one escape attempt against each of its eight worker-isolation controls, for at least 45. All three enumerations are transcriptions, each variant carrying its own dossier bullet verbatim, and the floor is recomputed from the tables rather than written down.\n\n\"Across every agent-readable artifact class\" is mechanical rather than asserted: `injection::readability` classifies each variant of `continuum-workspace`'s closed `ArtifactClass`, so a class the plan adds is a non-exhaustive match rather than a silent gap. Eighteen of plan §4.4's nineteen carry agent-readable content and each has at least one case; the nineteenth is `cap_*`, excluded because RFC 0027 S5 keeps a capability out of every trace, error and rendering — so there is no content of that class to plant in, and the attack it does admit (a guessed/forged token) is the `predictable handles` class, carried as handle cases.\n\nIt lives in `continuum-security` rather than beside the test because research/35's lane enlarges the corpus on every case that ever succeeds, and a corpus inside one test file could only ever be run by that file. This is the first code in that crate; its boundary contract is unchanged (one edge, `continuum-workspace`, for `ArtifactClass`).\n\n**`crates/continuumd/tests/g2_injection_corpus_evidence.rs`** — the enforcement evidence (1480 lines, 20 tests, in `just check` via `cargo test --workspace`). Every case is driven through the real wire boundary: a byte frame into `Server::answer`, a byte frame back.\n\n## The three claims it holds\n\n1. **Zero privileged operations triggered.** Read off the admission ledger (`AdmissionRecord.admitted`, the record RFC 0027 P5 requires), not off an error code. That distinction is load-bearing: `CapabilityDenied` is *also* what a handler answers for an artifact the daemon does not hold, because X2 forbids a distinguishable not-found — so the wire code alone cannot separate \"refused by admission\" from \"no such record\", and only the ledger can.\n2. **Zero prohibited outcomes.** A before/after comparison of every intent's registry status, every evidence claim's status, and the store's authorization audit log — research/35's first two prohibited outcomes literally, rather than an absence of error codes.\n3. **Capability checks, not output filtering.** Two measurements. `cap_reviser` sits at the steward's own `revise-intent` level with an *empty* `privileged_operations` list — docs/49's \"revise intent: proposal only\" cell — so byte-identical request frames are denied for it and admitted for the steward, while it is still admitted to `intent.propose_revision` (the control that shows this is the privilege bit and not the level ladder). And a hostile payload and its benign twin get byte-identical answers.\n\n**The not-a-filter half.** `inert::an_injected_payload_is_stored_and_returned_verbatim_never_scrubbed` is the negative of a sanitizer: the steward's privileged `intent.accept` carrying an injection in the acceptance signature *succeeds*, the status moves to accepted, and the payload comes back through `intent.get` byte for byte, escaped exactly as the daemon's own encoder escapes it. This is also the liveness proof for the whole file — the corpus's denied attempts are real attempts at a real operation. If output filtering is ever added it fails this test, correctly, because a filter here would be a second, weaker enforcement path standing beside the capability check.\n\nAnti-vacuity: an in-test mutant predicate that lets the payload decide privilege, caught by exactly one named corpus payload; a non-vacuity check on the verbatim comparison; a floor on how many cases produce a comparable hostile/benign pair; assertions that the corpus actually drives at every landed `@privileged` operation and that every operation it names is one the registry declares.\n\n## Honest edges, stated in the file rather than glossed\n\n- Seven corpus operations (`program.run`, `program.replay`, `proof.attempt`, `proof.check`, `forge.step`, `benchmark.run`, `debug.export`) have no family here. There is no solver worker, no Lean worker, no generated-code runner and no benchmark grader in this process, so an escape attempt against one is refused by the codec before its payload is read — **an accounting of absence, not a defence.** What is proved is that the absence is *total*: identical answer for every payload on the wire, and `decode_arguments` refuses by operation name for bytes that are not a document in any encoding. The corpus carries those cases so the lane that lands each worker inherits its escape attempt.\n- This does **not** clear plan §24.5's ratified promotion gate, which additionally binds a run to a build and dependency epoch, requires refusal *and* an append-only audit record per case, and re-locks on any later success. It clears the narrower Phase A exit condition. The corpus is the seed that gate's Phase B lane grows.\n- Relationship to `inv016_untrusted_source_evidence.rs`: INV-016 asks whether a free-text *request field* can instruct the daemon, walking the IDL's free-text inventory. This asks whether content in an *agent-readable artifact class* can reach a *privileged operation*. INV-016 is about interpretation; this is about authority.\n\n## Incidental\n\n- `DaemonState::intents()` added (18 lines) — the sibling of `evidence_nodes()`, out-of-band only and documented as such, because \"no intent's status moved\" has to hold over the whole registry and a check limited to handles the test already knew would be blind to an alteration that minted a record.\n- `tools/governance/dependency-rationale.toml`: new `continuum-security` entry (dev-only, used by continuumd) and `continuum-workspace`'s `used_by`/rationale extended. GOV-1-07 passes.\n\nNote for the lead: the bn-ymw workspace was pruned externally partway through this run and the work had to be rebuilt from scratch on a recreated workspace; everything is committed now."}	blake3:ktj31gTqgb3dldxcXuxq8x8O8ZHFURahQinRknt_5TM
1785880852542713	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEygws		item.comment	bn-221j	{"body":"Phase A algebraic and identity property suites landed in-workspace (commit 03d48f6). Test-addition only: nine new files, **zero modifications to any existing file**; no production, protocol, IDL or schema change; **no dependency added**.\n\n## Harness (no `proptest`, no rationale entry needed)\n\n`proptest` was **not** added. The workspace declares exactly one external dependency (`blake3`) and `dependency-rationale.toml` sets the bar for a second; `proptest` is a nine-plus-crate tree with a full RNG stack needing dev edges in four manifests, and its \"seed from the OS unless told otherwise\" default is ambient entropy wearing a test's clothes (INV-005). The suites instead generalize the idiom already in the tree (`deterministic_ordering.rs`'s seeded `Shuffle`; `continuum-benchmark/tests/support/mod.rs`'s shared-module shape) into a real harness:\n\n- `crates/continuum-value/tests/support/property.rs` — `Prng` (splitmix64; one constructor, seed is an argument written into a `Plan` in the test source), `Domain` (generate/shrink/size), `check`, `Sexp` counterexample text (hex atoms, one spelling, strict parse), `replay`/`expect_replay_refutes`, `Pairs`/`NearPairs`/`Triples`.\n- Shared across four crates by `#[path = \"../../continuum-value/tests/support/property.rs\"] mod property;`. The file names no crate, so the include carries **no dependency edge** — it is text compiled into whichever test binary asks. A new crate was not available to a test bone (`crates/*` is the plan §20 list, enforced by `check_crate_boundaries.py`).\n- `support/value_domain.rs` — the generated `Value` domain. Its counterexample text **is** the CVNF-1 encoding in hex, read back through the strict decoder, so a retained case cannot silently become a different input.\n\n## Per-law coverage map\n\n| Law | Site | Suite |\n|---|---|---|\n| canonical round trips | `continuum_value::value` CVNF-1, `identity::ContentIdentity` | `continuum-value/tests/phase_a_canonical_round_trip.rs` |\n| total-order laws | `Ord for Value` (= lex order on CVNF-1 bytes), `AssuranceLevel` | `continuum-value/tests/phase_a_total_order.rs` |\n| epoch pinning | `epoch::EpochSet`, `first_mismatch`, pinned-artifact identity | `continuum-value/tests/phase_a_epoch_pinning.rs` |\n| Merkle identity | `continuum_workspace::snapshot::Snapshot` | `continuum-workspace/tests/phase_a_merkle_identity.rs` |\n| idempotency | `continuum_context::expansion::{ExpansionHandle::derive, ExpansionQuestion}` | `continuum-context/tests/phase_a_idempotency.rs` |\n| serialization stability | `continuumd::codec` `Document` over `Json` + `Cbor` | `continuumd/tests/phase_a_serialization_stability.rs` |\n| the harness itself | — | `continuum-value/tests/phase_a_property_harness.rs` |\n\nBeyond the headline laws: `decode∘encode` **and** `encode∘decode`, single-byte-mutation injectivity, prefix/extension rejection; the *defining* order law `a.cmp(b) == a.encode().cmp(b.encode())` on every generated pair plus an exhaustive 25-pair/125-triple `AssuranceLevel` sweep; Merkle *locality* (editing one file moves exactly the identities on its path and no others) and insertion-order independence; `derive` idempotent over eight repeats, injective in all four question terms, typed refusal for all 19 non-pack artifact classes; codec purity, fixpoint, member-order independence, JSON/CBOR agreement through a schema-guided reader, injectivity.\n\n## Shrinking + deterministic replay\n\nGreedy descent accepting only strictly-smaller candidates, capped by `MAX_SHRINK_STEPS`; pairs/triples also shrink **jointly** (same reduction on both halves plus a capped joint square) so shared noise comes off and only the difference that matters remains. Termination is proved against a hostile `Domain` whose candidates never shrink.\n\nReplay has two teeth per suite: (1) the shrunk counterexample's text is pinned as a `RETAINED_*` constant, asserted equal to the live run's, and the same plan re-run to show the refutation is a function of the seed not the run; (2) `expect_replay_refutes` parses that constant and re-runs the law with **no `Plan`, no `Prng`, no `Domain`** — the retained string is an input the suite still executes. Retained cases are also asserted to pass the *real* law they were built to refute.\n\n## Seeded law violations — every suite proven able to fail\n\n| Suite | Seeded violation | Retained minimal counterexample |\n|---|---|---|\n| round trip | encoder spelling a `nat` with the `int` tag (`Value::Nat`'s own documented hazard) | `#0300` = `Nat(0)` |\n| total order | comparator reading only the encoding's *length* (reflexive/antisymmetric/transitive/total — still merges identities) | `(#0b00 #0900)` = `Set{}` vs `Tuple()` |\n| total order | cyclic tournament over kind tags (every pairwise law holds; only arity three sees it) | `(#0900 #0f00 #01)` |\n| epoch pinning | pinned identity omitting the `corpus` epoch | `((#30 # # # # # #30) (#30 # # # # # #))` |\n| epoch pinning | `first_mismatch` skipping `corpus` | same shape |\n| Merkle | `ContentIdentifier` over a **truncated preimage** | `(((#7a #0001)) ((#7a #0000)))` — one file, contents differing only in the last byte |\n| idempotency | `ContentHasher` drifting with a per-thread call counter | `(#30 #30 #30 #30)` |\n| idempotency | derivation forgetting `depth` (perfectly idempotent, still wrong) | `((#30 #30 #30 #31) (#30 #30 #30 #30))` |\n| serialization | object writer keeping presentation order | `(#6d (#62 (#6e)) (#61 (#6e)))` = `{b:null,a:null}` out of canonical order |\n| serialization | every `U64` written as a JSON number past the exact range | `(#75 #39303037…3932)` = `MAX_EXACT_INTEGER + 1` |\n\nThree further anti-vacuity guards: `expect_refuted` is shown to **panic** when a law holds, so a missed violation cannot read green; a corpus that *cannot reach* the seeded bug is reported as vacuous (the failure mode a property suite is likeliest to have); and the codec suite's own JSON writer is asserted byte-identical to the real encoder over the whole corpus, so its two defects are single-rule perturbations rather than strawmen. Each falsification test also asserts the *determinism* laws still pass under the broken seam — which is the argument for why injectivity is a law in its own right.\n\n## Discipline\n\nNo ambient nondeterminism: every corpus is a function of a `u64` in the test source. The one stateful shim (`DriftingHasher`) is the deliberately broken seam, `thread_local!` so it stays deterministic under parallel tests. Adversarial inputs are **linear** — `MAX_DEPTH`-deep values and paths, 255-byte segments, 512-child directories, 1,024-element sets, a 512-step depth sweep, all built one element/level at a time; no textual doubling. Identity seams are injective by construction (`HexIdentity`), so \"two workspaces have two identities\" is a fact rather than a claim about BLAKE3 (ADR-0013).\n\n## Real law violations found\n\n**None.** Every property held against real code on first run. Two behavioural facts now pinned, both already correct: `Value`'s `Ord` agrees with lexicographic order on CVNF-1 bytes at *every* kind including `Multiset`/`Map`/`Record` interiors; and both wire readers enter at nesting zero, so `MAX_DEPTH` enclosing containers is the deepest admissible document (not `MAX_DEPTH - 1`).\n\n## Gates (workspace root, all exit 0)\n\n- `cargo fmt --all --check` — clean\n- `cargo clippy --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings` — clean\n- `cargo test --workspace --locked` — **2,625 passed, 0 failed** (baseline 2,557; **+68**)\n- `just check` — exit 0 (fmt, lint, test, boundaries, covenant, governance, dossier, Lean)\n\nPer-suite: round trip 10, total order 11, epoch pinning 10, harness 8, Merkle 9, idempotency 10, serialization 10.\n\nBone left in `doing`; not merged, not pushed, not closed."}	blake3:DbkfGFK3B2fxyVt1F_cbs_bvuTQVzUHby6hDLE9t15U
1785880906054217	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyhAs		item.comment	bn-221j	{"body":"Lead: reviewed and merged @ epoch bd53226. Ratifications: (1) NO proptest — the from-scratch splitmix64/xorshift seeded harness generalizing the tree's own deterministic_ordering.rs idiom is the RIGHT call: proptest's OS-entropy default is exactly the ambient nondeterminism INV-005 forbids, and the shared-via-#[path] support file names no crate so it adds no §20 boundary edge. (2) Six laws mapped to their real sites (CVNF-1 round-trip, Ord=lex-on-canonical-bytes at every kind incl Multiset/Map/Record interiors, EpochSet pinning, Snapshot Merkle identity, ExpansionHandle idempotency, codec Document stability). (3) Anti-vacuity is first-class and satisfied — ten seeded local-seam violations, each caught by the unmodified law, each shrinking to a pinned RETAINED_* replayed deterministically; plus the expect_refuted-panics-when-law-holds and vacuous-corpus-reported guards. (4) Two behaviors newly pinned (lex/Ord agreement at interior kinds; both wire readers enter at nesting zero so MAX_DEPTH enclosing containers is admissible). Zero modifications to existing files, +68 tests → 2,625."}	blake3:8yE_1R4AP_lXLjvWYUV6qFw8AHwFnaLGvfBhzusmFUI
